# Cyrius Field Notes — Compiler Internal Gotchas
# Non-obvious traps an agent will hit when working on cyrius's
# compiler internals: regalloc, layout, alignment, IR ordering,
# heap caps, fn-table conventions, codegen-vs-IR divergence.
#
# Earn their slot when an agent is debugging a cyrius
# regression that doesn't fit a clean pattern. The shape is
# always "this surprised future-me; here's the diagnosis +
# fix + how to recognize it again."
#
# For process methodology see methodology.cyml.
# For design patterns see patterns.cyml.
# For full backstory + chronological context see retros/.
#
# ⚠ `vr01_` IN OLDER ENTRIES IS A HISTORICAL FILENAME, NOT AN INSTRUCTION. That prefix
#   selected the cross-OS corpus until v6.5.11; the selector is now the
#   `tests/tcyr/crossos/` DIRECTORY and zero `vr01_*` files remain. Naming a new test
#   `vr01_*` today opts it OUT of the release gate's cross-OS leg — the opposite of what
#   the prefix used to mean. Live paths were repaired at the v6.6.1 audit; remaining
#   mentions are inside narratives about bugs found when the file was called that.
#
# 122 entries (re-DERIVED at 6.6.11 after the CVE-55 line-counter entry; at 6.6.10 bite 8 it read 119 against a live 120; first derived at v6.6.6 bite 21 review with `grep -c '^\[\[entries\]\]'`, which is the only
# way this number should ever be written. v6.6.1 was an AUDIT that took it 158 -> 62; it then
# drifted to a live 63 while still reading 62, to 96 while 6.6.5 was in flight, and to 100
# while still reading 98 — the header was not re-derived when an entry was appended.)
#
#   ⛔ THIS FILE WAS APPEND-ONLY FOR TOO LONG — **158 entries / 5,566 lines / 461 KB**,
#   a volume nobody searches. A gotchas file you will not read is worth LESS than none:
#   it still costs a lookup and returns noise.
#
#   ⭐ THE FINDING THAT MATTERS. This compiler has a SMALL NUMBER OF RECURRING DEFECT
#   CLASSES, and this file was recording each recurrence as a fresh discovery. **59 of
#   the 158 entries were instances of 14 classes.** Quantified, because the count IS
#   the data — seven cap-sweep failures says the caps need DERIVING, not documenting:
#
#     7  a cap is declared in one place and believed in five
#     7  there are seven forks; the change landed in one of them
#     7  a stated cause or a carried number is not evidence
#     6  a syscall number on Darwin/aarch64 is not the number you wrote
#     5  identifier identity is fragile in this lexer
#     7  a green gate measuring nothing
#     4  inline replay is a prerequisite for SIMD residency
#     5  any pass over emitted bytes must know every site in the stream
#     3  prove the mutation actually reached the gate
#     3  agnos is an x86 host that is not Linux
#     3  a guard in the loop implies a second pass somebody has to write
#     2  aarch64 atomics: alignment is architectural, CAS is not a full barrier
#     2  the callptr spill slot is a real local and can alias one
#     2  install.sh copies are where the toolchain breaks
#
#   Recording the seventh instance of a class as a NEW entry hides that it IS a class —
#   and the class is the thing worth knowing.
#
#   ⚠ NOT everything with a common adjective is a class. Five entries share "silent
#   corruption with no diagnostic" (i64::MIN negation, the syntactic tail-call guard,
#   the prefix-compare P0, aggregate copy by type, the value-form Err return) and were
#   deliberately LEFT SPLIT: the mechanisms are unrelated and each is independently
#   actionable. Merging on a shared symptom would cost specificity and teach nothing.
#
#   How it got here — nothing deleted:
#     158 -> 149  · 13 merged into 4
#     149 -> 105  · 44 archived to `retros/resolved_traps.cyml`
#     105 ->  76  · 28 merged into 6, 7 more archived
#      76 ->  62  · 19 merged into 5
#   **62 live traps + 51 archived = 113 distinct entries preserved**, with 59 originals
#   consolidated into 14 class entries that keep every instance as evidence.
#
#   Also fixed: ~500 lines of v5.11.x BACKSTORY compressed to trap-plus-pointer
#   (`retros/v511x.cyml` already held it); 1 entry FACTUALLY WRONG IN ITS OWN NAME
#   (asserted the reserved-token count is "67 not 51"; live count is 76); 1 whose flat
#   claim had become FALSE (`cyrius has no inliner` — `#inline` shipped v6.5.63); 1
#   pre-existing syntax defect; 12 dead test paths.
#
#   ⭐ ONE ENTRY BECAME A REPAIR ISSUE — the double-assigned token types 79/111, a defect
#   still live that nobody had filed
#   (`docs/development/issues/2026-09-08-lexer-token-types-79-and-111-double-assigned.md`).
#   **A gotcha documenting a defect nobody intends to fix is a bug report nobody filed.**
#
#   ⭐ THE ADMISSION RULE, because this file regrows otherwise:
#     · TRAP or CHANGE RECORD? "v6.4.51 raised output_buf to 1 GiB" is not a gotcha.
#     · An INSTANCE of a class above? Add it as evidence INSIDE that entry and increment
#       its count. Do not open a new one. The class is the lesson; the instance is proof.
#     · Over ~40 lines? Narrative to `retros/`; only the TRAP stays here.
#     · Now impossible (hard-errored, gated, path removed)? -> `resolved_traps.cyml`.
#     · A live defect nobody intends to fix? FILE IT.
#
#   Net: **158 -> 62 entries, 5,566 -> 1983 lines, 461 KB -> 164 KB.**
#
#   Live state at 6.6.1: cycc x86_64 **1,247,608 B** (.text 1,090,832) · seed **29,024 B**
#   · check.sh **240 passed / 0 failed** · **144** shell gates · **301** .tcyr (68 crossos)
#   · **102** lib/*.cyr · api-surface **5152** · heap map **102** regions · self_compile
#   **731-734 ms** · **1** open issue · cross-OS SELFHOST_OK on ecb/ach/cass/pi.
#
[[entries]]
name = "undefined_fn_ud2_stub_vs_undefined_const_hard_error"
description = "cyrius treats an unresolved FUNCTION call and an unresolved CONSTANT/enum-value asymmetrically: a call to a function defined nowhere compiles to a `ud2;ud2;nop` (SIGILL) stub at the call site + a `warning: undefined function` and STILL exits 0 with a valid binary (src/backend/x86/fixup.cyr overwrites the `e8` call placeholder with `0F 0B 0F 0B 90` when the fn target is the -1/-2 undef sentinel — deliberate, so it's a loud localisable SIGILL not a jump into low memory); but a reference to an undefined constant/enum value is a HARD compile error (`undefined variable '…'`). CYRIUS_STRICT=1 does NOT promote the fn warning. Consequence: a stdlib fn that resolves to nothing on some target ships GREEN and traps only at runtime. Surfaced v6.2.35 on agnos (lib/sync.cyr had no agnos mutex backend → patra's mutex_* were ud2 stubs → patra_init SIGILL), and explains why the M6-chain issue's 'add inert SYS_FUTEX constants' fix was both unnecessary AND a misdiagnosis (patra ≤1.11.x referenced SYS_FUTEX as a raw CONSTANT → real hard error; 1.12.x uses mutex_lock() a FUNCTION → silent ud2 → no error)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["codegen", "fixup", "ud2", "undefined-function", "undefined-constant", "agnos", "cross-target", "silent-failure", "v6.2.35"]
content = """
**UPDATE v6.3.2 (2026-06-28) — the default FLIPPED.** A *reachable* undefined-fn call is now a
**HARD ERROR by default** (`error: refusing to emit binary with N reachable undefined function(s)`;
exit 1, no output). The check in `src/backend/{x86,aarch64}/fixup.cyr` gates on a new `_allow_undef`
global (default 0 = error) instead of `_strict_mode`. `--allow-undef` (CLI) / `--al` downgrades back
to the warn+ud2+exit-0 behavior described below. **Dead-host (DCE-unreachable) refs STAY
warn+ud2+exit-0** (filtered out of the reachable count). So the asymmetry table below now describes
(a) any undef under `--allow-undef`, or (b) a genuinely dead-host ref — NOT the default reachable
case, which is now symmetric with the constant case (both hard-error). The flip's repo-wide blast
radius (forks, cross-arch builds, aspirational tools — not just the tcyr corpus) was cleaned with
ZERO `--allow-undef` via stubs/guards; see project memory `project_v632_undef_hard_error`.

**The asymmetry** (pre-v6.3.2 default; now the `--allow-undef` / dead-host case):

| unresolved symbol | cycc behavior |
|---|---|
| **function call** | `warning: undefined function 'f'` + emit `ud2;ud2;nop` at the call site; **exit 0, valid binary** |
| **constant / enum value** | `error: undefined variable 'X'`; **exit 1, no output** |

So `return f(x)` where `f` is defined nowhere COMPILES (and SIGILLs at
runtime if reached); `return X` where `X` is an undefined enum value does
NOT. `CYRIUS_STRICT=1` does not change the function case.

**Why** (src/backend/x86/fixup.cyr ~170-194): for a function-call fixup
whose target is the undef sentinel (<0), cycc overwrites the 5-byte
`e8 XX XX XX XX` call placeholder with `0F 0B 0F 0B 90` = `ud2; ud2; nop`.
Intentional — a SIGILL at the exact call site beats the disp32 resolving
to an arbitrary low address (the sandhi M2 2026-04-24 jump-into-entry bug).
Runtime confirmation: such a binary exits 132 (128+SIGILL) at the call.

**Where it bit (v6.2.35, the M6 persistence chain on agnos):**
`lib/sync.cyr` defined `mutex_new/lock/unlock` only under
`#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_{WIN,MACOS,LINUX}` — no agnos branch. patra includes
`sync.cyr` (not `thread.cyr`/`thread_agnos.cyr`), so on `--agnos` those
calls were undefined → ud2 stubs, compiling GREEN. `patra_init()` calls
`mutex_new()` UNCONDITIONALLY, so patra_init itself SIGILLs at runtime on
agnos. The filed fix ("add inert `SYS_FUTEX`/`FUTEX_*` to the agnos peer")
was a misdiagnosis: those constants are only referenced by stale patra
≤1.11.x's `_patra_lock`, which used `SYS_FUTEX` as a RAW CONSTANT (→ hard
error → that's the error the reporter actually saw). patra 1.12.x moved to
`mutex_lock()` (a FUNCTION → silent ud2), so once 1.12.3 is folded nothing
references the constants. Real fix: an agnos `#ifdef` no-op mutex backend in
sync.cyr (single-core cooperative-iron — no contender, lock is a no-op).

**Recurring class — `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX`-only stdlib helpers / raw
Linux syscall numbers.** This is the same shape, hit THREE times now:
v6.2.33 (io.cyr's `xopen`/`xstat`/… substrate), v6.2.35 (sync.cyr mutex), and
**v6.2.36 (io.cyr's `file_lock`/`file_unlock`/`file_trylock`/`file_lock_shared`/
`file_append_locked` — Linux-only + raw `syscall(73)`, so undefined `ud2` stubs
on agnos+macOS and the wrong number on aarch64; descent's `persist_init` →
libro FileStore → `file_append_locked` trapped at BOOT).** The fix pattern is
always the same: **route through the portable `x*` wrapper** that centralizes
the per-target syscall number (`xflock` → #59 agnos / #92 macOS / #73 x86 / #32
aarch64), and where a target lacks a feature the others have, branch it (agnos
has no kernel `AO_APPEND` yet → `xlseek` SEEK_END under the lock instead of
`O_APPEND`). PREVENTION: periodically grep `lib/` for `#ifdef
CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX` blocks that define `fn` (without sibling agnos/macОS
branches) and for raw `syscall(<small Linux num>)` outside the peer files —
each is a latent off-Linux `ud2`.

**How to recognize / guard it:**
- A consumer "compiles clean for `--agnos`" (or any target) but crashes
  early at runtime → grep the build stderr for `warning: undefined function`.
  Those are live landmines, not noise.
- A regression GATE for this class must NOT just check `exit 0` — it must
  assert the symbols are DEFINED (compile a probe that references them and
  FAIL if stderr contains `undefined function '<name>`). See
  scripts/agnos-crossbuild-gate.sh probe 1f.
- Mirror of the macОS-rot placebo: self-host + check.sh never exercise
  agnos codegen, so undefined-fn stubs ship "found by ports."
"""

[[entries]]
name = "element_typed_array_slot_spelling_v621"
description = "Bare `var a[N]` is byte-sized in a fn / slot-sized at top level — an accidental divergence that silently corrupts any address-taken local written with the `store64(&a + i*8)` slot idiom (the daimon route-404 class). v6.2.1 adds `var a: T[N]` (N elements of T) as the unambiguous slot/typed spelling; the audit found the class live in cycc itself (PARSE_SWITCH/PARSE_MATCH jump tables) + stdlib + folded libs."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["layout", "var-array", "byte-vs-slot", "0x12A000", "daimon", "v6.2.1"]
content = """
**The trap**: the SAME syntax `var a[N]` reserves different sizes by
scope. In a function (`PARSE_ARRAY`, parse_decl.cyr) it's `(N+7)&~7`
**bytes**; at top level (`PARSE_GVAR_ARR`) it's `N*8` (N i64 **slots**).
Both register into the one var-size table at `0x12A000`. A
function-local slot array written with `store64(&a + i*8, …)` therefore
runs off its byte-sized backing — `var parts[4]` is 8 bytes (1 slot),
so `store64(&parts + 24, …)` corrupts the next static object. Layout-
sensitive, so it stays dormant until an unrelated change shifts the
layout (daimon: deleting dead code moved `parts` 24 B before sandhi's
`" "` literal → every HTTP route 404'd).

**The fix (v6.2.1)**: `var a: T[N]` = N elements of T, sized
`N*sizeof(T)` identically in BOTH scopes. `i64[N]` = N slots (the slot
spelling), `u8[N]` = N bytes, widths i8..i64/u8..u64/u128. Pure
**frontend** change — the element width threads through `scalar_type`
(in-fn, `PARSE_ARRAY(S,noff,ew)`) / `ann_scalar` (top-level,
`PARSE_GVAR_ARR(S,noff,sti,ew)`) into the `0x12A000` byte size, and
every backend (x86/aarch64/macho/pe/cx) inherits it by SUMMING that
table — no backend edits. Bare `var a[N]` keeps its legacy per-scope
meaning (best reserved for byte buffers).

**Two-step bootstrap REQUIRED when migrating cycc's OWN arrays**:
because the OLD build/cycc byte-sizes the new `i64[N]` annotation,
`build/cycc → stage_a` gives stage_a byte-sized internal tables;
`stage_a → stage_b` (stage_a has the feature) gives slot-sized tables.
So `stage_a != stage_b`; the fixpoint is `stage_b == stage_c`. cycc's
own source had no >32-arm switch/match, so the under-sized `ends[256]`
(32 slots) self-hosted by luck for years — the classic latent-until-
layout-shift signature.

**Audit lesson**: run the audit against the lib SOURCE repos, not the
vendored fold — the fold-copy scan missed sigil `snp_report_verify`'s
`parsed[4]` that the source check caught (same `memset(&x,0,32)`-on-an-
8-byte-buffer bug as its sibling `inters[4]`). The ecosystem libs
(sigil/sakshi/agnosys) are the language's own stdlibs: patch each
lib's source, hold for release, then re-fold — never hand-edit the
`# Do not edit` vendored copy.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "large_fn_local_var_array_is_bss_not_stack"
description = "A large function-local `var buf[N]` in cycc is allocated in `.bss` static storage, NOT the stack frame — and a `var[]` sitting in a statically-dead region (e.g. after a `#ifdef`-gated early `return`) STILL reserves its `.bss` even on the path that never reaches it. Diagnose via section deltas, not `sub rsp` scanning."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["layout", "bss", "var-array", "agnos", "stack", "v6.1.12"]
content = """
**The trap**: `lib/io.cyr` `getenv()` had an 8 KB `var buf[8192]`
*after* a `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` early `return`. The agnos
build never touches `buf`, but the agnoshi consumer #PF'd and the
filed issue blamed an "8 KB stack frame reserved past the early
return." Plausible — but WRONG about the mechanism.

**What actually happens**: in cycc a function-local `var[]` of
this size is emitted into **`.bss`** (static storage), not as a
stack-frame allocation. Verified on the real `--agnos` build by
diffing section sizes before/after guarding the buffer behind
`#ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS`:

  .bss   0x2f18 -> 0xf08   (-8208 B = buf + align)
  .text  -928 B            (the now-dead environ reader)
  max stack frame: unchanged at 0xd0 — no `sub rsp, 0x2000`
  ever existed.

So scanning the disassembly for a big `sub rsp` finds nothing and
can make you think "the buffer isn't there" — it IS, just in
`.bss`. Always check `llvm-objdump -h` section deltas, not just
prologue `sub rsp`, when chasing a "phantom buffer" on a
constrained target.

**Why it still faulted on agnos** (mechanism the issue got wrong):
an oversized `.bss` grows the PT_LOAD memsz the loader must
zero-map; the agnos #PF is `.bss`-image / loader-mapping related,
NOT init-stack overflow. A "widen the ring-3 init stack" fix
aims a layer off.

**The fix**: guard the whole reader (buffer + parse loop) behind
`#ifndef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` so the dead region contributes
nothing to the agnos image. The broader compiler-side want —
elide storage for a `var[]` that's provably never live on the
taken path — stays a (low-priority) follow-on; the preprocessor
guard fully resolves the case. Related: the v6.0.79 `cyml.cyr`
oversized-`var[]`-on-constrained-target overflow.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "libssl_needs_fdlopen_not_dynlib_open"
description = "libssl's OPENSSL_init_ssl uses a recursive pthread mutex whose __kind field lives in the glibc TCB. cyrius's dynlib_bootstrap_tls zeroed TCB reads __kind=0 (non-recursive); same-thread re-entry deadlocks on futex. Use lib/fdlopen.cyr (foreign-dlopen) instead."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["dynlib", "tls-loader", "fdlopen", "v5.6.37"]
content = """
**The trap**: cyrius has two ways to load shared libraries at
runtime:

1. `lib/dynlib.cyr` — pure-cyrius ELF .so loader via mmap +
   GNU hash table walk. No libc, no glibc dlopen. Bypasses
   glibc's TLS/TCB setup entirely.

2. `lib/fdlopen.cyr` — foreign-dlopen wrapper. Calls into
   glibc's actual dlopen via the AT_BASE+offset trick, which
   means the loaded library gets glibc's full TLS/TCB setup
   including pthread_*.

For libssl specifically, dynlib_open works for getting symbol
addresses — but calling `OPENSSL_init_ssl()` deadlocks on a
futex inside libcrypto's pthread_mutex_lock. Why: libssl's
init uses `PTHREAD_RECURSIVE_MUTEX_INITIALIZER_NP`. The mutex
struct has a `__kind` field in glibc's TCB. cyrius's
dynlib_bootstrap_tls (the minimal TLS setup that dynlib_open
provides) zeros the TCB. `__kind == 0` means "non-recursive"
in pthread's encoding. Same-thread re-entry to a mutex that
the same thread already holds → futex wait forever (because
non-recursive).

**Rule**: if a library's init function uses pthread mutexes
internally, you NEED fdlopen (real glibc dlopen → real glibc
TLS/TCB), not dynlib (cyrius minimal loader). The list of
libraries that hit this includes:

- libssl (OPENSSL_init_ssl)
- libpthread (everything)
- libgomp (OpenMP runtime)
- libc-internal locking primitives (rare in pure cyrius use,
  but anything that touches TLS-cancellation hooks)

Conversely, dynlib is fine for:

- Pure C libraries with no pthread internals (libz, libcrypto's
  primitive routines if you skip OPENSSL_init_*)
- Libraries you call only for symbol addresses (dlsym then
  call manually)

**Recognize again**: any "dlopen succeeds, dlsym returns valid
pointer, calling that pointer hangs forever" shape on a
shared library that uses pthread mutexes.

Full backstory: v5_6.cyml's `libssl_needs_fdlopen_not_dynlib_open`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "sysv_odd_stack_args_rsp_alignment"
description = "SysV ABI requires rsp 16-aligned at every CALL (so callee sees rsp+8 16-aligned at entry). Cyrius's ECALLPOPS for n>6 args used `add rsp, 48` unconditionally; for odd nextra (N=7,9,11) this left rsp 8-aligned at CALL, propagating misalignment."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["abi", "sysv", "alignment", "v5.6.41"]
content = """
**The trap**: SysV AMD64 ABI requires rsp 16-byte aligned at
every CALL instruction (so the callee sees rsp+8 16-byte
aligned at entry, with the saved rip below it). Cyrius's
codegen for fn calls with >6 args uses ECALLPOPS to clean up
stack arguments after the call:

```cyr
# n > 6 args, n_extra = n - 6
# Push n_extra extra args
# call helper
# add rsp, n_extra * 8  ← cleanup
```

Pre-v5.6.41, the cleanup was `add rsp, n_extra * 8`
unconditionally. For odd `n_extra` (N = 7, 9, 11 args), this
leaves rsp 8-byte-aligned at the next CALL. Misalignment
propagates downstream until something does SSE on a stack-
saved value (libssl/libc prologs commonly do `movdqa xmm,
[rsp+N]`) — SIGSEGV at first instruction.

**Fix**: align rsp to 16 BEFORE pushing extra args:

```cyr
if ((n_extra & 1) == 1) {
    # Pre-pad rsp by 8 to absorb odd n_extra count
    push rax  # any value; we sub rsp, 8 effectively
}
push n_extra args
call helper
add rsp, (n_extra + (n_extra_odd ? 1 : 0)) * 8
```

⛔ **6.6.5 GENERALISES THIS AND RETRACTS ITS LAST LINE.** Two
corrections, both measured:

* The parity is `(n_extra + DEPTH) & 1`, not `n_extra & 1`.
  `n_extra` alone assumes the call is emitted with nothing else
  pending on the expression stack — true between statements,
  false inside an expression, where every enclosing operator and
  argument has pushed a value. At depth 0 the new rule reduces to
  exactly the old test, which is why statement-level 7-arg calls
  are byte-identical across 6.6.5.
* **"Win64 path was already correct" was wrong twice.** Its
  `framesize & 15` branch only rounds the frame; the parity pad
  beside it (`n & 1`) was equally depth-blind. And underneath
  both, the PE BASE was inverted — Windows enters an image with
  the return address pushed (rsp ≡ 8) and cyrius never
  re-aligned, so on Windows the STATEMENT-level calls were the
  misaligned ones.

See `nested_call_alignment_stack_machine` below for the whole
story.

**Recognize again**: any "SIGSEGV on the first SSE instruction
of a libc / libssl entry" shape. Common triggers: 7-arg fn
calls, OpenSSL's EVP_DecryptInit_ex (8 args), recvmmsg
(7 args). Always check rsp alignment via `gdb p $rsp & 0xF`
at the failing instruction.

Full backstory: v5_6.cyml's `sysv_odd_stack_args_rsp_alignment_violation`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "lsp_globals_before_fn_definitions"
description = "Cyrius requires top-level `var X` to be declared before any fn body that references X. Applies to programs/cyrius-lsp.cyr's symbol-table backing buffers too."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "lsp", "global-init-order", "v5.7.39"]
content = """
**The trap**: cyrius's parser does a single-pass top-down
walk of source. When a fn body references a top-level
`var X`, that `var X` must already be declared at parse time.
Forward fn calls work (the parser pre-scans fn signatures);
forward GLOBAL VAR references DO NOT work.

This is the v5.7.32 cyrlint warning class
(`global-init-order forward-ref`) that mabda surfaced. The
compiler ALSO doesn't catch every shape — the warning is
opt-in via `cyrius lint`.

**The cyrius-lsp specific instance** (v5.7.39): the LSP
extension added cross-file go-to-def + documentSymbol via a
symbol-table backing buffer. The buffer was a top-level `var
symbol_table[N]`. The fns that read it (`SYMBOL_LOOKUP`,
`SYMBOL_INSERT`) were defined before the `var` line in the
source layout.

Pre-fix: the parser saw the fn defs first, registered them,
parsed their bodies → reached `symbol_table` reference →
"undefined variable." Post-fix: moved `var symbol_table[N]`
above all fn defs that reference it.

**Recognize again**: any "undefined variable" error on a top-
level var that VISUALLY appears in the source somewhere.
Check it appears BEFORE the fns that read it.

**Generalize**: any new top-level `var X` declared in
cyrius's own source (or any cyrius project) MUST go above the
fn bodies that reference it. The parser is single-pass for
globals.

Full backstory: v5_7.cyml's `lsp_globals_before_fn_definitions_v5_7_39`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "snapshot_ping_pong_lib_corruption"
description = "RETRACTED v6.6.4: the lib-edit snapshot-refresh recipe corrupted released install slots; its copy-back premise expired at v5.11.17"
category = "cyrius"
---
Surfaced 2026-05-03 mid-v5.8.23 bite #2 when `lib/tagged.cyr`
edits reverted between Edit calls during the sum-type migration.

== ROOT CAUSE ==

Two-step ping-pong loop:

  1. `version-bump.sh` runs `install.sh --refresh-only` which
     copies `lib/*.cyr` from the repo INTO
     `~/.cyrius/versions/<v>/lib/` and `~/.cyrius/lib`.
  2. Subsequent `cyrius deps` resolution (e.g., during
     `check.sh`) copies the snapshot version BACK into the
     repo.

If you edit a `lib/*.cyr` file AFTER a `version-bump.sh` ran
(snapshot is now stale), the next `check.sh` invocation will
overwrite your edit with the prior snapshot.

== SYMPTOM ==

You make an Edit. You verify the change. You run `check.sh`.
Tests pass. But the next time you read the file, your edit is
GONE. cycc is also gone — the snapshot's version was pulled back
in along with the lib file, AND the snapshot's lib is what's
actually in `~/.cyrius/lib`.

== MITIGATION — RETRACTED (v6.6.4) ==

The 3-step recipe that stood here (copy the edited `lib/<file>.cyr`
into `~/.cyrius/versions/$(cat VERSION)/lib/`, or run the
same-version `version-bump.sh`) is WITHDRAWN. Two things were
wrong with it:

  1. Its premise expired. Since v5.11.17 `cyrius deps` in the
     cyrius source repo resolves `./lib` directly
     (`_dep_find_stdlib_dir` branch (a)), and since v6.4.77
     `cmd_lib_sync` refuses to run in the source repo — so
     nothing copies a snapshot BACK over a repo edit any more.
     The loop this entry describes cannot occur at HEAD.
  2. The write it prescribes is a CORRUPTION. Between a tag and
     the next bump, `$(cat VERSION)` still names the RELEASED
     version, so the copy lands in the released slot — the thing
     every consumer pin resolves. That is exactly how the
     installed "6.6.2" stdlib became 6.6.3's (12 files; one file's
     08:49 mtime on 2026-09-12 was attested, the rest inferred) and "6.6.1" gained three
     6.6.2 files. hisab filed it after a `cyrius build` under an
     unchanged pin silently re-vendored and re-locked the
     mutated files.

What replaced it: `install.sh --refresh-only`, `cyrius pulsar`
and `cyrius lsp` REFUSE to write a released slot from a drifted
tree (CYRIUS_REFRESH_RELEASED=1 for throwaway homes only);
`check.sh` stages its own throwaway CYRIUS_HOME from the tree,
so mid-slot lib/cbt changes are tested without touching the
store; every refresh stamps `SOURCE_COMMIT`; and
`scripts/verify-store.sh` (with `--restore <v>`) compares every
tagged slot against its tag. Re-cutting a version at a new
commit is `git tag -f <v> HEAD` FIRST, then refresh.

THE GENERAL LESSON, stronger than the specific one: a mitigation
recipe outlives its premise silently. This entry told agents to
perform a write for seven minors after the loop it guarded against
had been closed, and the write became the defect. When a recipe
says "copy X into the live store", check whether the tool has
since learned to read X itself.
[[entries]]
name = "foldin_symlink_vs_regular_file"
description = "When folding a sibling distfile into cyrius's lib/ via the sandhi pattern, the vendored file MUST be a regular file (mode 100644), not the absolute-path symlink that `cyrius deps` creates by default. Symlinks point into the user's local cache (/home/<user>/.cyrius/...) and dangle on any other machine — including CI."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["foldin", "deps", "ci", "sandhi-pattern", "v5.8.65"]
content = """
**The trap**: `cyrius deps` resolves a `[deps.<name>]` entry by
populating `~/.cyrius/deps/<name>/<tag>/` and then creating
`lib/<name>.cyr` as a SYMLINK to that cache path:
```
lib/sakshi.cyr -> /home/<user>/.cyrius/deps/sakshi/2.2.3/dist/sakshi.cyr
```
The symlink target is an ABSOLUTE PATH in the resolving user's
home. On the user's local machine this works because the cache
is populated. On any OTHER machine — including GitHub Actions
CI runners (whose home is `/home/runner/...`) — the symlink
dangles.

When folding a dep into stdlib (sandhi pattern), naive `git
add lib/<name>.cyr` commits the symlink. Git records mode 120000
(symlink). On checkout elsewhere, the symlink resolves against
a non-existent path → file effectively missing.

**Surfaces as**: tcyrs that `include "lib/<name>.cyr"` fail to
compile with "file not found" → cycc exits non-zero → under
`bash -e` (GHA's default shell mode), the test loop terminates
silently mid-loop. No `FAIL:` print, no summary, just exit 1.
Easy to misdiagnose as "test crashed the runner."

**The fix at v5.8.65** (the slot that surfaced this):
1. Convert the symlink to a regular file:
   ```
   cp --remove-destination "$(readlink lib/<name>.cyr)" lib/<name>.cyr
   ```
   `--remove-destination` is essential — `cp` without it follows
   the symlink and writes through to the cache, not the repo.
2. Commit the type change. `git diff` shows mode 120000 → 100644.
3. The cyrius distlib walker (`scripts/lib/audit-walk.sh`) skips
   files matching `^# Generated by: cyrius distlib$` in the first
   5 lines — those byte-snapshot bundles must not be reformatted.
   See also `distlib_marker_styles_v5865`.

**Recognize again**: any time you fold a `[deps.X]` into `lib/`
under the sandhi pattern, audit `git ls-tree HEAD lib/<X>.cyr`
for mode 120000 BEFORE pushing. The v5.7.0 sandhi precedent
(`lib/sandhi.cyr`) is the canonical example — mode 100644,
never a symlink.

**Why it wasn't caught locally**: the user's local machine
HAD the symlink target populated. `check.sh` self-host green,
all 127 tcyrs pass via `cyrius test`. CI is the first place
the absolute path doesn't resolve.

The fix is one shell loop, but the diagnosis took multiple
re-pushes (audit-walk marker fix, then ci.yml inline marker
fan-out, then sakshi-specific bundle.sh marker style). Saved
here so the next foldin slot starts with the de-symlink step
in the playbook.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "preprocess_out_vs_str_data_caps"
description = "Two distinct heap caps gate compilation. str_data caps the bytes the COMPILER writes for tracked string literals (raised to 2 MB at v5.8.59). preprocess_out caps the bytes of EXPANDED SOURCE the preprocessor produces from include resolution (still 2 MB since v5.6.40). Hitting one isn't the same as hitting the other, and the workarounds are different."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["heap", "preprocessor", "str_data", "v5.8.60"]
content = """
**The trap**: at v5.8.60 (NFKC/NFKD), the original design
used 116-char fixed-width compat-decomp records. 3833 records
× 116 chars = ~445 KB hex-encoded source — well under the
v5.8.59-bumped str_data 2 MB cap. Compiled fine in isolation.

But every consumer tcyr that includes lib/unicode/normalize.cyr
(transitively pulling in _normalize_data.cyr's ~445 KB blob)
also includes the rest of stdlib via auto-prepend. The
*expanded source* — the post-preprocessor concatenation of
all includes — overflowed `preprocess_out` (the 2 MB buffer at
S+0x44A000):
```
error: expanded source exceeds 2MB (2114828 bytes)
```
str_data has plenty of room. preprocess_out is the wall.

**These are two DIFFERENT walls**:
- `str_data` (`0x21A000`, 2 MB at v5.8.61): bytes the compiler
  writes for tracked string literals during codegen. Cap
  applies per-translation-unit.
- `preprocess_out` (`0x44A000`, 2 MB since v5.6.40): bytes of
  the source after include resolution. Cap applies BEFORE the
  lexer even runs.

A consumer that auto-prepends 14 stdlib modules + 6 sibling
distlib modules can sit at 1.6 MB expanded source even before
tcyr-specific code. Adding a 445 KB data table easily blows
the cap.

**The fix at v5.8.60**: switch the data-table encoding from
fixed-width 116-char records (445 KB total) to a 2-table
split — sorted IDX (12 chars/record × 3833 = 46 KB) +
variable-width DATA blob (count + cps, ~41 KB). Total ~87 KB,
80% reduction. Same lookup semantics (binary search IDX → data
offset → read count + cps), but consumer-side preprocess_out
stays well under cap.

**Recognize again**: any new fold-target that adds large
data blobs to lib/ should be sized against BOTH caps:
- Single-file size vs 64 KB string-literal cap (token
  encoding's u16 length field).
- Aggregate across ALL auto-prepended stdlib + the consumer's
  own source vs preprocess_out 2 MB cap.

The 2 MB preprocess_out cap is the easier one to forget
because it's not in the compiler's diagnostic surface during
single-file edits — only manifests when consumers compile.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "relocs_stripped_vs_ovmf_v51149"
description = "OVMF firmware treats COFF Characteristics RELOCS_STRIPPED (0x0001) as 'load at exact ImageBase or fail'. UEFI Application binaries must NEVER set this bit, even with zero actual fixups. Standard UEFI loaders (rEFInd, systemd-boot, GRUB-EFI) all clear it"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "pe", "uefi", "ovmf", "efi-application", "relocs-stripped", "coff-characteristics", "v5.11.49", "gnoboot", "silent-rejection"]
---
**THE TRAP.** OVMF firmware treats the COFF Characteristics bit
`RELOCS_STRIPPED (0x0001)` as *"load at exact ImageBase or fail"*. A UEFI
Application must **NEVER** set it — even when the image genuinely has zero fixups
and setting it looks accurate. Every standard loader (rEFInd, systemd-boot,
GRUB-EFI) clears it.

**RECOGNIZE AGAIN**: a flag that is *descriptively true* about your output but is
read by the consumer as a *demand*. The structural gate passed — Subsystem 0xA,
NX_COMPAT, Data Dirs [1]/[12] zeroed, a valid `.text` — because it checked what the
bits SAY, not what a firmware DOES with them. Boot it.

⟳ Full narrative in `retros/v511x.cyml`.
[[entries]]
name = "portable_sleep_no_raw_nanosleep_off_linux"
description = "Raw syscall(35) nanosleep faults off Linux: Darwin has NO plain nanosleep BSD syscall (and on aarch64 macho, 35 COLLIDES with unlinkat), and PE never routed 35. Portable sleep = poll(NULL,0,ms) on Linux+macOS (poll 7→230 is routed on both Mach-O backends) + a kernel32 Sleep reroute on Windows. WaitForSingleObject on a process/thread pseudo-handle does NOT block as a sleep."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["macho", "windows", "syscall", "nanosleep", "poll", "sleep", "esysxlat", "v6.0.65"]
content = """
**The trap**: `syscall(35, &timespec, 0)` (Linux nanosleep) in a
stdlib/consumer sleep helper works on Linux and faults or
misbehaves on every other target:
- **macOS**: XNU has no plain `nanosleep` BSD syscall (libsystem
  builds it over semaphore primitives). Worse, on aarch64-macho
  the number 35 COLLIDES — ESYSXLAT maps aarch64 `35` to
  `unlinkat`→`unlink`, so `syscall(35, ts, 0)` tries to unlink a
  garbage path. (The x86-nanosleep-35 vs aarch64-unlinkat-35
  clash is the same multiply-defined-number swamp as the .63
  getdents 61-vs-217 bug.)
- **Windows (PE)**: 35 is not a routed reroute → fault.

**The portable fix** (v6.0.65, `lib/chrono.cyr` `sleep_ms`):
- Linux + macOS: `poll(NULL, 0, ms)` — `poll` (x86 7 → Darwin 230)
  is ALREADY routed on both Mach-O backends (the .59 socket port),
  and `poll` with 0 fds + a timeout is the portable ms-granular
  sleep. Verified ≈ms-accurate on Linux + ecb + ach (the constant
  ~300ms "overhead" the first time on macOS is one-shot
  codesign/AMFI launch, not poll — baseline-subtract to confirm).
- Windows: a real kernel32 `Sleep(ms)` reroute (0xF00F), 1-arg,
  mirroring the SRWLock reroutes.
- agnos/cx: no-op (no sleep syscall / single-threaded VM).

**Windows dead-end worth knowing**: `WaitForSingleObject(handle,
ms)` on the current-PROCESS (-1) or current-THREAD (-2)
pseudo-handle returns IMMEDIATELY — it does NOT block for the
timeout (verified on cass: 0 ms). It only blocks on a genuinely
non-signaled object. So there's no "sleep via the already-routed
WaitForSingleObject" shortcut; you need a real `Sleep` reroute.

**Measurement gotcha (cmd.exe)**: `cmd /c "echo %TIME% & prog &
echo %TIME%"` prints the SAME time twice — `%TIME%` expands at
parse time (once), the `%errorlevel%` gotcha applied to TIME. Use
`cmd /v:on /c "... !TIME! ..."` or PowerShell `Measure-Command`.

**Recognize again**: any consumer doing raw `syscall(35)` for a
sleep on macOS/Windows; or a new "syscall not routed on Mach-O"
warning naming 35. The fix is always: route through
`lib/chrono.cyr sleep_ms`, never hardcode nanosleep.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "macos_owns_thread_pointer_register"
description = "macOS owns TPIDR_EL0 (arm64) / %fs (x86) — Darwin saves+restores it as thread state and overwrites any userspace `msr TPIDR_EL0` / arch_prctl on the FIRST preemption, so cyrius cannot durably own the thread-pointer register for thread-local storage on macOS."
detail = """
v6.0.84. Symptom: sigil's crypto-scratch bank (`cbank()` → `thread_local_get`,
lib/thread_local.cyr) SIGSEGV'd on real ecb (Apple Silicon) AFTER an ECDSA
sign — but every ingredient (sha256, hmac, fl_alloc, the secret vars, the
rfc6979 reject loop) ran clean in isolation. The .76 issue chased it to a
`&_fl_heads` freelist codegen bug via an early lldb; THAT DIAGNOSIS WAS WRONG —
the `&global` adrp+add path is provably correct (EVADDR emits a fresh
adrp+add per site, FIXUP_ADRP_ADD masks 0x9F00001F so it can't corrupt the
opcode, var-address resolution is deterministic per index).

Real cause: thread_local.cyr installs a per-thread block via `msr TPIDR_EL0`
(arm64) and reads it back via `mrs TPIDR_EL0`. On Linux the kernel lets EL0
own TPIDR_EL0. On Darwin the kernel uses it for its own thread self-data and
RESTORES it on every context switch. Decisive repro on ecb: install the
block (TPIDR=0x1xxxxxxxx), run a 2-billion-iteration pure-compute loop with
ZERO syscalls, read TPIDR again → 0x200d (a Darwin thread value). Pure
preemption, no syscall, clobbers it. `cbank()` lazy-inits ONCE
(`_crypto_tls_inited` guard), so the first preemption during crypto poisons
every later `thread_local_get`. This is why it looked "context-dependent,
after a preceding svc" — it's preemption, and a long crypto op always gets
preempted.

How to recognize: lldb shows the fault at `ldr xN, [x1, x0, lsl #3]` right
after `mrs x1, TPIDR_EL0`, with x1 a tiny value (~0x2010), NOT a heap addr.

Fix: a macOS process-global slot array (no TPIDR), like the Windows TlsAlloc
path. Safe because cyrius threads don't run on macOS at all — thread.cyr is
clone(2)-only (no Darwin bsdthread path) — so crypto-scratch is
single-threaded. Since preemption (not a syscall) is the trigger, NO
"re-msr before access" scheme is reliable; you must not use the register.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "emit_fmt_macho_before_pe_precedence"
description = "When deriving an output-format selector (`_emit_fmt`) from the _TARGET_* flags, test Mach-O BEFORE PE — matching the pre-existing EMITELF ladder's order. _TARGET_MACHO and _TARGET_PE come from INDEPENDENT, unguarded env vars (CYRIUS_MACHO / CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN; main.cyr has no mutual-exclusion guard), so the combined state is reachable. The old code let Mach-O win; flip the order and you silently change the conflicting-combo output (Mach-O → PE)."
detail = """
v6.0.89 (the _TARGET_* consolidation first bite: collapse the format fan-out +
entry-base ladder, duplicated in BOTH x86/fixup.cyr and aarch64/fixup.cyr, into
_emit_fmt(S)→{0=ELF,1=Mach-O-x86,2=Mach-O-ARM,3=PE} + _entry_base(S)).

An adversarial-review workflow caught a DRAFT _emit_fmt that checked `_TARGET_PE==1`
first: for `CYRIUS_MACHO=1 CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN=1` the old EMITELF (macho-first) emitted
Mach-O 8192B, the draft emitted PE 1536B — a real divergence, not a false alarm,
even though no sane build sets both. Fix: `if(_TARGET_MACHO==1)…;if(_TARGET_MACHO==2)
…;if(_TARGET_PE==1)…` so Mach-O wins, byte-identical for that combo too.

VERIFICATION SHAPE (this is the most fixup-fragile code — entry feeds every absolute
fixup): build .PREV-vs-.NEW compilers and diff EMITTED binaries across EVERY mode —
ELF-user, kernel ELF32+ELF64, shared, object, EFI (real source), AGNOS, PE, Mach-O
x86, Mach-O ARM, AND the Mach-O+PE conflict. A single-format self-host run only
exercises ELF; the macho/pe/kernel paths need the explicit matrix + ecb/ach/cass.
TEST-HARNESS TRAP that wasted a cycle: zsh does NOT word-split an unquoted `$var`, so
`env $combo sh -c …` with combo="A=1 B=1" set ONE malformed var and silently tested
the no-flags case — the output's ELF magic (not Mach-O) gave it away. Use literal
`env A=1 B=1 …`, and ALWAYS check the actual output bytes, not just the exit code.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "per_fn_local_tables_indexed_by_slot_not_var"
description = "The four per-fn local tables (fn_local_names / fn_local_depths / fn_local_types / fn_local_slice) are indexed by SFLC, which counts stack-frame SLOTS, NOT vars — a `stack var buf[N]` registers N/8 filler slots — so any frame larger than ~2 KB silently overran the old 256/576-entry tables (CVE-24). v6.1.40 relocated all four to the heap top at 16384 slots each + added a loud SFLC cap."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "local-tables", "sflc", "heap-cap", "overflow", "cve-24", "v6.1.40"]
content = """
**The trap (CVE-24)**: cyrius's four per-function local tables —
`fn_local_names`, `fn_local_depths`, `fn_local_types`,
`fn_local_slice` — are indexed by `SFLC` (the stack-frame local
count). `SFLC` counts stack-frame **SLOTS**, not user-visible
variables. A `stack var buf[N]` (a byte array on the frame) doesn't
register as one entry — it registers **N/8 filler slots** (one per
8-byte slot the array occupies). So a single `stack var buf[2048]`
burns 256 table entries by itself.

The old tables were sized 256 / 576 entries. Any function whose
frame exceeded ~2 KB of slot-arrays silently ran `SFLC` past the
table end and wrote past the region — a heap overrun with no
diagnostic. Self-host never tripped it (cycc's own fns stay small),
so it was invisible until a consumer with a large stack buffer (the
daimon report) hit it.

**The fix (v6.1.40)**: relocate all four tables to the heap top,
each sized **16384 slots**:

  - fn_local_names   @ 0x5D9D000
  - fn_local_depths  @ 0x5DBD000
  - fn_local_types   @ 0x5DDD000
  - fn_local_slice   @ 0x5DFD000

plus a LOUD `SFLC` cap check (error + exit, not silent truncation)
so a future overrun reports the slot count and the file to raise,
instead of corrupting the heap.

**The footgun behind the report — local vs global array sizing**:
the array-size convention differs by scope, which is exactly what
let the frame grow past expectation:

  - **local** `var a[N]` = **N BYTES**, rounded UP to 8 (so
    `var a[5]` still consumes one 8-byte slot; `var a[2048]`
    consumes 256 slots).
  - **global** `var a[N]` = **N i64 SLOTS** (N*8 bytes).

So a buffer that looks identical in source (`var a[2048]`) is 2 KB
on the stack but 16 KB in `.bss` as a global. Size local arrays by
BYTE count; size globals by slot count. Mixing the two mental
models is how the daimon frame quietly grew past the old cap.

**Recognize again**: any "large stack buffer corrupts compiler
state / heap" shape, or a consumer whose fn has a multi-KB
`stack var buf[N]`. Check `SFLC` against the table cap; the slot
count is `frame_bytes / 8`, not the var count. Related:
`large_fn_local_var_array_is_bss_not_stack` (the dual — big
fn-local arrays land in `.bss`, not the stack frame).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "phase0_growable_relocation_and_scattered_cap"
description = "Two mechanics that bite when migrating cycc's fixed tables to growable: (1) a relocating buffer (codebuf) is only safe if every access re-derives from the base gvar and never holds a captured pointer across an EB — and the disp32 patch sites do an 8-byte read-modify-write that bypasses EB's cap-check, so the grow needs a guard band; (2) the 8192 fn-cap was a magic literal scattered across 6 subsystems and textually colliding with 5 UNRELATED 8192-capped tables, so the swap had to be surgical per-context, never a global replace."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["growable", "codebuf", "fn-tables", "relocation", "magic-number", "v6.2.0"]
content = """
**Relocation safety (codebuf)**: `_codebuf_grow` `alloc()`s a new
buffer and repoints `_codebuf_base`. That is only correct because
EVERY codebuf access re-derives `_codebuf_base + offset` fresh:
- the disp32 patch sites (`var a = _codebuf_base + GCP(S); S64(a, …)`)
  capture-and-store with NO `EB` in between, so no grow can run
  between the capture and the use;
- the DSE/LASE/regalloc peephole passes read+patch already-emitted
  code AFTER all emit, when the buffer is stable.
A pattern that captured `_codebuf_base + cp` and then called `EB`
(which can grow → relocate) before using it would dereference the
freed old buffer. There is none today — keep it that way.

The disp32 stores also do an 8-byte read-modify-write at `cp`
(`S64(a, (L64(a) >> 32 << 32) | disp)`), writing 4 bytes PAST the
4-byte slot, and they bypass `EB`'s cap-check entirely. So
`_codebuf_grow` triggers at `cp >= _codebuf_cap - 64` (a 64-byte
guard band), not at the exact cap, or the last RMW before a grow
would write past the buffer end. The cx bytecode backend has its
OWN codebuf at `0x54A000` (separate region, still fixed 512 KB) —
it is NOT `_codebuf_base`.

**The scattered magic number (fn-tables)**: `8192` (the fn-cap)
was hard-coded in ~6 independent places — `REGFN`'s cap-check,
the `fn_name_hash` + `fn_start_hash` probe/mask/clear bounds, the
`live[1024]` DCE bitmap, and the 85 %-full warning — with NO single
source of truth. Worse, the literal `8192` also appears in 5
UNRELATED capped tables (var table, IR pool, PE reloc, struct
field pool, the TS scratch stack). A blind `8192 → _fnt_cap` would
have corrupted those. The migration was surgical per-context:
`& 8191 → & _fnt_hash_mask` (hash masks only), `probes/sh_clr <
8192 → _fnt_cap` (hash bounds only), `live[1024] → live[4096]`
(the DCE bitmap, sized to the 32768 ceiling), REGFN's check → the
grow call. Everything now reads one `_fnt_cap`.

**Recognize again**: any "raise/remove a fixed cap" task where the
cap value is a bare literal. Grep first; a literal that doubles as
an unrelated table's size will silently break that table.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cross_os_self_host_real_hardware_non_negotiable"
description = "Cross-OS self-host on REAL hardware — ecb (macOS arm64), cass (Windows PE), pi (aarch64 Linux), one ssh away each — is a STANDING release gate, not a closeout-only or CI-only check. A GREEN CI CHECK IS NOT VERIFICATION. Two v6.3.x bugs shipped SILENTLY behind green smoke and were caught ONLY by running on the hardware: (1) v6.3.43 — Windows sys_write/sys_read on FILE handles (EWRITE_PE always translated the fd → GetStdHandle, so only std handles 0/1/2 ever worked; a CreateFileW handle wrote to garbage) — never caught because no test had ever written to a FILE on Windows. (2) v6.3.44 — getpid returned positive GARBAGE on aarch64-macOS (untranslated syscall 172) — a callable-only smoke passed on it. Hello-world / exit-code / callable-only smoke is a PLACEBO; the actual behavior asserted on the actual hardware IS the test. The macOS COMPILER self-host rotted silently ~9 minors behind a CI job that only ran hello-world."
type = "gotcha"
tags = ["cross-os", "self-host", "ecb", "cass", "pi", "release-gate", "found-by-ports", "smoke-is-placebo", "v6.3.43", "v6.3.44"]
body = """
**A checkmark is not the compiler running on the hardware.** After ANY
compiler-backend or stdlib change, cycc must self-host BYTE-IDENTICAL and
the platform tests must PASS with real assertions on real macOS (ecb,
arm64), real Windows (cass, PE), and real aarch64 Linux (pi) — each one
`ssh` away via `~/.ssh/config`. This is a STANDING per-release gate (the
LIBTEST `vr01_` glob + `SELFHOST_OK`/`LIBTEST_OK` on ecb/cass/pi), not a
closeout-only or CI-only step.

**Two silent-rot cases from v6.3.x, both found only by running:**
  - **v6.3.43, Windows file I/O.** `EWRITE_PE` (src/backend/x86/emit.cyr)
    ALWAYS translated the fd into a *standard handle* via GetStdHandle
    (`neg;sub 10`), so `sys_write`/`sys_read` only ever worked for fd
    0/1/2 — a real `CreateFileW` handle (a large value) mistranslated into
    a bogus std-handle index → WriteFile to an invalid handle → the whole
    file round-trip silently failed. Never caught because NO test had ever
    written to a FILE on Windows (tests/win/ was all stdout). Fix: an
    unsigned `cmp rax,2; ja` fd-branch (fd>2 = handle direct), mirroring
    the fix EREAD_PE already had since v6.0.45.
  - **v6.3.44, aarch64-macOS getpid.** getpid returned positive GARBAGE
    (an untranslated syscall 172) — see
    `macho_syscall_xlat_source_numbers_arch_specific`. A callable-only
    smoke passed on the garbage.

**The precedent.** The macOS COMPILER self-host rotted silently for ~9
minors (v5.3.13 → v6.0.32, 400+ patches) behind a CI job named "Mach-O
ARM64 Native ✓" that only ran hello-world / exit-code programs and never
once built or self-hosted cycc. It surfaced only when a human installed on
a Mac and got a broken toolchain. Hello-world / exit-code / callable-only
smoke is a PLACEBO — the compiler self-hosting on the target, and the
actual behavior asserted on the actual hardware, IS the test.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "vex_encoding_vvvv_ones_complement_and_fma_cpuid_leaf"
description = "The v6.4.8/.9 SIMD arc emitted the FIRST VEX/AVX instructions the toolchain has ever produced (f32v8 256-bit: 2-byte C5 VEX for vaddps/vsubps/vmulps at v6.4.8; 3-byte C4 VEX for vfmadd231ps + vextractf128 at v6.4.9). Two traps bit here that hand-rolled hex-literal encoders on the SSE side never had. (1) THE VEX vvvv FIELD IS THE 1's-COMPLEMENT OF THE src1 REGISTER — the non-destructive 3-operand form encodes src1 in the inverted vvvv nibble, so a wrong-polarity vvvv assembles to a VALID instruction that silently reads the WRONG register (no crash, no decode error — just wrong math). It is INVISIBLE to a size/self-host check and can even pass a coarse value test if the mis-read register happens to alias. The only defense is to round-trip EVERY new VEX op through a real disassembler (llvm-mc / llvm-objdump) and diff the mnemonic+operands against intent — a disasm gate, not an eyeball. (2) FMA NEEDS A DIFFERENT CPUID LEAF/BIT THAN AVX2 — the runtime feature probe for vfmadd231ps is CPUID leaf 1, ECX bit 12 (simd_has_fma), NOT the AVX2 probe of leaf 7, EBX bit 5 (simd_has_avx2). They are genuinely separate capabilities (a CPU can have AVX2 without FMA in principle), so a wrapper that gates FMA on the AVX2 bit is wrong; each 256-bit op checks the RIGHT bit and falls back (2×SSE, or scalar for FMA). AVX2/FMA are NOT x86-64 baseline — the CPUID probe + fallback is mandatory, not optional. Corollary from the same arc: decode.cyr's VEX length-decode was DROPPED because it collided with a pre-existing SYSCALL/CPUID mis-decode of no-ModRM 0F opcodes (filed P3) — the disassembler gate uses external llvm tooling, not the in-tree decoder."
category = "cyrius"
---
The v6.4.4–.9 SIMD compute arc took x86 codegen from SSE-only into
**AVX/VEX** territory for the first time: v6.4.8 emits 2-byte (C5) VEX
for the f32v8 elementwise ops (vaddps/vsubps/vmulps on ymm), v6.4.9 adds
3-byte (C4) VEX for `vfmadd231ps` + the 8-lane `vextractf128` reduce.
Two traps here have NO analogue in the older hand-rolled SSE hex-literal
emitters (EMIT_F32V_LOOP etc. in src/backend/x86/float.cyr).

**TRAP 1 — the VEX `vvvv` field is the 1's-COMPLEMENT of src1.** The
non-destructive 3-operand VEX form encodes the first source register in
the `vvvv` nibble **inverted** (`~reg & 0xF`). Get the polarity wrong
and you STILL assemble a valid instruction — it just reads a DIFFERENT
register. No SIGILL, no decode failure, no assembler complaint: silently
wrong math. It is invisible to a size/self-host check (the bytes are a
legal encoding) and can even sneak past a coarse value test if the
mis-read register happens to hold a compatible value. **The only defense
is a disassembler gate:** round-trip every new VEX op through real
external tooling (llvm-mc / llvm-objdump), diff the decoded
mnemonic+operands against intent. Eyeballing the hex is not enough — the
whole point of the trap is that the wrong bytes look plausible.

**TRAP 2 — FMA and AVX2 are DIFFERENT CPUID capabilities.** The runtime
probe for `vfmadd231ps` is **CPUID leaf 1, ECX bit 12**
(`simd_has_fma`), which is a DIFFERENT leaf AND bit from the AVX2 probe
**CPUID leaf 7 (ECX=0), EBX bit 5** (`simd_has_avx2`). They are not
interchangeable — a part can advertise AVX2 without FMA — so gating the
FMA path on the AVX2 bit is a real bug. AVX2/FMA are NOT x86-64 baseline;
each 256-bit wrapper MUST probe the correct bit and branch to a fallback
(2×SSE for elementwise, scalar for FMA) when absent. This is mandatory,
not a nicety — a plain `-march=x86-64` box will `#UD` on an unguarded
`vaddps ymm`.

**Corollary — the in-tree decoder was NOT extended for VEX.** During the
arc, `decode.cyr`'s VEX length-decode was DROPPED because it collided
with a PRE-EXISTING mis-decode of no-ModRM 0F opcodes (SYSCALL/CPUID),
filed P3 (issues/2026-07-05-decode-len-mislengths-no-modrm-0f-opcodes.md).
So the disasm gate deliberately uses EXTERNAL llvm tooling, not the
in-tree `decode.cyr` — don't reach for the in-tree decoder to validate
VEX bytes; it can't, and fixing it is a separate tracked item.

**Recognize again.** Any first-of-a-new-encoding-family codegen
(VEX→EVEX, a new prefix, a new 3-operand form) where the encoding packs
an operand in a non-obvious way (inverted, split across bytes): assume a
plausible-but-wrong encoding will assemble cleanly and pass self-host,
and add a disasm round-trip gate BEFORE trusting value tests. And any new
ISA-extension op: find its EXACT CPUID leaf+bit (they are not grouped by
"AVX-ness") and wire a runtime fallback.

[[entries]]
name = "two_step_bootstrap_for_toplevel_array_sizing_and_legit_codegen_diff"
description = "Two paired differential-testing / bootstrap gotchas from the v6.4.x SIMD-and-fixes minor. (1) A LAYOUT change to how cycc sizes its OWN data needs a TWO-STEP BOOTSTRAP, same as a heap-map or W^X change. v6.4.10 fixed a P1 kernel-blocker: a bare top-level `var X[N]` was silently 8× UNDER-sized (N bytes, the fn-local default) instead of N×8 (the PARSE_GVAR_ARR slot size) when the array was declared AFTER the first bare top-level statement — because pass-2 PARSE_ARRAY fell through to the fn-local N-byte path. Fixed in parse_decl.cyr (~61): a bare NON-fn-local array gets N×8. Because cycc's OWN top-level arrays were mis-sized under the old compiler, flipping the sizing needs the two-step (old cycc → cc5b, then cc5b compiles itself; cycc==cc5b) — a plain one-step fixpoint can bake in the old layout. This joins W^X-default-flip and array-convention as the recurring 'cycc's own storage layout changed → two-step' class. (2) A BUG FIX legitimately shows differential codegen-diff>0 WITH status-diff=0 — the opposite of a pure refactor (which must be codegen-diff=0). When a fix CHANGES emitted bytes for the affected inputs (e.g. correct array sizing, a sign-extension fix, a correct register), the differential corpus SHOULD show codegen-diff>0 on exactly the affected programs while status-diff stays 0 (nothing that passed now fails); a codegen-diff=0 on a supposed behavior fix means the fix didn't actually fire. Read the two axes separately: status-diff guards regressions, codegen-diff measures blast radius."
category = "cyrius"
---
Two paired gotchas about READING differential/bootstrap results, from the
v6.4.x minor's fixes (not the SIMD codegen itself).

**(1) A change to how cycc sizes its OWN top-level data needs a TWO-STEP
BOOTSTRAP.** v6.4.10 fixed a P1 kernel-blocker: a bare top-level
`var X[N]` was silently **8× under-sized** — it got N BYTES (the
fn-local array default) instead of N×8 (the `PARSE_GVAR_ARR` slot size)
**whenever it was declared AFTER the first bare top-level statement**,
because at that point pass-2 `PARSE_ARRAY` fell through to the fn-local
N-byte path instead of the global slot path. Fix (parse_decl.cyr ~61): a
bare **non-fn-local** array is sized N×8. The catch: **cycc's own
top-level arrays were affected**, so the sizing flip cannot be validated
by a one-step fixpoint alone — the old (mis-sizing) cycc could bake the
old layout into the new binary. Run the **two-step**: old cycc → `cc5b`,
then `cc5b` compiles itself, assert `cycc == cc5b` (+ seed-derive). This
is the SAME class as the W^X default-on flip and the local-vs-global
array-convention gotcha: **whenever a change alters cycc's own storage
layout / data sizing, it's a two-step, not a one-step.**

**(2) A real behavior fix SHOULD show differential codegen-diff > 0 with
status-diff = 0** — and that is CORRECT, not a red flag. The differential
harness reports two independent axes over the corpus:
  - **status-diff** — did any program's pass/fail status change? For a
    fix this MUST stay 0 (nothing that compiled+ran now breaks).
  - **codegen-diff** — did the emitted BYTES change, and for which
    programs? For a fix that changes what's emitted (array sizing, a
    sign-extension, a corrected register) this SHOULD be > 0, on exactly
    the affected programs.

The trap is conflating the two. A pure REFACTOR must be
codegen-diff = 0 (identical bytes). A BUG FIX is the opposite: if a
supposed behavior fix shows codegen-diff = 0, the fix **did not fire** —
you changed a path nothing exercises, or guarded it wrong. So read the
axes separately: status-diff is the regression guard; codegen-diff is the
blast-radius measurement. `codegen-diff > 0 && status-diff = 0` on the
programs you expected is exactly the signature of a landed, contained fix.

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_transcendental_polyfill_dispatch_and_dekker_dd_range_reduction"
category = "cyrius"
description = """
aarch64 has no native transcendentals — x86 gets them from x87 (fsin/fcos,
f2xm1/fscale for exp/exp2, fyl2x for ln/log2, fpatan for atan). Every f64
transcendental on aarch64 is therefore a lib/math.cyr POLYFILL that the aarch64
backend dispatches to. The dispatch pattern (v5.7.31 exp/ln → v6.4.16 sin/cos →
v6.4.25 exp2/atan) is identical: EF64_<OP>(S) in src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr does
`var pf = _FINDFN_CSTR(S, "_f64_<op>_polyfill", <len>); if (pf<0) ERR_MSG(...requires
include lib/math.cyr...); EPUSHR(S); ECALLPOPS(S,1); ECALLFIX(S,pf);` — i.e. it emits
a 1-arg call into the stdlib fn resolved by name. The x86 EF64_<OP> in
src/backend/x86/emit.cyr keeps emitting native x87 bytes and is NOT touched. THREE
consequences that trip you up:

(1) x86 cycc stays BYTE-IDENTICAL across a pure-aarch64 transcendental change. The
x86 fork (src/main.cyr) includes ONLY backend/x86/emit.cyr — not the aarch64 emit —
and cycc does NOT include lib/math.cyr (it's a stdlib, not compiler source). So
adding a polyfill + rerouting the aarch64 EF64_* changes ONLY the three aarch64 forks
(main_aarch64{,_macho,_native}.cyr) and lib/math.cyr. The x86 self-host fixpoint and
the differential (343 inputs, codegen-diff=0) are trivially green — that is CORRECT,
not a missing test. The real verification is the aarch64 self-host fixpoint (build the
x86-hosted aarch64-emitting cross-compiler `main_aarch64.cyr | cycc`, then `| itself`,
cmp — byte-identical under qemu) PLUS running the actual polyfill on real hardware
(pi + ecb) via a vr01_ tcyr. A green x86 run proves nothing about the aarch64 path.

(2) The polyfill must NOT recurse into the builtin it backs. _f64_exp2_polyfill uses
round/sub/mul/add/to (NOT f64_exp2); _f64_atan_polyfill uses gt/div/sub/add/mul/neg +
bit ops (NOT f64_atan). Each polyfill compiles on x86 too (arch-independent body) but
is only INVOKED on aarch64 — x86 keeps the x87 path.

(3) Large-argument trig range reduction on a machine with only f64 add/sub/mul (no
scalar FMA builtin) requires a Dekker double-double. The single-constant reduction
`r = x - round(x*2/pi)*pi_2` catastrophically cancels for large |x| (rel err ~5e-2 at
1e15). The fix is a NO-FMA Dekker TwoProduct: split via (2^27+1), form x*(2/pi) and
frac*(pi/2) as hi+lo words with the constants pre-split into _SH/_SL halves so the
product is exact without fma. Validated numerically (Python float64 == IEEE754) BEFORE
touching the compiler — double-double is exact to ~1e-16 through |x|≈1e15; triple-double
is NOT needed. GATE it (|x| >= 8192) so the cheap small-angle path is untouched and
stays BYTE-IDENTICAL — the issue's "small path unchanged" is a byte-identity
requirement, not a vibe. The dd quadrant k must come from the dd sum (round(ph+plo)),
NOT a naive round(x*2/pi) which is off-by-one for large x — so the dd block writes k_f
locally, and (no multi-return in this compiler) the ~14-line block is inlined verbatim
in both sin and cos, same as their small-path reduction already is. Keep each emitted
Horner/dd line under 120 chars up front (cyrlint gate) by accumulating into a stepwise
var, not one deep f64_add(f64_add(...)) nest — cyrfmt won't split it for you.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "pe_kernel32_reroutes_fixed_sub_rsp_only_aligns_on_right_parity_closehandle_faults_on_misalign_wine_cannot_repro"
description = "Windows PE kernel32 reroutes must DYNAMICALLY 16-align rsp before the call — a fixed `sub rsp, 0x28` is a latent bug. Win64 requires rsp = 0 (mod 16) immediately before a `call`; a fixed sub-rsp achieves that only when the caller's rsp lands on the exact parity the offset assumes, which depends on the function's frame size + expr-stack depth + call chain — so it silently works or faults by context. CloseHandle (and any kernel32 fn with an internal aligned SSE access, e.g. movaps) faults 0xC0000005 on an 8-misaligned stack; GetExitCodeProcess/WaitForSingleObject happen to tolerate it, which is why the crash localizes to CloseHandle though its siblings share the bug. EXPOSED at v6.4.44 by the async subprocess resume (lib/async_win.cyr _async_process_task): main -> task_join -> _async_pump -> _async_step -> fncall1 -> resume -> CloseHandle is a deep stack, and TWO sequential async_run_process calls crashed on real cass while a SINGLE call and the BATCHED (spawn-all-then-join) form worked — pure alignment-parity luck. CreateProcessW's ECREATEPROC_PE was already rbx-anchor-aligned at v6.0.51, but its own comment asserted 'the <=4-arg reroutes tolerate a misalign this 10-arg call does not' — that generalization was FALSE. FIX: route each of the 8 fixed-offset reroutes (ECLOSE_PE/EWAIT_PE/EGETEXIT_PE/ESETHANDLE_PE/ECREATEPIPE_PE/EGETCMDLINE_PE/EGETTICKS_PE/EMMAP_PE) through the existing rbx-anchored helpers (_pe_call_0/2/3/4arg_aligned, _pe_srw_call_1arg: push rbx; mov rbx,rsp; and rsp,-16; sub rsp,0x20; call; mov rsp,rbx; pop rbx). cycc SHRANK ~4 KB (inline reroutes -> shared helpers). HOW TO RECOGNIZE: a real-Windows-ONLY 0xC0000005 that WINE cannot reproduce (WINE aligns leniently -> returned 42 throughout) AND flips crash<->pass with call-depth / caller-frame changes -> suspect a fixed-`sub rsp` reroute, not the callee. Localize with a WriteFile (syscall(1,1,...)) trace bracketing each op; bisect step-0 vs resume vs the post-fncall1 loop code with manual GQCS + fncall1 replays. Self-host + seed-derive stay byte-identical (PE-emit-only; cycc's Linux codegen never calls these). See CHANGELOG [6.4.44]."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "win64_retptr_shifted_deep_stack_param_homing_offbyone_prologue_postloop_missing_pp_shift_f32v8_make_lanes_567"
description = "On Win64 PE a by-value SIMD/struct return uses a HIDDEN RETPTR in RCX, so every user arg shifts down one ABI slot (a,b,c -> rdx/r8/r9; d,e,f,g,h -> stack). The callee prologue homes params in TWO places and they must agree on that retptr shift: the IN-loop homing (parse_fn.cyr ~3321) computes `_pp_shift = (_cur_fn_ret_stash>0 && !aarch64) ? 1 : 0` and calls ESTOREPARM(int_pc+_pp_shift, li) -- pidx shifted for the source Win64 slot, lidx contiguous for the dest local slot. But the POST-loop that homes the DEEP stack params (pidx>=6, parse_fn.cyr ~3404) did `ESTOREPARM(spi, spi, pc)` -- using spi as BOTH pidx AND lidx with NO shift. Correct only with no retptr. With one, it homed each deep stack param ONE LOCAL SLOT TOO DEEP (dest lidx = true+1) and stopped one param short (spi<pc never reached the last source idx), so the first excluded stack param was never homed, the rest got the previous param's value, and the last param's incoming bytes were ignored. The body reads each param at the contiguous -0x30-lidx*8 slot via FINDLOCAL, so it diverged. SYMPTOM: f32v8_make(a..h) returned lanes 0-4 correct but lanes 5,6,7 = garbage/shifted on real cass; Linux/SysV all 8 lanes correct (SIMD returns via the xmm PAIR there, no retptr, so _pl_shift==0 and the emit is textually the old code -> self-host byte-identical). FIX: mirror _pp_shift in the post-loop -- `pidx = spi + _pl_shift` (shifted source), `lidx = spi` (contiguous dest), start `spi = 6 - _pl_shift`, guard `pc + _pl_shift > 6` (also catches the exact-6-param retptr case pc>6 dropped). PRE-EXISTING since v6.4.31 (Win64 value-form SIMD params/returns); latent for many minors because vr01_f32v8_ctor.tcyr carried a stale comment saying it 'skips cass', but v6.4.31 made CYRIUS_HAS_VAL_SIMD_PARAMS true on PE so the native cass cycc actually RAN the f32v8 assertions -- surfaced only when the v6.4.44 permanent Windows async gate ran the full vr01_ corpus on cass. HOW TO RECOGNIZE: a value-form-SIMD/struct-returning fn with >6 params whose DEEP args (3rd+ stack arg) are wrong on Win64 but correct on Linux -> suspect the retptr-shift being applied inconsistently between the two prologue-homing sites. Confirm by disassembling BOTH the prologue homing (`mov rax,[rbp+src]; mov [rbp-dst],rax`) and the body read (FINDLOCAL disp = -((lidx+1)*8)-regalloc*8-ret_stash) -- they must land on the same local slot. The caller (ECALLPOPS / parse_decl retptr push) was CORRECT; the bug is callee-side. Same '_cur_fn_ret_stash disp<->idx off-by-one' class as project_v6431_win64_valueform_simd, here the one remaining site. See CHANGELOG [6.4.44]."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "shift_right_split_gtgt_stays_logical_gtgtgt_is_arithmetic_reverse_of_js_never_flip_gtgt"
description = "v6.4.46 added `>>>` as the ARITHMETIC (sign-preserving) right shift and KEPT `>>` LOGICAL (zero-fill) — the REVERSE of JS/Java (there `>>` is arithmetic, `>>>` logical). The split is NOT arbitrary: `>>` MUST stay logical because two load-bearing sites depend on it. (1) CRYPTO ROTATES: keccak/sha/chacha rotate via `(x << n) | (x >> (64-n))` — logical `>>` is required or the high bits sign-extend and the rotate is wrong; flipping `>>` to arithmetic silently miscompiles SHA-3, cascading into TLS (tls_native_alpn went red). (2) THE COMPILER'S OWN UNION FLAG: `ISUNION()` is `fcount >> 63` — logical `>>` gives 1 for a tagged (high-bit-set) fcount, arithmetic `>>` gives -1 and the union sizeof/layout miscompiles (structs went red). BOTH were caught ONLY by check.sh's corpus — the self-host fixpoint AND seed-derive stayed GREEN because cycc's own source didn't exercise the exact miscompiled path until the corpus ran (a reminder that green self-host is necessary, not sufficient). LESSON: do NOT 'fix' `>>` to arithmetic to match JS/Java — it is a deliberate, load-bearing choice; route signed-shift needs through the NEW `>>>` (IR_ASHR=26; x86 `sar rax,cl` = 48 D3 F8; aarch64 `asrv` = 0x9AC12800; cx op 37). The drishti bug that motivated it was a signed value silently zero-filled by `>>`; the fix is `>>>`, never a `>>` semantics flip."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["shift", "logical-shift", "arithmetic-shift", "operator", "crypto", "rotate", "union", "isunion", "sign-extension", "green-selfhost-not-sufficient", "v6.4.46"]

[[entries]]
name = "cfo_const_fold_rewind_class_four_occurrences_grep_the_shape_not_the_operator_and_every_tier_that_reenters_the_fold"
description = "THE most-repeated codegen defect of the v6.x line — four occurrences, each fix scoped to the tier its repro landed in and each declared complete. MECHANISM: the constant folder keeps `_cfo` (fold armed), `_cfv` (folded value) and `_cfp` (the code pointer captured at the START of the foldable region). A parse path clears `_cfo = 0` BEFORE calling a lower tier that RE-ARMS it — `PARSE_FACTOR` sets `_cfp = GCP(S)` then `_cfv = val; _cfo = 1` for a bare NUM — so the flag comes back on with `_cfp` pointing PAST code that was already emitted. The next operator then takes the const-fold branch with a live-but-stale fold and `SCP(S, _cfp)` rewinds the code pointer over that already-emitted code, replacing it with a constant. The symptom is always a silently wrong constant: no error, no warning, valid binary. OCCURRENCES: (1) 2026-06-11, cyrius-doom, `A * B * 4` -> 800 (the EIMUL path). (2) v6.4.74 — 17 sites in the `PARSE_TERM` tier (`/ % << >> >>>`): `100 >>> 1 + 1` == 2 (want 51), `100 >> 0 + 1` == 1 (want 101). `>>` and `<<` mostly escaped because their own fold path handles a literal RHS, so it bit exactly where that fold could not apply (shift count 0, or a value outside the 0x10000 window); a parenthesised RHS also masked it, since the paren path never re-arms. (3) v6.4.80 — 16 sites one tier UP in `PEXPR` (`+ - & | ^`): `1 - 2 + 3` == **5**, `2 - 5 + 10` == 15, `3 + 1 - 5 + 2` == 7. The emitted code is the expression with the leading `a -` deleted. Trigger is the `cfr >= 0` test in the subtract fold — a NEGATIVE intermediate fails it and takes the runtime fallback, and the fallback was the buggy one, which is exactly why non-negative intermediates were always right (`5 - 3 + 1` == 3) and it hid. Measured blast radius: 40 of 400 (10 %) systematic 3-term constant expressions wrong. (4) v6.4.81 — one tier DOWN in `EMIT_OP_DISPATCH`, struct operator overloading: it parses its RHS with `PARSE_TERM` (re-arms) and ends with `SESTYPE(S, 0)`, so the following `+`/`-` rewound over the emitted operator CALL — the overload silently deleted and replaced by a constant. `p * 3 + 1` == 4 (want 1004). The tell was a 2-of-4 asymmetry in four structurally identical lines: `add` (parse_expr.cyr:2809) and `sub` (:2837) already cleared `_cfo`; `mul` (:2550) and `div` (:2610) never did. THE LESSON: **grep the SHAPE, not the operator — and grep EVERY TIER that calls back into the fold, not just the tier the repro landed in.** A single `grep -c \"_cfo = 0; E.*PARSE_TERM\"` at .74 would have found the .80 sites eight releases earlier; at .80 the sweep recorded 'zero occurrences of the shape remain in parse_expr.cyr' and that was true only of PEXPR — the dispatch helper one level down was untouched. THE OTHER LESSON: **251/251 tcyr were byte-identical across the .80 fix, and that is the finding, not reassurance** — the corpus contained zero expressions of the failing shape, so 10 % of constant arithmetic was wrong with every gate green (self-host fixpoint, seed-derive, check.sh, cross-OS on four hosts). A byte-identical corpus proves a change did not perturb what the corpus covers; it says nothing about what it does not. COVERAGE TRAPS recorded while building the regression (`tests/tcyr/const_chained_multiply_fold.tcyr`, now 39 assertions and the single home of this one mechanism): an operator-overload helper whose body ALIASES the folded answer (`OpV_mul` returning `a * b`) passes while broken — make helpers return values plain integer arithmetic cannot produce; and getting a mutation to fail took three rounds, because `1 - 2 & 3` fires the SUB fallback not the AND one, AND needs a left operand folding to 0 (`0 & 1 & 1`), OR/XOR need a ZERO right-hand operand (`1 | 0 ^ 1`), and ADD is reachable only via i64 overflow (`9223372036854775807 + 1 + 1`) since a literal `crv` is never negative. Before that work 8 of the 16 .80 sites had no coverage at all. When a mutation PASSES, fuzz good-vs-mutant to find a discriminator rather than concluding the site is dead. Found at .80 by an adversarial doc-sweep verifier RUNNING the compiler to re-check an unrelated vidya claim about precedence — not by reading code. See CHANGELOG [6.4.74], [6.4.80], [6.4.81]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["_cfo", "_cfp", "const-fold", "code-pointer-rewind", "SCP", "PARSE_TERM", "PEXPR", "PARSE_FACTOR", "EMIT_OP_DISPATCH", "operator-overload", "grep-the-shape-not-the-operator", "byte-identical-is-not-coverage", "mutation-proof", "aliasing-helper", "v6.4.74", "v6.4.80", "v6.4.81"]

[[entries]]
name = "cybs_cannot_lex_gtgtgt_the_cycc_fixpoint_does_not_substitute_for_seed_derive"
description = "v6.4.74's constant folder first spelled its arithmetic shift `v >>> _CF_FACTOR(S)`. It compiled, self-hosted, and FIXPOINTED byte-identical on `build/cycc` — then `scripts/seed-derive-cycc.sh` failed at step [3/5] with a bare `syntax error` and nothing else. Cause: **cybs, the hand-assembly bootstrap compiler the 29 KB seed assembles, cannot lex `>>>`** — `bootstrap/cybs.cyr`'s `lexer_shr` knows only `>`, `>=`, `>>`, so it tokenised `>>` `>`. The `>>>` operator has existed since v6.4.46; cybs never learned it. Fixed by writing `_CF_ASR`, which sign-extends using only `>>`/`<<` and masks the count to 6 bits to match the backend's `sar`. This is the cleanest available demonstration of Release Gate item 2: **the cycc self-host fixpoint does NOT cover the seed chain.** `build/cycc` is a full v6.x compiler; cybs is far more limited and fails SILENTLY, or with an unattributed one-line error, on constructs `build/cycc` compiles fine — too many global/string-literal references in one function (the ceiling that forced `_var_grow` into its `_grow_g1..g7` tail-call chain, that made v6.4.77 split the 67-entry `TOKNAME_BUILTIN` out of `TOKNAME`, and that made v6.4.81 split `PP_FNAME_TOO_LONG` out of an already-heavy `PREPROCESS`), tail calls, and now newer operators. PRACTICAL RULES: run `seed-derive-cycc.sh` for ANY `src/` change on EVERY release, not only at closeouts (a doc/lib-only bump is the sole exception); when writing compiler source prefer the OLDEST spelling that works; when a new helper carries a large literal table, expect the ceiling and split the fn before the gate tells you to; and note that gen1 (cybs's output) being ~72 KB smaller than `build/cycc` is NORMAL — only `gen2 == build/cycc` matters, do not chase it. RECOGNITION: a bare `syntax error` from step [3/5] with no file, no line and no token is the signature — read it as cybs hitting a ceiling, not as a defect in your source. See CHANGELOG [6.4.74]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cybs", "seed-derive", "bootstrap-chain", "gtgtgt", "arithmetic-shift", "_CF_ASR", "release-gate-item-2", "silent-failure", "global-reference-ceiling", "fixpoint-is-not-enough", "v6.4.74"]

[[entries]]
name = "the_heap_map_is_machine_read_prose_on_the_map_line_is_parsed_as_the_size_and_unit_suffixes_were_invisible"
description = "`src/main.cyr`'s heap map is NOT documentation — `tests/heapmap.sh` parses it with awk and it IS the overlap gate. Two parser holes made that gate blind for years, both fixed at v6.4.81, and both are things to know BEFORE editing a map line. (a) **Unit suffixes did not exist.** The size pattern was `/\\[([0-9]+)\\]/` — a bare integer only — so every entry written `[16 MB]`-style was skipped ENTIRELY and the region did not exist as far as the auditor was concerned. That hid **20.02 MB of live heap**: `ir_nodes [16 MB]`, `ir_cp [4 MB]`, the lex_pp file scratch `[16KB]` and three PP `#derive` scratch regions. The failure mode is worse than a missing row — the auditor then reported a **phantom ~21.4 MB free gap** above `ir_live_out` that is in fact fully occupied, so a future region placed 'in the free space' would land inside `ir_nodes` and the gate would report PASS. The pattern now accepts `KB/MB/K/M`. (b) **It took the LAST bracketed number on the line**, so prose after the size won. The `fn_param_struct_mask` line ends `(v6.3.36 issue [5]: bit N = …)` and was parsed as **FIVE BYTES** — off by 13,107x. The same trap immediately bit the v6.4.81 `include_fname` correction: the corrected line carried the old `0x190500 [256]` in an explanatory parenthetical and the region was re-parsed as 256 B. The parser now takes the FIRST bracketed size after the name. **DURABLE RULE: a map line carries offset, name, size and a short label — keep every explanatory parenthetical, historical address and issue reference on CONTINUATION lines that contain no `0x… [N]` pattern.** This is the third instance of the class: v5.5.40 fixed a whitespace pattern that had silently dropped every region past 0xFC000, because 7-char hex offsets left only ONE space before the name. A heap auditor that cannot see a region cannot guard it, and it reports green either way. State after the fix: **100 regions, 0 overlaps** — numerically the same as through v6.4.74 but by a different route (94 at .75, when six fn-indexed side tables went lazy-alloc and their fixed bands were freed; back to 100 at .81 when the parser learned to see unit-suffixed entries). Do not read the recurring '100' as the old number having been right all along. Mutation-prove any change here by planting a probe region inside an existing one and requiring exit 1. See CHANGELOG [5.5.40], [6.4.81]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["heap-map", "heapmap.sh", "awk-parser", "unit-suffix", "prose-on-the-map-line", "continuation-lines", "phantom-free-gap", "ir_nodes", "fn_param_struct_mask", "region-count-100", "mutation-proof", "v5.5.40", "v6.4.81"]

[[entries]]
name = "a_heap_map_entry_can_document_a_region_no_code_has_ever_written_and_the_auditor_will_validate_the_fiction"
description = "The preprocessor include / `#ref` filename scratch was recorded in the heap map as `0x190500  include_fname [256]`. **No code has ever written 0x190500.** Every real use is `0x190400`, and it was UNBOUNDED — three separate copy loops (`include`, post-macro-expansion `include`, `#ref`) with no length check. So `tests/heapmap.sh` spent three minors proving non-overlap for a region that exists only in a comment, one page above a live region it therefore never checked. The live one could be walked straight through the include-count cell at `S+0x197F00`: `include \"zzz/<31490 A's>.cyr\"` **SIGSEGV'd cycc (exit 139)**, while 31480 bytes still gave a clean error — an exact boundary, with everything below it corrupting preprocessor state silently. That is CVE-32 (High), fixed at v6.4.81 by bounding all three loops at 4096 B through a shared `PP_FNAME_TOO_LONG` helper — split into its own fn because `PREPROCESS` was already near cybs's per-fn literal/global ceiling and seed-derive is the gate that would have caught the overflow. THE GENERALISATION: **the heap map is a machine-read CLAIM ABOUT the code, and nothing cross-checks the two directions.** A gate over the map proves the MAP is self-consistent; it says nothing about whether the code obeys it. So when auditing the map, do not read down the list checking arithmetic — **grep the tree for each base address, confirm a writer exists at exactly that address, and confirm its writes are bounded.** An entry with no writer is either dead (delete it) or a decoy sitting one page away from a live unbounded region (fix the code first, then the map). Companion instances from the very same audit, all three passing the gate: the map claimed a 13.3 MB TS frontend reservation that does not exist at all; `src/main.cyr` and `src/frontend/ts/lex.cyr` both quoted a stale TS base of `0x1D0B000` that lands inside `fixup_tbl`; and the live TS base overlapped `tok_types` by 10 MB. Sibling CVEs found the same way and worth the same treatment — `READFILE`'s `CYRIUS_HOME/lib/` fallback composed into `var fbuf[512]` with NEITHER copy loop bounded (CVE-33), and `var _cyrius_lib[256]` is a BARE TOP-LEVEL array so it is 256x8 = 2048 B per the v6.4.10 contract, with a `HOME=` copy bounded only by `elen` and ~32 further bytes appended unconditionally afterwards (CVE-34). cycc compiles untrusted source by design; every one of these is on the wrong side of that boundary. See CHANGELOG [6.4.81]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["heap-map", "include_fname", "CVE-32", "CVE-33", "CVE-34", "unbounded-copy", "preprocessor", "PP_FNAME_TOO_LONG", "gate-validates-fiction", "grep-for-the-writer", "cybs-ceiling", "untrusted-source", "bare-toplevel-array-is-Nx8", "v6.4.81"]

[[entries]]
name = "win64_ecallpops_five_register_shuttle_broke_at_ten_args_and_the_bug_was_written_down_as_a_language_rule"
description = "**The Win64 caller-side stack-arg path corrupted argument 1 AND never wrote the stack args at all for any call with >=10 arguments** — silently, on a Tier-1 target, for about a year. Fixed v6.4.64. Win64 passes 4 args in `rcx/rdx/r8/r9` and the rest at `[rsp+32..]`; because cyrius pushes args left-to-right (aN on top) but Win64 wants a5 at the LOWEST stack slot, the caller must REVERSE the extras. The PE branch of `ECALLPOPS` did that by shuttling them through a FIXED 5-register table (`r10/r11/r14/r15/r12`) with `if (nextra >= 1..5)` pops and `if (nextra == 1..5)` write-backs. With `nextra = n - 4`, `n >= 10` fell off the end of its own table two ways at once: only 5 of the >=6 extras were popped, so the following `pop r9/r8/rdx/rcx` read SHIFTED slots and `rcx` (argument 1) got the wrong value; and no `nextra ==` branch matched, so the stack args were never emitted and the callee read garbage. Both silent — wrong answers, no error, no warning. Measured on real cass: arity 5-9 correct, 10/11/12 corrupt. SysV had been migrated off the identical register shuttle to rsp-relative addressing at v5.6.24/v6.0.57 for exactly this bug class — **the Win64 branch was never brought along**, which is the durable structural warning: when one ABI branch is rewritten to fix a class, open the sibling branch in the same commit. FIX SHAPE if you touch it again: load `rcx/rdx/r8/r9` IN PLACE via `_emov_rsp_disp` (disp8/disp32 auto-select, no pops); carve the frame BELOW the pushed args so the reversed copy's destination is strictly below its source and cannot overlap at ANY arity (an in-place reversal cannot be done by a single ascending or descending shift — every order clobbers a slot a later read still needs); reverse-copy through `r10`, which is CALLER-saved (the v5.6.24 lesson — r12-r15 are callee-saved and regalloc may pin caller locals there); and share one frame formula between `ECALLPOPS` and `ECALLCLEAN` (`_pe_call_frame`: 32 shadow + nextra*8, 16-rounded, +8 parity pad for odd `n` — the old path got alignment free by popping all `n` slots first, so an in-place path must pay for it explicitly). **WHY IT SURVIVED: it had been written into CLAUDE.md as a LANGUAGE RULE** — 'fns take <=6 args cleanly (register ABI); args 7+ go on the stack and have shown corruption — restructure instead.' Wrong in BOTH directions (7-9 were always fine; the cliff was 10, on one target), it read as settled so nobody re-measured, and on 2026-07-14 it was cited to file an issue **against sigil**, asking a stdlib to restructure around a defect in the compiler that compiles it. The release gate also could not see it structurally: sigil sits outside cycc's include closure and cycc's own PE calls happen to stay <=9 args. The live consumer was sigil 3.12.0 — `argon2id_into` takes 10 args, `argon2_hash_into` 17 — i.e. a password KDF returning a wrong-but-plausible hash, and sandhi's HTTP client (11 fns taking >=10 args, 15 call sites passing >=10) was miscompiled on Win64 in both 1.8.2 and 1.9.0. **THIS IS THE LANGUAGE REPO: when the compiler cannot compile valid cyrius, fix the compiler. If a rule here tells you to work around codegen, treat the rule as the bug report.** Gate: `tests/tcyr/crossos/win64_stack_args.tcyr` (arity 5/9/10/17 + nested), mutation-tested on real cass — old codegen 2 passed / 3 failed, fixed 5 / 0. See CHANGELOG [6.4.64]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["win64", "PE", "ECALLPOPS", "ECALLCLEAN", "stack-args", "nextra", "arity", "_pe_call_frame", "r10-caller-saved", "sysv-migrated-pe-was-not", "codegen-bug-as-language-rule", "sigil-argon2", "sandhi-http", "vr01_win64_stack_args", "v5.6.24", "v6.4.64"]

[[entries]]
name = "a_temporal_invariant_can_guard_a_spatial_collision_invisibly_ts_arena_overlapped_tok_types_by_10mb"
description = "The TS frontend arena was a FIXED base — `S + 0x298B000`, with `TS_HEAP_SIZE = 0xD81000` — so it ran `0x298B000..0x370C000` and **overlapped `tok_types` entirely plus 1.6 MB of `tok_values`: 10,027,008 bytes.** Nothing was ever corrupted, because all three TS modes (`--lex-ts`, `--parse-ts`, `--emit-js`) `SYS_EXIT` before `PREPROCESS` and `LEX`, so the token arrays are never populated while the arena is live. **A TEMPORAL invariant guarding a SPATIAL collision** — held by nothing but call ordering, written down nowhere, and one added 'lex the TS then keep going' path away from a 10 MB heap smash. TWO THINGS MADE IT UNDETECTABLE. `tests/heapmap.sh` structurally could not see it: the base was a **code literal at the call sites, not a map entry**, and the auditor only knows the map. And the map actively said something else — it claimed a 13.3 MB TS reservation between `fixup_tbl` end (`0x207B000`) and `tok_types` (`0x2D7C000`), which does not exist and is 524,288 B smaller than `TS_HEAP_SIZE` regardless. THE ORIGIN IS THE DURABLE PART: `0x298B000 - 0x207B000 = 9,502,720` — **exactly the 9.06 MB the v5.11.68 reorg shrank the heap by.** That reorg moved ~35 region bases and MISSED THIS ONE LITERAL, which had been the pre-reorg `fixup_tbl` end. Which is the entire argument for the v6.4.82 fix: the arena is now `alloc(TS_HEAP_SIZE)`, taken only when a TS mode is actually active, so a normal compile pays nothing and the brk carries no TS carve-out at all. `src/frontend/ts/lex.cyr` had documented `alloc()` as the contract the test drivers use all along — the fixed base was the deviation. **A literal is what the next reorg misses too.** Relocating to another fixed base was never really available anyway: it needs ~14.2 MB contiguous, which does not exist below the arena end, so a fixed-base fix would have been a brk/heap-LAYOUT change requiring the two-step bootstrap — which is why v6.4.81 deliberately filed it rather than bundling it into the tail of an already-gated release. LESSONS: when auditing the heap, grep for `S + 0x` literals in CALL ARGUMENTS, not just map lines; a subsystem reachable only in an early-exit mode is exactly where a spatial invariant rots unnoticed, because no test ever runs past the exit; and prefer `alloc()` to a new fixed base for anything not required to sit at a fixed offset. See CHANGELOG [5.11.68], [6.4.81], [6.4.82]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["ts-frontend", "ts-arena", "TS_HEAP_SIZE", "temporal-invariant", "spatial-collision", "tok_types", "heap-layout", "alloc-over-fixed-base", "v5.11.68-reorg", "missed-literal", "heapmap-blind-spot", "two-step-bootstrap", "early-exit-mode", "v6.4.82"]

[[entries]]
name = "toktyp_reads_past_gtcnt_as_token_type_0_never_12_eof_so_every_eof_test_fails_and_recovery_cannot_terminate"
description = "`TOKTYP` (`src/backend/common/tokens.cyr`) is an unchecked `L64`. Past `GTCNT` it reads zeroed heap and returns token type **`0`, never `12` (EOF)**. Once panic-mode multi-error recovery existed (v6.4.62) that had a compounding consequence: every `t == 12` EOF test in `_sync_skip` and in the block loops silently failed, recovery could never terminate normally, and `PARSE_STMT`'s forced-progress guard was disarmed too — it only advances while `GTI < GTCNT`, which is exactly the condition that is false out there. Input ending mid-construct — a clipped file, an unclosed `fn`, EOF mid-expression or mid-arg-list — produced **166,670 stderr lines** before the v6.4.62 watchdog aborted. Clamped to EOF inside `PEEKT` at v6.4.78: 166,670 -> 5 lines (unclosed `fn`: 3). **THE PERF OBJECTION THAT GOT IT DEFERRED DID NOT SURVIVE CONTACT WITH THE FIX.** It was filed as its own slot because `PEEKT` is THE hottest function in the parser and a compare+branch there is a hot-path change. But `PEEKT` ALREADY has an `if (_had_error == 1)` guard for the watchdog, and the runaway is exclusively a POST-ERROR phenomenon — LEX unconditionally appends an EOF token and every parse loop stops on type 12, so a well-formed parse never advances past it. Putting the clamp INSIDE that existing guard therefore costs nothing on a clean compile: measured **-0.07 %** (672.8 vs 673.3 ms, interleaved medians on one box), size-neutral, 251/251 tcyr byte-identical. Clamping unconditionally in `TOKTYP` would also work and would tax every lookahead for no benefit. LESSON: **before deferring a hot-path fix on cost, check whether an existing guard already gates the path** — the cheap placement often already exists, and the fix that was 'too expensive to bundle' turns out to be free. The v6.4.62 watchdog is kept deliberately alongside the clamp — a `_had_error`-gated stall detector in `PEEKT` (>500,000 reads with `GTI` unmoved, far above any legal recursive-descent depth so it never false-fires) that bounds ANY future desync the clamp does not anticipate, and it is what broke the VR-02 fuzz wall that had stopped two prior attempts at multi-error recovery. GATE THE SHAPE THAT MATTERS: a garbage-TOKEN input can happen to recover — the pre-existing case did, and missed this entirely — so the gate uses inputs ending MID-CONSTRUCT, asserts <=200 stderr lines, no hang, no SIGSEGV, no output, **and that the watchdog does NOT fire** (its firing is itself the regression signal). Independently fuzz-verified with 60 deterministic random truncations of `lib/str.cyr`: worst case 166,680 -> 18 lines, 0 hangs. See CHANGELOG [6.4.62], [6.4.78]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["TOKTYP", "PEEKT", "GTCNT", "EOF-token-12", "token-type-0", "panic-mode", "_sync_skip", "PARSE_STMT-progress-guard", "watchdog", "hot-path-objection", "existing-guard", "VR-02-fuzz", "truncated-input", "v6.4.62", "v6.4.78"]

[[entries]]
name = "textual_includes_mean_per_item_file_identity_must_be_stamped_at_preprocess_time_and_the_parser_is_two_pass"
description = "Per-item FILE IDENTITY is the whole substrate under `public`/`private` (v6.5.0), and cyrius includes are TEXTUAL — by parse time there is one flat expanded buffer and the origin file of each fn/var has to have been recorded on the way through. Three traps, all of which shipped green before being caught. (1) **The preprocessor must emit a RESUME marker after an include closes, not just an ENTER marker before it opens.** With enter-only markers every fn declared in the enclosing file AFTER an include is attributed to the INCLUDED file, so `alloc_init` and `atomic_cas` — different files — collided on id 19 and were mutually visible under `private`. **My Phase 1 gate passed anyway because the fixture put its includes first and declared nothing after them**, which is the shape that cannot observe the bug; the rebuilt gate uses a nested include AND code-before-include, and was run RED first to prove it. If you add file tracking to a textual-include preprocessor, the fixture must have code after an include or the gate is decorative. (2) **The parser is TWO-PASS and a pending-modifier flag leaks across the boundary.** `_pub_pending` was consumed in `PARSE_FN_DEF` (pass 2) but not in `_prescan_fn_sig` (pass 1), so the flag set by a `public` in pass 1 survived into the next file — `private` worked in a main source and SILENTLY did nothing when the file arrived via an include. Any per-item modifier needs consuming in BOTH passes; stamp the fileid in both too. (3) **A byte-identical binary is a TELL, not a reassurance.** The Phase 2b var stamp was inserted inside the annotated-only branch (`ann_scalar < 8`), so it fired only for `var x: i32` and never for a bare `var x = 7;`. Nothing failed — the rebuild just came back byte-identical when a real behaviour change had been made, which is the signal that the edit is on a path the compiler's own source never takes. Move the stamp before the width branch (`SVCNT`) and it changes. RECOGNITION for the class: when adding cross-file semantics to a language whose includes are textual, every question of the form 'which file did this come from' is answered at PREPROCESS time or not at all — the parser cannot recover it later. See CHANGELOG [6.5.0]."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "parse_return_tail_path_is_the_third_place_found_missing_what_parse_fncall_has"
description = "The overload-suffix dispatch carried TWO independent defects from v5.10.25 to v6.5.1, and the pair is a lesson about WHERE to look rather than about overloads. (1) **The redirect never checked the TARGET's arity** — it decided on argument 1's type alone, so `base(oneArg)` could be rewritten into a call to a two-parameter `base_str`, surplus parameter bound to whatever occupied the register, reported as a WARNING with the binary emitted anyway. (2) **`PARSE_RETURN`'s tail-call fast path never consulted the overload tables at all** — it emits epilogue+jmp itself — so `var r = println(myStr)` routed to `println_str` while `return println(myStr)` silently called the cstr base. **The same call, spelled two ways, ran two different functions.** RECOGNITION: when a transformation lives in ONE call-parsing function, enumerate every path that emits a call without going through it — cyrius has at least three (normal `PARSE_FNCALL`, the `PARSE_RETURN` tail path, inline replay), and this is the THIRD time the tail path was found missing something the normal path had (v6.3.36 plain-struct params, v6.4.53 value-form SIMD params, now overload dispatch). The fix is the same each time and it is NOT to duplicate the logic: set `tc_has_addr = 1` to divert onto the normal path. **THE MEASUREMENT THAT MATTERS**: the arity fix changes **0 of 253** .tcyr while the tail divert changes **81** — attribute a differential per-fix instead of reporting one total, because '0 files changed' is not reassurance that the fix is inert, it is proof the CORPUS HAD ZERO COVERAGE OF THE SHAPE, which is exactly why the defect survived five minors. Identical lesson to v6.4.80 (251/251 byte-identical across a real wrong-answer fix). Also: escalating the arity check from warning to error was verified safe by measuring FIRST — the whole repo (253 .tcyr, all 7 main forks, programs/) emits zero such warnings — and it deliberately does NOT set `_panic`, since an arity mismatch is not a token-stream desync and all bad sites should report in one compile. See CHANGELOG [6.5.1]."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "qemu_user_reports_a_false_thread_va_leak_because_proc_self_statm_measures_the_host_process"
description = "`tests/tcyr/crossos/thread_detach.tcyr` (v6.5.8) asserts that 100 detached threads grow virtual address space by **exactly 0** — before the fix they grew it by **210,124,800 B**, i.e. 100 x 2,101,248 (`THREAD_STACK_SIZE + THREAD_GUARD_SIZE`) to the byte, with `vm.max_map_count` (65530) as the ceiling, so a thread-per-request server degraded to `thread_create` returning 0 after ~65K spawns. **DO NOT TRUST `qemu-aarch64` FOR THIS TEST — IT REPORTS A FALSE LEAK.** Under qemu-user the file fails with ~1,665 residual pages per detached thread, which is not even the 513-page stack: qemu maps its OWN per-thread emulator state into the same address space, and `/proc/self/statm` measures the HOST process, not the guest. On real pi the identical binary reports a delta of exactly 0 and the file passes 115/0. Same class as the v6.4.42 aarch64 epoll bug that only real pi could surface — **if a VA/mapping assertion goes red under qemu, run it on pi before believing it.** THREE MORE MEASUREMENT TRAPS baked into that file, each of which cost a false reading while it was being written. (1) **VA, not RSS.** Thread stacks are touch-on-demand, so the leak showed as ~200 resident pages while VA grew 51,300 — an RSS-based test misses it entirely. (2) **Poll until it settles; never sample at a fixed instant.** A spin loop is not a synchronisation primitive: on a slow machine the children simply have not reached their `munmap` yet, which reads as a leak. The property that actually distinguishes fixed from broken is that VA comes BACK DOWN AT ALL — with the leak it never does, so a bounded retry loop (400 x 5 ms) separates 'slow' from 'broken' where a fixed sleep cannot. (3) **Let the warm-up threads finish BEFORE sampling the baseline**, or they unmap during the measurement and you get a NEGATIVE growth of -513 pages — exactly one thread — the measurement racing the thing it measures. **AND ASSERT ZERO, NOT 'LESS'**: the detached child unmaps its stack AND the TLS block carved from the top of that same mapping and allocates no handle at all, so 0 is the correct number and a threshold like '< 20 MiB' would still pass with the TLS or the handle leaking. See CHANGELOG [6.4.42], [6.5.8]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["qemu-user", "false-positive", "proc-self-statm", "host-vs-guest", "thread_create_detached", "VA-not-RSS", "poll-until-settled", "baseline-race", "assert-zero-not-less", "real-pi-hardware", "vr01_thread_detach", "v6.5.8"]

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_does_not_inline_so_a_one_load_accessor_on_a_dispatch_path_is_a_real_call_frame"
description = "⟳ **CORRECTED v6.6.1 — the flat claim below is no longer true.** `#inline` became a
REAL directive at v6.5.63 (token 163) and the inline-replay machinery (`SFINL`,
`_INLINE_OK`) is live. What remains true is the DEFAULT: `_INLINE_OK = 0`, so ordinary
calls are still not inlined unless a fn is marked, and the accessor cost below is still
what you pay on an unmarked dispatch path. Read the measurement, not the absolute.

**cyrius does not inline ordinary calls BY DEFAULT, so every unmarked accessor is a real frame — and an accessor whose entire body is one `load64` costs strictly more than the load it wraps.** `alloc_via` (`lib/alloc.cyr`), the hot path of the whole `_a` allocator convention, cost **15.1 ns** while the `arena_alloc` bump underneath it is about eight instructions: **5.1 ns of that was two accessor CALLS** — `allocator_alloc_fn(a)` to perform one `load64(a)` and `allocator_state(a)` to perform one `load64(a + 32)` — against ~6.0 ns for the bump itself. A third level was pure trampoline: `_arena_alloc`, whose entire body was `return arena_alloc(state, size)`. v6.5.10 reads the vtable inline in the four dispatch helpers (`alloc_via` / `realloc_via` / `free_via` / `reset_via`) and registers `&arena_alloc` / `&arena_reset` directly: **15-16 -> 12 ns** from the inlining, **-> 11 ns** with the trampolines gone. The accessor inlining is the large half. **THIS IS SAFE ONLY BECAUSE `allocator_new` FIXES THE LAYOUT** — reading `a + 0` / `a + 32` directly is exactly as safe as calling the accessors, which remain as public API; only the hot path stopped calling them. **DO NOT GENERALISE THE INLINING INTO A DELETION.** `_bump_alloc` and `_bump_reset` look like the same trampoline shape and are NOT: they ADAPT ARITY (`alloc(size)` and `alloc_reset()` take fewer arguments than the vtable signature passes), so they genuinely translate rather than pass through, and removing them corrupts the call. The distinction — a frame that only forwards vs. a frame that changes the argument list — is the thing to check before deleting any wrapper, and `tests/alloc_via_no_plumbing.sh` asserts both directions. **WHY A FEW NANOSECONDS EARNED A RELEASE SLOT:** `_a` threading multiplies the cost by the size of the object graph, which is the entire point of the `_a` families. agnosai counted **112 allocations on one route — 32 % of the request** — with a counting allocator wrapped around the arena's own vtable, and that was AFTER a hoisting pass; `bayan_json_v_obj_new_a` alone is three `alloc_via` calls. See CHANGELOG [6.5.10]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["no-inlining", "alloc_via", "allocator-vtable", "dispatch-plumbing", "trampoline", "_bump_alloc-adapts-arity", "arity-adapter-vs-passthrough", "_a-convention", "allocator_new-fixes-layout", "alloc_via_no_plumbing", "v6.5.10"]

[[entries]]
name = "negating_at_i64_min_is_a_no_op_so_every_while_n_gt_0_decimal_formatter_printed_a_bare_minus"
description = "`n = 0 - n` is a **NO-OP at `i64::MIN`** — two's-complement absolute value overflows back to itself — so a formatter written as 'emit the sign, negate, then `while (n > 0)` peel digits' emits the sign byte **and nothing else**. `str_from_int(i64::MIN)` was the one-character string `\"-\"`, and bayan then serialised `{\"n\":-}`, which is not JSON, **with no error raised at any layer**. Both the `n == 0` special case and the digit loop are skipped, because the negated value is still negative. Fixed at v6.5.8 across **12 sites, not the 7 the roadmap named**: four in `lib/fmt.cyr`, plus `string.cyr`, `log.cyr`, sakshi (fixed UPSTREAM at 2.4.8 and re-vendored, per 'fix the source repo, not the fold'), **two inside the compiler itself that the roadmap never listed** — `PRNUM` (`src/common/util.cyr:849`) and `_emit_decimal` (`src/main.cyr:888`) — and three shipped demos. **THE PART WORTH CARRYING IS THAT THIS EXACT BUG HAD ALREADY BEEN FIXED IN THE FUNCTION DIRECTLY ABOVE.** `fmt_hex` sits immediately below `fmt_int` in the same file and was corrected for the same `> 0` vs `!= 0` reason at v6.4.69 — **the decimal siblings were never brought along**, which is the same one-branch-at-a-time shape as the Darwin `AT_*` flags and the macOS `Stat` enum. When you fix a loop-termination predicate in one formatter, grep the whole tree for the predicate, not the function name. PREREQUISITE VERIFIED BEFORE RELYING ON IT: truncation toward zero for negative `/` and `%` was confirmed on x86_64, aarch64 **and** cx, since the digit-peel for a still-negative `n` depends on it. See CHANGELOG [6.4.69], [6.5.8]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["i64-min", "twos-complement", "0-minus-n-is-a-noop", "fmt_int", "fmt_hex", "PRNUM", "_emit_decimal", "gt-0-vs-ne-0", "sakshi-2.4.8", "fix-upstream-then-fold", "truncation-toward-zero", "grep-the-predicate-not-the-fn", "v6.4.69", "v6.5.8"]

[[entries]]
name = "an_allocator_returning_0_is_not_an_error_channel_when_the_type_system_has_no_option"
description = "**The arena primitives all handled exhaustion CORRECTLY and that was not the bug.** `arena_alloc` returns 0, `str_from_a` returns 0, `vec_push_a` returns -1. The bug is what the 0 does NEXT: a `Str` of 0 is **indistinguishable from a valid `Str`**, there is no option type and no error channel through the `_a` families, so it flows on and the first thing that dereferences it faults. 'Arena too small' was observable as a **SIGSEGV several layers away, in a JSON walker, with no indication which allocator ran out.** **THE REGRESSION DIRECTION IS WHAT MADE IT SERIOUS:** before threading a route onto an arena, an oversized response was a *leak* on the no-free global bump; after threading — a change whose entire purpose is to stop leaking — the same response was a *crash*. **Adopting the feature correctly made the failure worse and quieter**, and a 5,500-assertion suite missed it because allocation tests are small-fixture by nature: they assert `bytes == 0` over a handful of items and nothing about that shape ever probes the ceiling. v6.5.9 ships one **policy field** (`a + 24`) rather than three features: `ARENA_FULL_NULL` (0, today's behaviour and still the default, so no existing arena moves) / `ARENA_FULL_GROW` (1, chain another chunk — `arena_new_growable` / `arena_allocator_growable`) / `ARENA_FULL_SPILL` (2, serve overflow from the global allocator) / `ARENA_FULL_ABORT` (3, die loudly at the allocation instead of faulting three layers away). FOUR THINGS TO KNOW BEFORE TOUCHING IT. (1) **GROW RETAINS ITS CHUNKS ACROSS `arena_reset`** — the bump allocator underneath has no `free()`, so releasing them is not expressible, and it is not what an arena wants anyway: reset rewinds to the first chunk and re-uses the chain, so a request loop converges on its high-water mark and then allocates nothing (verified: 500 allocations twice over produce identical total capacity). (2) `arena_used` cannot show the whole chain once it has grown — that is what `arena_capacity_total` is for. (3) `arena_free` previously cleared only base/ptr/end and **left the policy set**, so a 'freed' growable arena would take the grow path and **succeed**; it now clears the policy back to `ARENA_FULL_NULL`. (4) **MUTATION TESTING CHANGED THE CODE HERE, NOT JUST THE GATE**: the mutation 'grow ignores an oversize request' PASSED, because `arena_alloc` returned the post-grow pointer **without re-checking the bound** — a silent heap overflow, not a failed allocation — and the test could not see it either, asserting only `!= 0`. Both were fixed: a defensive bound re-check, and a first/last-byte write-and-read-back that only survives if the memory is really there. `lib/alloc.cyr` is inside cycc's include closure and on the seed path, so all of this carried the full fixpoint + seed-derive gate. See CHANGELOG [6.5.9]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["arena", "exhaustion-policy", "ARENA_FULL_NULL", "ARENA_FULL_GROW", "ARENA_FULL_SPILL", "ARENA_FULL_ABORT", "zero-is-not-an-error-channel", "no-option-type", "regression-direction", "grow-retains-chunks", "arena_capacity_total", "arena_free-left-policy-set", "mutation-changed-the-code", "seed-path", "v6.5.9"]

[[entries]]
name = "the_distlib_deps_sidecar_scanned_includes_not_the_declaration_and_a_wrong_sidecar_switches_off_cyriuss_own_consumer_check"
description = "`dist/<lib>.deps` says it lists 'stdlib leaves this fold requires in scope', but until v6.5.10 it was built **ONLY by scanning bundled module sources for literal `include \"lib/X.cyr\"` lines** — so it captured what a module INCLUDES and never what it REFERENCES through the manifest declaration. setu 0.8.2 emitted **8** leaves against a declared **12**; the four missing (`result`, `net`, `chrono`, `args`) are all referenced at top level in `src/client.cyr`. **THE CONSEQUENCE IS NOT A MISLEADING FILE — IT IS A DISABLED CHECK.** `cyrius deps` validates a consumer's `[deps] stdlib` against the dep's sidecar, correctly and loudly. The filer measured both directions: with a corrected 12-leaf sidecar an under-declaring consumer got a hard error naming `chrono` and `args`; with the raw 8-leaf one **the same consumer built `OK`, silently**, on agnos. A wrong sidecar switches OFF the machine check that exists to catch exactly that mistake. Fixed by making the sidecar the declared `[deps] stdlib` **UNIONED** with the include-scan, not either alone — the scan can legitimately catch a leaf the author forgot to declare, so the union can never under-report relative to either source. **THE CONSTRAINT THAT KEEPS THE FIX FROM BEING WRONG IN THE OTHER DIRECTION: base bundle only.** A `[lib.X]` PROFILE is a narrow subset of modules, so its true dep set is a SUBSET of the declaration; unioning the whole declaration into e.g. a `sha` profile would OVER-report and could fail a legitimately-narrow consumer against the very check this re-enables. Profiles keep the pruned inference, and `tests/distlib_deps_sidecar.sh` asserts that asymmetry explicitly. **THREE HYPOTHESES WERE MEASURED AND FALSIFIED BY THE FILER BEFORE FILING** — it is not the declaration, not an inline-comment parse bug, not a direct-symbol scan — and they are recorded in the gate so none of them gets re-investigated. See CHANGELOG [6.5.10]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["distlib", "deps-sidecar", "cyrius-deps-validation", "under-report", "union-declaration-with-scan", "base-bundle-only", "profiles-keep-pruned-inference", "over-report-hazard", "setu", "falsified-hypotheses-in-the-gate", "v6.5.10"]

[[entries]]
name = "the_tail_call_guard_was_syntactic_so_a_pointer_laundered_through_a_local_freed_the_frame_under_the_callee"
description = "`return f(args)` takes cycc's tail-call path: the epilogue tears the frame DOWN and JMPs, so the callee executes on released stack. v5.8.16 §8 already knew this and guarded it — **by scanning the call's argument list for a literal `&` token**. That scan is SYNTACTIC, so it only ever saw the direct spelling. `return f(&buf)` was guarded from v5.8.16; `var p = &buf; ... return f(p)` walked straight past it and read dead stack. **Same source semantics, opposite results, no diagnostic — a silent wrong answer, not a crash.** Fixed at v6.5.14 by replacing the syntactic test with a semantic one: `_fn_local_addr` is set at the SINGLE `ELOAD_LOCAL_ADDR` site in `parse_expr.cyr` wherever a fn materialises the address of one of its own locals, reset per fn in `PARSE_FN_DEF`, and read by the guard in `parse_fn.cyr`. One site covers every laundering path — through variables, arithmetic, structs, globals — because they all have to go through `&local` first. A fn that never takes a local's address keeps the flag at 0, so its codegen is byte-identical (cycc itself grew **16 bytes**). **THE ARITY IS WHY IT HID FOR YEARS AND IS THE PART WORTH CARRYING:** cycc already declines TCO above **6 arguments** (stack args are clobbered by the epilogue), so the defect is reachable ONLY at ≤6 args — the identical helper called with 7 args was fine. Downstream that does not look like a compiler bug, it looks like a cursed *buffer*. sigil hit exactly this: its 10-arg RSA verify wrappers localised cleanly at 3.12.3 while their 6-arg SIGN twins 'broke the KATs', so **three consecutive sigil releases (3.12.3, .4, .5) each shipped a source comment saying localisation regressed tests for reasons not understood** — and the shared `cbank()` lanes that comment justified were an **authentication bypass** (1 of 800 forged PSS signatures accepted under lane aliasing). **THE DIAGNOSTIC LESSON IS SHARPER THAN THE FIX.** 3.12.3 recorded that 'callee-clobbers-caller was ruled out'; that came from a probe window over the buffer's first 8 bytes, and the corruption was at **offset 223**. A probe that cannot see the defect is not evidence of absence — it froze a security fix for three releases. The measurement that finally settled it was two addresses printed side by side: a callee's local sat ABOVE every one of the caller's locals, which is only possible if the caller's frame is already gone. **This is the FIFTH thing found missing from this tail path** (v6.3.36 plain-struct params, v6.4.53 value-form SIMD params, v6.5.1 overload dispatch, v6.5.2 the literal-to-cstring error, now this) — when you touch `PARSE_FNCALL`, ask what the tail path does NOT do. Gate: `tests/tcyr/crossos/tailcall_frame_escape.tcyr`, in `crossos/` because the epilogue exists on every backend and the frame it frees is ABI-shaped; acceptance is **aliased ≡ direct**, not aliased ≡ a literal. See CHANGELOG [6.5.14], sigil [3.12.6]."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["tail-call", "TCO", "frame-escape", "_fn_local_addr", "v5.8.16-section-8", "syntactic-vs-semantic-guard", "ELOAD_LOCAL_ADDR", "6-arg-threshold", "silent-wrong-answer", "sigil-3.12.6", "rsa-pss", "authentication-bypass", "probe-window-too-narrow", "offset-223", "fifth-miss-on-the-tail-path", "crossos-gate", "v6.5.14"]

[[entries]]
name = "visibility_enforced_at_reference_not_insertion"
description = "A feature enforced only where a name is USED is not a boundary — private fns collided for five minors (v6.5.0-.37)"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE SHAPE, which is the reusable part: `private` was implemented as a CHECK AT
THE REFERENCE SITE layered on top of an UNCHANGED global symbol table. That
makes the feature look complete — the error message fires, the gate is green,
`nm` output shrinks — while the thing it claims to guarantee is absent.

What actually happened (v6.5.0 through v6.5.37): the fn table held exactly one
entry per NAME. A second definition reused that entry and overwrote its body,
so a private helper was silently replaced by any same-named fn in another file,
INCLUDING for its own file's internal calls. Declaring `private` on BOTH sides
changed nothing, because neither declaration touched INSERTION.

    a.cyr:  private fn _helper(s) { return 1; }
            public  fn a_entry(s) { return _helper(s); }
    b.cyr:  private fn _helper(c) { return 0; }
            public  fn b_entry(c) { return _helper(c); }

    a_entry(0) * 10 + b_entry(0)  ->  exit 0.   Correct is 10.

⭐ THE TELL WAS IN THE DOCS THE WHOLE TIME. The guide promised "private fns are
omitted from the exported symbol table". That is a statement about the SYMBOL
TABLE, and nothing in the implementation touched the symbol table. When a
feature's documented guarantee names a data structure the code never modifies,
the feature is a check, not a boundary.

⚠ AND THE WARNING ACTIVELY MISLED. The collision surfaced as
`duplicate fn ... last definition wins`, which reads as benign shadowing and
usually is. It is not benign when the two definitions disagree about arity,
struct layout, or RETURN POLARITY — and nothing in the warning says which case
you are in. The filed instance (vyakarana + sankoch, both defining a private
`_stream_grow`) disagreed about all three; a wrong binding there reports every
successful buffer grow as a FAILURE, inside a tokenizer, on file content.

⚠ ZERO ADOPTION HID IT. 0 of 292 first-party files across seven repos declared
`private`, so nothing in the ecosystem exercised the boundary for five minors.
A feature with no users generates no bug reports — that is not evidence it
works. The first consumer to actually try it filed this within days.

⚠ THE GATE COULD NOT HAVE CAUGHT IT, for a mechanical reason worth copying:
the v6.5.37 gate builds every probe by CONCATENATING sources into one file. The
defect keys on ORIGIN FILE, so every concatenated probe gets a single file id
and exercises nothing. A multi-file defect needs a multi-file gate, on disk,
with real `include`s.

FIX: split same-named definitions into separate symbols at insertion when a
private one is involved and the origin files differ; resolve preferring the
caller's own private definition, then the first non-private one, then fall
through so the precise privacy error still fires rather than "undefined
function". See tests/gates/frontend/private_scoped_at_insertion.sh (v6.5.38).

[[entries]]
name = "one_shot_process_registration_closes_the_raw_thread_path"
description = "bsdthread_register is one-shot per process and libSystem always takes it first, so arm64-macOS cannot install its own thread trampoline (v6.5.44)"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE SHAPE, which is the reusable part: a kernel facility can be PRESENT, correctly
ROUTED, and still UNUSABLE, because something else in the process already consumed
a one-shot resource. Routing proves reachability, not availability.

v6.5.43 routed all five Darwin concurrency syscalls on both Mach-O backends and
proved them reaching the kernel on real ecb and ach. Every one of those routes is
correct. And `bsdthread_create` is STILL unusable on arm64-macOS, because:

  * `bsdthread_register` installs the process-wide thread-start trampoline and is
    ONE-SHOT. A second call returns EINVAL.
  * cyrius's arm64 Mach-O writer emits a dyld/LC_MAIN PIE that links
    /usr/lib/libSystem.B.dylib, so libpthread's initializer registers first.
  * Measured from a REAL cyrius-compiled binary on ecb: exit 22 (EINVAL).
  * A libSystem-free process is not an escape either — on macOS 26 a static
    LC_UNIXTHREAD arm64 binary is SIGKILLed by AppleSystemPolicy.

⭐ THE ASYMMETRY IS THE INTERESTING PART, and it is a property of the two WRITERS,
not of the OS. The x86 Mach-O writer emits a static MH_NOUNDEFS/LC_UNIXTHREAD binary
with no dyld and no libSystem — so on Intel-Mac registration DOES work and the raw
bsdthread path is the right one, while on Apple Silicon it is closed. One "#ifdef
CYRIUS_TARGET_MACOS" spans two targets whose thread primitives cannot be the same.

WHAT WORKS INSTEAD, and it needed no new machinery at all: __got[5] had been bound
to `_pthread_create` since v5.6.6 and never used. No GOT growth, no new symbol, no
bind-opcode arithmetic. And no trampoline: a Darwin start routine is
`void *(*)(void *)`, which is exactly the shape of a cyrius `fn body(arg): i64` —
arg 1 in x0, result in x0, stock AAPCS64 prologue.

⚠ THE TEMPTING WRONG TURN was to hand-build a pthread_t and use libpthread's
already-registered trampoline. It WORKS — it was prototyped end to end — but it
depends on sig@0x00 (validated against a per-process ptr_munge cookie), fun@0x90,
arg@0x98, tsd@0xE0 and a PAC modifier, none of which is stable ABI. Working is not
the bar; surviving an OS update is.

[[entries]]
name = "check_which_manifest_key_CONSUMERS_write_not_the_one_your_repo_writes"
description = "v6.5.51: v6.5.49 taught `cyrius build` to fall back to `[build] src` / `output` in ./cyrius.cyml when the positional arguments are omitted. Measured across ~/Repos at v6.5.51: **120 of 125 cyrius.cyml files declare `entry`, and only 5 declare `src`** — one of the five being the cyrius repo itself, which is where the implementer read the key from. So the convenience shipped INERT for 96 % of the ecosystem: a pre-fix CLI handed an `entry=` manifest prints the usage text and exits 1, which reads as 'this feature does not exist'. ⚠ AND ITS GATE PASSED, because the gate's fixture manifest was written with the same key the implementation read. That is the transferable failure: a gate authored alongside an implementation tends to encode the implementation's assumptions, so it confirms the code does what the code does rather than what users need. LESSON: when a feature reads a config/manifest key, count the key across real consumer repos BEFORE implementing (`awk '/^\[build\]/{f=1;next}/^\[/{f=0}f&&/^(src|entry)/' ~/Repos/*/cyrius.cyml`), and make the gate's fixture use the key CONSUMERS write. Fixed by accepting both with `entry` first, plus a gate axis for each and one for the both-keys precedence."
category = "cyrius"

[[entries]]
name = "copy_semantics_beats_lifetime_analysis_for_unboxing_a_sum_type"
description = "v6.5.55: the way to stop boxing a payload enum was NOT to find a safe place to put the box — it was to stop having one. Two relocations were disproven; the register pair was never in that set."
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.5.15 tried to stop cyrius boxing `Ok(v)`/`Err(e)` by RELOCATING the 16-byte box to a
per-call-site anonymous global. It passed every gate and shipped a compiler that "reported a
failed file open as SUCCESS in a retaining loop", and was reverted. The project's standing note
then read: caller-frame is too short-lived (`?` in an unannotated helper returns the box out of
that helper's frame), a global is too shared, therefore "no relocation-based design can work;
the survivors are escape analysis or a scope-tied arena with reclaim."

⭐ THAT ENUMERATION SILENTLY DROPPED THE OPTION THAT WORKS, and the dropped option needed no
new machinery: return `(tag, payload)` in the multi-return register pair the language already
had. **Copy semantics kills both failure modes at once** — each binding is its own copy of two
registers, so two values built at one call site cannot alias, and there is no storage for
anything to dangle into. Verified before designing anything: a probe of
`fn mk2(x): (i64, i64)` called at ONE call site in a loop returned distinct values with
`alloc_used()` delta of exactly 0.

The general trap: **when every candidate is a variant of "where do we put the object", the
question to ask is whether there needs to be an object.** Lifetime analysis is the expensive
answer to a question that value semantics deletes.

⚠ WHAT IT COST TO NOT SEE THIS: the standing note was framed as a closed set of survivors, so
each revisit re-derived escape analysis and re-concluded it was too big. A closed-set claim in
a design doc deserves the same premise-check as a perf number — ask what it EXCLUDED and why.

⚠ SECOND LESSON, ON SCOPE. The default was NOT flipped, and that was the right call rather than
timidity: the boxed layout is the documented API. The guide spells `Ok(42)` as "16-byte heap
alloc — tag at +0, payload at +8", `?` desugars to `load64(rax + 8)`, and ~300 ecosystem sites
read those offsets by hand. A register pair read as a pointer is a plausible-looking address,
so a default flip breaks them SILENTLY. Opt-in via a keyword that was ALREADY RESERVED (`stack`,
token 111, from `stack var buf[N]`) costs no lexer change and cannot collide with an
identifier — adding a `value` keyword would have broken any program using `value` as a name.

[[entries]]
name = "a_property_held_by_an_unrelated_invariant_breaks_silently_when_that_invariant_moves"
description = "v6.5.56 P0: cyrius's identifier dedup was a PREFIX compare that was exact only because bucketing put one length per chain. Changing the bucketing removed the invariant and nobody added the check it had been standing in for."
category = "cyrius"
---
cyrius's `LEXID` deduplicates identifiers through a hash chain. The compare walked `klen` bytes of
a stored pool entry and never checked that the stored entry ENDED there — a PREFIX compare. It was
nonetheless correct for years, because `bucket = klen` put exactly one length on each chain, so
two entries in a chain could not differ in length. **The exactness was a side effect of the
bucketing, not a property of the compare.**

v6.5.50 replaced `bucket = klen` with a content hash — a real fix for an O(N²) chain walk — and
thereby removed the invariant, without adding the terminator check the invariant had been standing
in for. Result, live for six releases: a shorter identifier that is a prefix of a longer one in the
same bucket took the longer one's pool offset and **the two names became one symbol**.
`var ah = 7; var ahxaa = 99;` then read `ah` as **99**. Exit 0, no diagnostic. 127 repos under
`~/Repos` carried at least one colliding pair.

⭐ THE COMMENT ASSERTED THE PROPERTY AND THAT IS PART OF THE FAILURE. Three lines above the compare
sat "dedup remains an exact byte-compare … verified: self-host fixpoint + differential corpus". It
was false. A reader checking whether the hash change was safe found a sentence saying it was.

⛔ THE SELF-HOST FIXPOINT IS STRUCTURALLY BLIND TO THIS ENTIRE CLASS, and this was PROVEN, not
argued: a compiler with the fix deliberately removed **still reproduces itself byte-identically**
while miscompiling consumer code. The reason is that the defect is a function of the INPUT's
identifier set — cycc's own source has 0 colliding prefix pairs out of 54,089 — and the aliasing is
deterministic, so it reproduces identically across generations. **Byte-identity after a hashing or
interning change proves nothing about consumer output.** Any change that alters which inputs collide
needs a gate on a known-colliding input, not a fixpoint.

⚠ THE RECOGNITION RULE, worth more than the fix: when you remove or replace an invariant, ask what
was RELYING on it — not just what it was for. `bucket = klen` was introduced for lookup speed; it
was silently also providing compare exactness. Grep for code that reads the same state and check
each one against the NEW invariant. A helper whose correctness argument mentions a property you are
about to change is the code to re-derive first.

**Instance 2 — v6.6.6 bite 16, `_cur_fn_pslots`, and the same comment failure.** The variable
answers "is this frame slot a parameter", and `parse.cyr`'s comment said it was "saved/restored
around the async constructor exactly like `_cur_fn_ix`". It was not: `_async_emit_constructor`
saves ELEVEN pieces of enclosing state and this was not one of them. It was correct only because
`_PARSE_FN_DEF_IMPL`'s teardown zeroes it FOUR LINES before it emits the constructor — a LINE-ORDER
dependency, documented as a save/restore. Move either line and the constructor inherits the body's
parameter span, at which point every slot in it reads as an address-passed struct parameter, with
no test that would notice. Same shape as the prefix compare: a property held by something else,
with a comment asserting the property directly. Fixed by making the comment true (`sv_psl`), not by
rewriting the comment — when the cheap option is "describe the accident accurately", prefer
"remove the accident".

[[entries]]
name = "regalloc_param_exclusion_is_by_count_not_slot"
description = "The picker excluded params by param COUNT while its frame-slot map is by SLOT — a scalar after a by-value vector param got promoted to a register and read as 0"
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.5.62. The register-allocator picker (an inline block inside `_PARSE_FN_DEF_IMPL`,
`src/frontend/parse_fn.cyr`) skipped parameters when choosing promotion candidates with:

    var iv_li = pc;        # pc = PARAMETER COUNT

but the frame-slot indices it walks are SLOTS. A by-value vector parameter occupies
`nslots` slots — `nslots-1` anonymous fillers (name sentinel -1) and then its NAMED slot,
which carries the SLTYPE. So for `fn f(v: f64v4, s)`:

    slot 0,1,2 = fillers for v      slot 3 = v (named)      slot 4 = s
    pc = 2  ->  the loop started at slot 2  ->  s was a "local"

`s` became a promotion candidate and was held in a callee-saved register, while the
prologue's homing store still wrote the incoming argument to its frame slot. The callee
read the register. `s` came back as 0 — silently, exit 0, from ordinary user code.

THE FIX is to capture the real boundary where the parameter list closes, because GFLC
there counts exactly the param slots:

    var ra_pslots = GFLC(S);     # right after the `)` of the param list
    ...
    var iv_li = ra_pslots;       # instead of `pc`

For a function with no wide params this equals `pc`, so nothing moves: 406 corpus inputs
byte-identical, 0 codegen diffs.

HOW TO RECOGNISE IT. Four things must coincide, and dropping any one makes it vanish —
which is why it survived so long and why a narrow probe can "disprove" it:
  1. a by-value VECTOR param (both widths reach it; f64v4 reliably, f64v2 whenever the
     picker actually spends a register on the slot),
  2. another param AFTER it (scalar-first is fine),
  3. a body the inline-replay path REFUSES, so a real call happens. A FLAT body inlines
     and dodges it entirely,
  4. the picker enabled.

⭐ THE ONE-COMMAND OWNERSHIP TEST. `CYRIUS_REGALLOC_PICKER_CAP=0` recompiles with the
picker disabled — ⚠ it is a COMPILE-time knob: `CYRIUS_REGALLOC_PICKER_CAP=0 cycc < x.cyr > x`,
never `CYRIUS_REGALLOC_PICKER_CAP=0 ./x` (the 6.6.2 gate did the latter and its "off" axes
silently re-tested the ON binary until 6.6.5). If the wrong answer becomes right, the picker owns the bug; if it stays
wrong, the picker is exonerated and you are looking in the wrong subsystem. Run this
BEFORE reading any allocator code. In the same session it exonerated the picker for a
different SIMD defect (v6.5.61's value-form binding bug, which was in `PARSE_VAR`) and
then convicted it for this one — two bugs with overlapping symptoms in the same wrappers.

⚠ AND THE TRAP THAT ALMOST BURIED IT: the same release flattened `lib/simd.cyr`'s
wrappers for performance, which makes `f64v4_scale` INLINE — and an inlined callee never
takes the broken path. The perf change would have made the symptom disappear while the
compiler stayed broken for every consumer writing their own non-flat wrapper. When a
library change makes a wrong answer go away, find out whether it fixed the bug or only
stopped reaching it: rebuild the PRE-change library against the FIXED compiler and check
it passes on its own.

[[entries]]
name = "simd_value_form_cost_is_store_to_load_forwards"
description = "The cost of a chained SIMD value-form expression is 16-byte store-to-load forwards, NOT instruction count — removing 22 of 28 instructions per link measured 1.00x"
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.5.64. A value-form vector chain like

    var t1: f32v4 = f32v4_add(a, b);
    var t2: f32v4 = f32v4_add(t1, b);

expands, per link, to 28 real instructions and ~15 memory ops for exactly ONE `addps`. The
obvious conclusion — "delete the scaffolding" — is WRONG, and it was measured wrong before
the code was written.

A hand-written asm replica that removes exactly that scaffolding (3 lea + 3 pointer spills +
3 reloads + a bounded loop that runs one iteration) and changes nothing else runs **33.99 ms
against the current 33.96 ms**. Twenty-two instructions per link removed, zero time saved.

WHY. The critical path is made of 16-byte STORE-TO-LOAD FORWARDS, and the pointer machinery
is not on it. A calibrated cost model over five variants predicts within 3.6%:

    time(2M iters) = 4.111ms x (critical-path store->load forwards) + 1.370ms x (packed ops)

Per link there are two forwards: the inline replay's copy of a param into the callee slot
(reloaded by the kernel), and the kernel's result store (reloaded by `return r`). What
actually bought 1.48x was making the store and its reload ADJACENT so the v6.5.53 SLASE
peephole could collapse them — the peephole matches at exactly +8, and before this change the
pair sat ~0x31 bytes apart, which is why SLASE's own comment recorded it deleting nothing.

MEASURE THE REGIME, NOT JUST THE SHAPE. Two of my own baselines were mis-framed the same way:
a benchmark chain that is DEAD at the end of each iteration lets consecutive iterations overlap
in the out-of-order window, so the dependency stall reads as ~20%. Carry the chain across
iterations (feed the accumulator back) and the same code shows ~2.3x. If you are measuring
dependency latency, the value must be live into the next iteration; otherwise you are measuring
throughput and will under-read the prize by a factor of two.

Staged prize for a vector register class, all measured:
  direct form alone .................. 1.00x
  + elided return reload ............. 1.59x   (v6.5.64)
  + elide replay param copy .......... ~2.07x latency / 5.20x throughput
  + full register residency .......... 8.26x latency / 9.48x throughput
And spilling the result once per iteration is FREE (4.114 vs 4.111 ms) — a register class only
has to kill the per-link RELOAD, not all stores.


[[entries]]
name = "aggregate_copy_by_type_not_by_occupancy_clobbers_neighbours"
description = "v6.5.57 P0, live SEVENTEEN releases: `X = Y;` between two struct-POINTER locals copied STRUCTSZ/8 slots and wrote over neighbouring locals. The discriminator is the FILLER SENTINEL (name -1), not the type — and the fixpoint was structurally blind because cycc's own source never uses the shape"
category = "cyrius"
---
`X = Y;` where both are locals of a struct type copied **the number of slots the
TYPE implies** rather than the number the VARIABLES occupy. For two inline
aggregates that is right. For two **pointers** to a struct it is catastrophic: the
locals are one slot each, so the copy walked `STRUCTSZ/8` slots off the end and
overwrote whatever sat beneath them — in yukti, a loop-bound PARAMETER, which then
walked its buffer off into the process stack.

**The discriminator is not the type.** An inline aggregate OWNS the slots beneath
its named one, and the frame marks them with a **filler sentinel** — name `-1`. A
pointer-to-struct local does not. So the guard is: before copying `nslots`, walk
both source and destination and refuse unless every slot beneath the named one is
a filler:

    while (ac_k < nslots) {
        if (L64(names + (dst_li - ac_k) * 8) != 0 - 1) { return 0; }
        if (L64(names + (src_li - ac_k) * 8) != 0 - 1) { return 0; }
        ac_k = ac_k + 1;
    }

⛔ **WHY IT SURVIVED SEVENTEEN RELEASES, and this is the part to carry.** The
byte-identical self-host fixpoint could not see it: **cycc's own source never uses
the shape.** Guarded and unguarded compilers emit `src/main.cyr` byte-identically
(1,247,608 B) — so the fixpoint agreeing proves the compiler does not *exercise*
the path, never that the path is correct. Treat "the fixpoint is green" as coverage
ONLY for constructs cycc itself contains.

**How it was actually found**, after instrumentation kept making it vanish: bisect
the TRACKED compiler binaries. Every tag carries `build/cycc`, so

    git show 6.5.56:build/cycc > /tmp/cc_56    # PASS
    git show 6.5.57:build/cycc > /tmp/cc_57    # FAIL

is an instant, download-free bisect over the real artifact. One fix then cleared
failures in TWO downstream repos that had been written off as pre-existing.


[[entries]]
name = "value_form_return_payload_alone_reads_as_success"
description = "v6.6.0 migration fail-open: `return res;` on an Err path returns the PAYLOAD by itself, so Err(77) reads downstream as tag=77 / is_err=0 — an error that reads as SUCCESS. 19 real sites in yukti alone"
category = "cyrius"
---
When `Result`/`Option`/`Either` became the value form (v6.6.0), a payload variant
returns `(tag, payload)` in a register pair. The natural-looking propagation

    var t, v = inner();
    if (is_err_result(t) == 1) { return v; }     # ⛔ WRONG

returns the **payload alone**. The caller then binds `t` from rax — which now holds
the payload — so `Err(77)` reads as **tag=77, is_err=0**: the error is silently
reclassified as a success carrying 77. Correct:

    if (is_err_result(t) == 1) { return (t, v); }

**This is the migration rule's own fail-open**, and it is worse than the bug the
flip fixed: an allocation leak is a resource problem, an error that reads as success
is a correctness one. Measured: **19** real sites in yukti (→2.3.10) and 3 in vani.

A mixed-return function — some paths returning a pair, some a single value — now
raises a compiler **warning**. It is deliberately not fatal (the dual-mode shape is
legitimate and tested both ways in vani), so the warning must actually be read.
⚠ Do NOT "fix" a warning by wrapping the single-value path in a constructor without
checking the tests: that was tried on vani and broke 3 of them, because those
returns were raw-by-design.


[[entries]]
name = "compaction_moves_code_geometry_computed_before_it_does_not"
description = "v6.6.1: CYRIUS_DCE=1 shrinks the code buffer, but PE geometry is computed at the TOP of FIXUP from the pre-elimination length — so the import payload was written at the new cursor while the section header named the old offset. The loader mapped the IAT from zero padding and the first call *IAT(%rip) faulted 0xC0000005 before main"
category = "cyrius"
---
v6.5.72 made `CYRIUS_DCE=1` physically REMOVE dead bodies (`wp_compact`) and shrink
`GCP(S)`. But `_pe_layout(S)` runs at the **top** of FIXUP off the **pre-elimination**
length, so every PE geometry field — section sizes, RVAs, `PointerToRawData`, and the
IAT RVA the ftype=4 fixups patch against — described a layout the emitted code no
longer had. `EMITPE_EXEC` then writes `.idata` at the **post-compaction cursor**
(`o = o + cp`) while the header still names the pre-compaction offset.

Measured on the filed repro: the import payload moved from file offset `0x220F6` to
`0x2AF6` — **128,512 B earlier**, against the 128,555 B DCE reported — so the loader
mapped the Import Address Table from what had become zero padding, every import
resolved to 0, and the first `call *IAT(%rip)` faulted `0xC0000005` **before main**.

⚠ **Two independent breakages, and fixing one is not enough.** The displacement was
ALSO wrong on its own terms: patched against the old geometry, then the instruction
moved, so it resolved to RVA `0x39DD` for an IAT the header puts at `0x23000`. A
compaction pass that repairs geometry but not rip-relative displacements — or the
reverse — produces a binary that looks fine and faults later.

**Two tells worth recognising.** (1) The flag reported "N bytes eliminated" while the
FILE SIZE did not change — output written earlier inside a fixed-size buffer looks
identical from the outside. (2) The crash read a function pointer whose bytes were
ASCII (`0x7465470000000057` = `W`…`Get`): an IAT slot holding hint/name-table content
means the loader never resolved it.

**Current state**: PE and x86 Mach-O DECLINE compaction, exactly as `_pie_mode`
already did and for the same stated reason — a rip-relative reference shape the pass
does not repair. They keep NOP-fill (correct, size unchanged). ELF still eliminates
for real (123,048 → 16,552 B, −86.5%).

⛔ **The filing said "PE / `--win` target only" and that was wrong.**
`main_x86_macho.cyr` includes the same `x86/fixup.cyr`, so Intel-Mac Mach-O took the
identical path and SIGSEGV'd on real hardware — absent from the report only because
the reporter does not build that platform. **A filing's target list is a report about
what the reporter builds, not about the bug.** Reproduce on every host that shares
the code path before scoping the fix.


[[entries]]
name = "guard_must_precede_a_range_reduction_not_sit_inside_it"
description = "v6.6.1: f64_exp/f64_exp2 returned NaN for ±inf in BOTH implementations. The reduction computes `inf - inf`, AND the exponent it assembles comes from f64_to(inf) saturating to i64::MIN — two ways to produce garbage, both upstream of the actual exponential"
category = "cyrius"
---
`f64_exp(+inf)` and `f64_exp(-inf)` returned **NaN**. The range reduction subtracts a
multiple of the argument *from the argument*, so for an infinite input it computes
`inf - inf` = NaN. A guard placed *inside* the reduction cannot fix this, because
there is a **second** failure on the same path: the exponent is assembled from
`f64_to(inf)`, which **saturates to `i64::MIN`**, so `(n + 1023) << 52` is not an
exponent either. The check has to come **first**, before any arithmetic touches the
argument.

    if ((x & 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) == 0x7FF0000000000000) {
        if (((x >> 63) & 1) == 1) { return 0; }    # exp(-inf) = +0
        return 0x7FF0000000000000;                  # exp(+inf) = +inf
    }

⚠ **There are TWO implementations and a consumer hits whichever its target selects**:
the `_f64_exp_polyfill` / `_f64_exp2_polyfill` pair in `lib/math.cyr`, and the emitted
x87 `f2xm1`/`fscale`/`frndint` sequence in `src/backend/x86/emit.cyr`. Fixing only the
polyfill leaves every x86 build wrong. The backend guard is emitted ahead of the x87
sequence and its two exits are patched after the x87 pop.

**NaN deliberately gets NO guard.** It already propagates correctly through both
paths, and adding one would only create a branch that has to stay correct forever.
Not every special value needs handling — check which ones actually break.

[[entries]]
name = "inline_replay_is_a_prerequisite_for_simd_residency_and_it_binds_params_by_slot"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from four v6.5.52-.53 entries. A SIMD value chain cannot keep a vector in a register across a call because SysV has NO callee-saved XMM — so inlining is a PREREQUISITE, not an optional optimisation. Admitting SIMD params to the inline-replay path then exposed four defects at once, rooted in the replay binding params to ADJACENT 8-BYTE slots that a 16-byte value overruns"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **This entry replaces four** (`no_callee_saved_XMM_on_SysV…`,
`inlining_is_a_PREREQUISITE…`, `inline_replay_binds_params_to_8_BYTE_slots…`,
`an_inline_replay_that_binds_params_BY_NAME…`). One arc, one lookup.

**The ABI fact that sets the order.** SysV has **no callee-saved XMM registers**.
So a vector CANNOT survive a call, and a value-form SIMD chain that emits a `call`
per link must spill and reload around every one of them. The roadmap and state.md
both pinned the blocker as "the allocator's picker only recognises REX.W mov to/from
`[rbp+disp32]`, so no xmm/ymm local is a candidate" — a TRUE statement about the
code and the WRONG lever. Removing the calls (inlining) has to come first;
teaching the picker about xmm is downstream of it. **Inlining is a prerequisite,
not a nicety.**

**What admitting SIMD params to inline-replay then exposed — four defects, and the
instructive part is that each one ALONE still looked nearly working**, so every
single fix "almost" succeeded:

- The replay binds a callee's params to **temporary local slots** —
  `var p0_slot = saved_flc; var p1_slot = saved_flc + 1;` — i.e. ADJACENT **8-byte**
  slots. A 16-byte SIMD param written into one **overruns into the next**, so two
  vector params collide.
- `SFINL` packed the two param NAME offsets from local slots 0 and 1, which is only
  correct when the callee's first two locals ARE its params.
- The replay's param COPY was read back two instructions later — a 16-byte
  store-to-load FORWARD that the hardware cannot elide; the emitter must read the
  CALLER's local instead (v6.5.65, 1.27×).
- Dead inline-param copies left an `xmm0` clobber that stopped SLASE collapsing the
  inter-link pair; harvesting them is what finally gave register residency across
  chain links (v6.5.66, 2.50–2.60× cumulative).

⚠ **Measured, not assumed**: deleting the value-form scaffolding alone — 28
instructions for ONE `addps` — moved 33.99 → 33.96 ms, i.e. **ZERO**. The cost was
never the instruction count; it was the store-to-load forwards and the calls.


[[entries]]
name = "a_gate_only_proves_something_if_you_prove_the_mutation_reached_it"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from three v6.5.50-.54 entries. A mutation applied with a sed pattern that never matched left the tree UNCHANGED and the gate printed PASS; some gates need a source revert rather than a compiler swap; and a comfortable hypothesis about a flaky gate was wrong. Mutation-prove the gate AND check the mutation was actually applied"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **This entry replaces three** (`a_mutation_that_silently_fails_to_apply…`,
`some_gates_need_TWO_different_mutations…`, `test_the_comfortable_hypothesis…`).

- ⛔ **A mutation that does not apply reports PASS and proves nothing.** A gate was
  mutation-proven with `sed -i 's/                        if (IR_ENABLED(S) != 0) {/…/'`
  — **twenty-four** spaces of indentation against the file's **twenty**. The pattern
  never matched, the tree was never modified, the gate ran against the ORIGINAL code
  and printed PASS. Always assert the file CHANGED (`cmp`, or a grep count that moves)
  before believing a mutation result.
- **Some gates need a source revert, not a compiler swap.** Swapping `build/cycc` for
  a pre-fix compiler is not always the mutation: a gate axis that BUILDS from the
  working-tree source (`cycc < src/main_aarch64.cyr`) and runs the result is testing
  the SOURCE, so only a source revert perturbs it. Pick the mutation that reaches the
  axis you are proving.
- **Test the comfortable hypothesis before fixing it.** A gate went red once under
  load and never reproduced; the filing's leading explanation (a sampler counting a
  SIGKILLed-but-unreaped process) was checked and REFUTED. Fixing the comfortable
  story would have left the real cause live and closed the investigation.

⭐ Companion traps already known and still true: a gate fixture whose decoy sits
AFTER the real key is never reached (the scan stops at the first match), and a gate
whose fixture shares the implementation's assumption agrees with it forever — the
v6.5.49 manifest-key case, where the feature shipped inert to 96 % of consumers and
its gate stayed green.


[[entries]]
name = "install_sh_copies_are_where_the_toolchain_breaks_use_temp_plus_rename"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from two entries. install.sh has broken the toolchain twice by HOW it copies: `cp -L` over a symlink pair aborts with 'same file' under set -e before the symlink-update block (v5.10.37), and `cp` writes THROUGH the directory entry so installing over the running binary is ETXTBSY (v6.6.1). Both leave a half-written tree; rename() replaces the entry instead"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **This entry replaces two** (`install_sh_cp_L_same_file_silent_fail`,
`installing_over_a_running_binary_needs_rename_not_cp`). Same file, same class:
**how install.sh copies is the thing that breaks the toolchain.**

**Failure 1 — `cp -L` on a symlink pair (v5.10.37).** The `--refresh-only` lib-copy
loop uses `cp -L lib/*.cyr`. When `lib/<dep>.cyr` is a `cyrius deps`-resolved symlink
AND the snapshot dir holds a parallel symlink to the SAME real file, `cp -L` exits
with "same file". `set -e` makes that fatal, so install.sh dies BEFORE the
symlink-update block — `~/.cyrius/bin` and `~/.cyrius/current` stay pointing at the
PREVIOUS version. The user-visible symptom is a stale `cycc --version` in the shell
prompt, which reads as prompt/config corruption rather than a failed install.
⚠ Still live: `grep -c 'cp -L' scripts/install.sh` → 2.

**Failure 2 — `cp` over a running binary (v6.6.1).** `cp` writes THROUGH the existing
directory entry, so overwriting a currently-executing binary is `ETXTBSY`. `cyriusly`
re-execs the pinned toolchain, so it is **always running during its own install** —
structural, not a race. The install then aborted part-way, which is why deleting
`cyriusly` and re-running the installer did not recover it: the tree was already
half-written.

**The fix for both shapes**: write a temp file beside the target and `rename` it over.
`rename` replaces the DIRECTORY ENTRY; a running process keeps its open inode and
finishes, and the next exec picks up the new file. A per-file failure warns and
continues rather than aborting mid-tree (`_install_file`, `scripts/install.sh:90`).

⚠ **v6.5.3 had already fixed the ETXTBSY shape in ONE of THREE copy paths.** The
tarball path — the one `cyriusly install` actually uses — survived and was reported
from a clean machine three minors later. When a fix is "make this write atomic",
sweep every writer of that kind in the same pass.
⚠ The installer is frozen into each release's immutable tag, so a broken one cannot
be hot-fixed for an already-published version.

[[entries]]
name = "a_cap_is_declared_in_one_place_and_believed_in_five_sweep_every_consumer"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from seven entries. Every cap in cycc is declared once and then RE-STATED as a guard, a comment, a scratch size, a clear-loop bound and a doc line. Raising the declaration without sweeping the restatements is the single most repeated defect class in this compiler — it has produced a silent miscompile, an unsound DCE, and a SIGSEGV, across v3.3.17 through v6.5.50"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces seven entries** (`stale_fixed_cap_drift`, `any_fn_indexed_side_table_must_reach_32768…`,
`a_declared_region_size_becomes_the_bound_someone_guards_at`, `a_cap_raise_must_sweep_every_consumer_of_the_cap`,
`scratch_sized_for_an_old_cap_fails_SILENTLY…`, `stdlib_alloc_grow_step_must_cover_request`,
`identifier_pool_raises_in_place_only_below_the_0x100000_ceiling`). They were the same
lesson seven times, which is itself the evidence that it keeps happening.

**THE CLASS.** A cap lives in more places than the one you edit: the allocation, the guard
that compares against it, a `while (i < N)` clear-loop, a scratch region sized from it, the
heap-map comment, and the docs. Raise the declaration alone and the rest keep enforcing the
old number — and because the extra capacity exists, nothing faults at the boundary. The
failure is always *silent and late*.

**The instances, because the variety is the point:**

- **A clear-loop left behind.** `live[]` was widened 4096 → 16384 bytes when the fn ceiling
  went 32768 → 131072, but the clear loop still zeroed 4096. Three quarters of the DCE
  reachability bitmap started as stack garbage: sometimes "live" (a missed elimination,
  harmless), sometimes "dead" (the fn is NOP-filled and the program faults). ⚠ **This was
  the SECOND time** — v6.4.75 had fixed the same loop for the previous ceiling.
- **Side tables that did not follow the table.** Six fn-indexed side tables stayed at 8192
  while the fn table grew, so index 8192 aliased a neighbour.
- **A declared size became someone's guard.** Three capture loops guarded at 4095 because the
  heap map said `[4096]` — but two live things sat inside that span. The MAP was the source
  of the bound, and the map was fiction.
- **Scratch sized for an old cap.** `EMITELF_OBJ` carved sub-regions from a size that had
  moved; it failed silently long before it crashed.
- **A grow step that does not cover the request.** A fixed `0x100000` delta cannot satisfy an
  allocation larger than the step — grow logic must cover the REQUEST, not a constant.
- **A ceiling that is physical, not arbitrary.** The identifier pool at `S+0x60000` can only
  be raised in place while its END stays under `0x100000`; above that the compiler hangs
  rather than erroring.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** When you change a cap, `grep` the NUMBER — the old one and the new one —
across `src/`, the heap map comment block, and the gates, and fix every hit in the same
commit. A cap raise is never a one-line change. And prefer deriving a bound from the
declaration (`sizeof`-style) over restating the literal, so there is nothing to drift.


[[entries]]
name = "there_are_seven_forks_and_two_symmetric_backends_a_change_lands_in_one_of_them"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from seven entries. `ls src/main*.cyr` is SEVEN per-target forks, and several subsystems exist as symmetric pairs (x86/aarch64 emit, the two Mach-O backends). A change applied to the fork or backend you were testing compiles everywhere and is WRONG everywhere else — the compiler cannot tell you, because the other fork's stub returns 0 rather than failing"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces seven entries** (`new_pe_emitter_needs_aarch64_strict_stub`,
`new_pe_reroute_fn_needs_return0_stubs_in_aarch64_and_cx…`,
`cross_compile_arms_in_main_cyr_are_a_separate_fork…`, `a_diagnostic_that_exists_on_one_backend_only`,
`apply_a_consolidation_to_both_symmetric_places_or_neither`,
`one_ifdef_two_backends_different_pointer_contracts`,
`heap_region_shared_by_all_forks_must_relocate_to_arena_top…`).

**THE CLASS.** cyrius has **seven** per-target entry forks (`main`, `main_win`,
`main_x86_macho`, `main_aarch64{,_macho,_native}`, `main_cx`) plus symmetric emitter pairs.
There is no shared dispatch helper, so a change is applied by hand N times — and N-1 is the
normal failure. Two mechanisms make it invisible:

1. **Return-0 stubs.** A new `E*_PE` reroute needs a matching `fn E*_PE(S) { return 0; }` in
   the aarch64 and cx backends purely for symbol resolution. Those stubs make a missing
   implementation COMPILE and emit NOTHING — clean build, clean run, wrong answer, no
   diagnostic. Only `cass`'s `cycc_cx` caught one such miss.
2. **The cross-compile arm is a different code path from the native driver**, and a `.tcyr`
   run on the host can never reach it. Compiling FOR a target and running ON it are two
   different programs.

**Instances worth keeping:**
- A diagnostic (the unrouted-syscall warning) existed on arm64 Mach-O and **not** on x86
  Mach-O for ELEVEN minors — an asymmetry nothing was checking.
- One `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_MACOS` covers **two** Mach-O backends whose reroutes disagree
  about whether an argument is a pointer. One ifdef, two contracts.
- A consolidation (the query-mode route table) was applied to one of two symmetric backends;
  the other kept 279 hand-written cases. **Apply it to both places or neither** — a half-done
  consolidation is worse than none, because the next reader believes it is done.
- A heap region SHARED by all forks must relocate to arena top: the forks' arenas diverge, so
  a fixed offset that is free in one is occupied in another.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** After any emitter, predefine, or driver change: `ls src/main*.cyr` and
account for every one. The gate is byte-identical self-host **on all four hosts** plus
seed-derive — a green x86 build proves nothing about the other six.


[[entries]]
name = "a_syscall_number_on_darwin_or_aarch64_is_not_the_number_you_wrote"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from six entries. Between ESYSXLAT, EMACHO_SYSXLAT and per-arch stdlib peers, a raw syscall(N) reaches the kernel as something else — and the SOURCE number is arch-specific too, so the same wrapper name means different numbers in different files. Renumbering alone is never enough: VALUES and FLAGS diverge as well"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces six entries** (`macho_syscall_number_dual_definition_61_vs_217`,
`macho_socketpair_dual_number_199_vs_53`, `macho_syscall_xlat_source_numbers_arch_specific`,
`darwin_at_star_flags_diverge_from_linux…`, `the_macos_x86_stat_enum_was_a_mix_of_two_structs…`,
`esysxlat_is_a_linear_cmp_chain…`).

**FOUR INDEPENDENT WAYS THIS BITES**, and fixing one does not fix the others:

1. **The SOURCE number is arch-specific.** x86-macho feeds `syscalls_macos.cyr` (BSD-numbered:
   getpid 39) while aarch64-macho feeds `syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr` (getpid 172). So a fix
   reasoning only about the x86 `EMACHO_SYSXLAT` table misses the aarch64-macho stdlib
   ENTIRELY. Same shape for `SYS_GETDENTS64` (217 vs 61) and `socketpair` (199 vs 53).
2. **ESYSXLAT is a linear cmp-chain, so ORDER is semantics.** An entry must sit BELOW every
   entry that compares the number it PRODUCES, or its output is re-caught and re-translated.
   The aarch64-Linux arm must stay LAST in its chain.
3. **VALUES diverge, not just numbers.** `AT_FDCWD` is **-2** on Darwin, not Linux's -100 — and
   a pure renumbering translation passes a Linux-valued FLAG straight through, where it is
   simply invalid: EINVAL, forever, silently. ESYSXLAT renumbers NUMBERS, never VALUES.
4. **A struct definition can describe two structs at once.** `syscalls_macos.cyr`'s `Stat`
   enum mixed fields from two layouts because only the field that prompted a fix was
   corrected. Fix the WHOLE struct or none of it.

⚠ **A private alias band exists for the unwinnable case** (v6.5.7): when BOTH the native
number and the x86 number are already owned, use `1000 + native` — numbers ≥1000 are
cyrius-private and no OS will mint them.

5. **An UNNAMED number is invisible to the diagnostic built for it** (added v6.6.5). The
   ELF-aarch64 raw-literal warning derives its table from `SYS_*` names declared in BOTH Linux
   peers, so the case it exists for — a number nobody ever named — produces NOTHING. Measured
   under `qemu-aarch64 -strace` at 6.6.4: raw 77 (x86 `ftruncate`) ran **tee**, raw 46
   (`sendmsg`) ran **ftruncate**, raw 319 was ENOSYS, and the build was clean and silent.
   Naming a call is therefore not cosmetic; it is what makes the diagnostic able to see it.
6. **THE GENERATED TABLE IS THE DIAGNOSTIC; ESYSXLAT IS HAND-WRITTEN AND NEVER REGENERATED**
   (added v6.6.5, and the filing that prompted it had this backwards).
   `src/common/syscall_xlat.cyr` is produced by `programs/gen_syscall_xlat.cyr` from the two
   peers and is consulted only to WORD A WARNING. The renumbering is the `cmp x8 / b.ne /
   movz x8` chain in `src/backend/aarch64/emit.cyr`. **Naming a syscall in both peers
   renumbers nothing.** Rows are added by hand, in an order the chain makes load-bearing.
7. **A NEW NATIVE DECLARATION CAN SILENTLY DELETE A DIAGNOSTIC ROW** (added v6.6.5). The
   generator drops any x86 number that is also a valid native aarch64 number, because a
   literal there is plausibly correct. So declaring `SYS_CAPSET = 91` on the aarch64 peer
   removes the row that warns about raw x86 `fchmod`(91) — quietly, with every gate green.
   `tests/gates/platform/syscall_peer_kernel_agreement.sh` PINS that ambiguous set for
   exactly this reason.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** A wrapper that COMPILES on five targets is not a wrapper that RUNS. Put a
file in `tests/tcyr/crossos/` and run it on real ecb/ach/cass/pi — that is what turned up
seven wrapper defects at v6.5.7, five of which were half-fixes that stopped at the first
symptom.

⭐ **AND THE CHECKS THEMSELVES SHARED A SOURCE** (v6.6.5). Four gates guarded this machinery
and every pair of them was circular: the generated table re-derives FROM the peers, the
routed-literal allowlist derives FROM the emitter, the shadow gate compares the two peers TO
EACH OTHER, and the row-order gate checks the chain against ITSELF. A check that shares a
defect with the thing it checks reads GREEN. The fix was an OUTSIDE fact — committed kernel
UAPI tables under `tests/data/syscalls/`, the way `tests/data/ucd` is committed — against
which every declared number must MEAN the call its name claims. It found a live disagreement
on its first run.


[[entries]]
name = "a_green_gate_can_be_measuring_nothing_and_the_ways_it_happens_are_enumerable"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from four entries into the gate-proof lesson. A gate can pass BECAUSE of the bug it guards; a corpus can be unable to distinguish the thing it asserts from its absence; a test environment can make the code under test unreachable; and a path that never executes during self-host is untested no matter how green the fixpoint is"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces four entries** (`a_gate_can_pass_because_of_the_bug_it_guards…`,
`a_corpus_that_cannot_tell_real_threads_from_inline_calls`,
`an_empty_test_environment_makes_environment_code_unreachable`,
`phase0_growable_grow_path_self_host_invisible`). Companion to
`a_gate_only_proves_something_if_you_prove_the_mutation_reached_it`.

**FOUR DISTINCT MECHANISMS. Each produced a green gate over a real defect:**

1. **The gate depended on the defect.** Remove the bug and the gate fails — so it was
   asserting the broken behaviour. Also in this family: byte-pattern gates made vacuous by
   ASCII collisions, where the pattern matches something unrelated.
2. **The corpus could not tell the assertion from its absence.** Six concurrency tests passed
   on macOS for four minors while thread bodies ran **serially** — every assertion was
   honestly true, because a serial fallback satisfies every property the tests checked. The
   corpus could not distinguish a real thread from an inline call.
3. **The environment made the code unreachable.** The suite ran every test with `envp={NULL}`
   for reproducibility, so nothing could exercise `getenv` at all — the tests were green
   about a code path they never entered.
4. **Self-host does not exercise it.** The growable pressure tables never grow during cycc's
   own compile, so the grow path had zero coverage from the fixpoint. Force it with a
   low-cap build. ⚠ Generalise this: **the byte-identical fixpoint is coverage ONLY for
   constructs cycc's own source contains** — that is exactly why the v6.5.57 aggregate-copy
   P0 shipped for seventeen releases.
5. **The knob was set on the wrong process (6.6.5).** The 6.6.2 SIMD gate's "picker OFF" axes
   ran `CYRIUS_REGALLOC_PICKER_CAP=0 ./probe` — but that variable is read by **cycc at compile
   time**, never by the program. Both axes re-ran the picker-ON binary: against 6.6.1 they
   reported 139 (the ON crash) where a real picker-off build returns 10, the silent mode the
   issue file said was "now pinned". Any compile-time knob (`CYRIUS_*` read by cycc) goes on the
   COMPILER invocation. The repair adds the self-check the original lacked: the ON and OFF
   builds must differ in bytes, or the gate fails.
6. **The gate said SKIP and exited 0 (6.6.11).** A gate that could not run its check (a missing
   tool, host, fixture, /proc, wine) printed `SKIP: …` and exited 0 — the shape
   methodology.cyml's `ci_drift_detection_silent_skip_is_false_green` had prescribed as the
   "better" one. But both runners graded the EXIT CODE: the check driver's `_gate` mapped 0 to
   PASS and check.sh's `_chk_gate` knew only PASS / TIMEOUT / FAIL, so every such gate scored
   PASS and the SKIP line was prose nobody read; `CYRIUS_CHECK_NO_SKIP`, CI's strict switch,
   reached only the driver's own rows. A runner that reduces a gate to a number cannot see a
   word. Fix: exit **77** means "could not run" and both runners score it as a SKIP (a FAIL
   under `CYRIUS_CHECK_NO_SKIP=1`); a census axis
   (`tests/gates/toolchain/check_driver_skip_is_not_pass.sh` axis 11) fails any script under
   tests/gates that prints SKIP and then exits 0, so the old shape cannot come back one gate at
   a time.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** For any new gate ask: what would make this fail? If you cannot state the
mutation, the gate is decorative. Then apply it and confirm the tree actually changed.


[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_atomics_need_8_byte_alignment_and_are_not_a_full_barrier"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from two entries. On aarch64 ldxr/stxr MANDATORILY SIGBUS on a non-8-aligned address, and atomic_cas is NOT the full barrier the stdlib comment claimed — true on x86 (lock cmpxchg), false on aarch64"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces two entries** (`aarch64_atomic_on_global_needs_8_aligned_var_base`,
`aarch64_atomic_cas_is_not_a_full_barrier`). One subsystem, two traps, both aarch64-only.

1. **ALIGNMENT IS ARCHITECTURAL, NOT ADVISORY.** `ldxr`/`stxr` — what `atomic_cas` and
   `atomic_fetch_add` compile to — fault with **SIGBUS** on a non-8-aligned address. On x86
   the same code merely runs slower, so an unaligned global is invisible until it reaches ARM.
   Any global an atomic touches needs an 8-aligned base.
2. **THE BARRIER CLAIM WAS FALSE ON ONE ARCH.** `lib/atomic.cyr` documented `atomic_cas` as
   "a full barrier on both arches". TRUE on x86, where `lock cmpxchg` is a full fence. FALSE
   on aarch64, where the LL/SC pair orders only itself. Code relying on the documented
   guarantee is racy on ARM and correct on x86 — the worst possible split, because the
   development host is the one where it works.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** A stdlib comment asserting a MEMORY-MODEL property is a claim about two
architectures and must be verified on both. Run it on real `pi`; qemu-user will not surface
either of these reliably.


[[entries]]
name = "the_callptr_spill_slot_is_a_real_local_and_can_alias_one"
description = "MERGED v6.6.1 from two entries. Compiler-emitted indirect calls spill the callee address to a frame slot; that slot must survive the Win64 shadow space AND must not alias an &-taken local — it is a real frame slot competing with the ones the user declared"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces two entries** (`callptr_indirect_call_frame_slot_spill_survives_win64_shadow`,
`callptr_callee_spill_aliases_addr_taken_local`). Same slot, two ways it goes wrong.

`callptr` lowers to `IR_CALL_INDIRECT` — x86 `call [rbp-disp]`, aarch64 `ldr x6; blr x6` —
which requires spilling the callee address into a **frame slot**. That slot is not
bookkeeping; it is a real local, and it participates in every rule real locals do:

1. **It must survive the Win64 shadow space.** The callee may write the 32-byte shadow area;
   a spill slot placed inside it is destroyed by a conforming callee.
2. **It must not ALIAS an `&`-taken local.** When a function passes `&x` and also makes an
   indirect call, the spill slot can land on `x`'s storage — the callee writes through the
   pointer, or the spill overwrites the value, depending on order. Neither faults.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** Any compiler-allocated frame slot must be reserved through the same
allocator the user's locals use, never carved from "space that looks free". The same class
produced the v6.5.61 regalloc bug, where params were excluded by COUNT while the frame map is
by SLOT.

[[entries]]
name = "identifier_identity_is_fragile_in_this_lexer_five_ways"
description = "CLASS — 5 recurrences merged v6.6.1. Reserved words are a CLASS of 102 tokens, not a list; the set cannot be grepped (two keyword paths, >8-char names matched by u64+load8 tails); two token types are DOUBLE-ASSIGNED so a type cannot recover its spelling; and a struct field name that shadows a global silently reads the global. Every count ever written down for this set has gone stale"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces five entries.** They are one subject: **the lexer's notion of what a name IS,
and the several ways it goes wrong.**

**1 — Reserved words are a CLASS, not a short list.** `TOKNAME_BUILTIN` (`src/common/util.cyr`)
is the source of truth: **76** builtin/intrinsic names, disjoint from **26** statement
keywords in `IS_KEYWORD_TOK` — **102** reserved tokens. Any of them as an identifier is
refused. `fn` cannot be a parameter name; `mod` cannot be a var or param.

**2 — The set CANNOT be grepped, and every written count has rotted.** Two reasons a regex
sweep under-counts: the lexer has TWO keyword paths (`LEXKW_EXT` which returns a token, and
the inline `ADDTOK` chain), and a name longer than 8 characters cannot fit one u64 immediate
so it is matched by a u64 compare on the first 8 bytes PLUS per-byte `load8` tails — never a
single decodable literal (`f64_floor`, `f64v_fmadd`, the whole `f32v8_*` family: 16 names
invisible to a hex grep).
⛔ **Measured at v6.6.1: THREE live counts for one set** — 51 (a `util.cyr` comment), 67
(README + CLAUDE.md), **76** (the actual arm count). Derive it, never quote it:
`sed -n '/^fn TOKNAME_BUILTIN/,/^}/p' src/common/util.cyr | grep -cE 'if \(typ == [0-9]+\)'`.

**3 — Two token types are DOUBLE-ASSIGNED.** Token 79 is BOTH `object` and `f64_sqrt`;
token 111 is BOTH `stack` and `callptr`. Both compile correctly — the grammar disambiguates
by POSITION — but anything mapping a type back to a name has lost the information. Before
v6.4.77 `var f64_sqrt = 1;` reported `reserved keyword 'object'`, naming the WRONG keyword.
`TOKNAME` now returns both spellings, which is a workaround, not a fix.
⭐ **FILED v6.6.1**: `docs/development/issues/2026-09-08-lexer-token-types-79-and-111-double-assigned.md`.

**4 — A field name that shadows a global reads the GLOBAL.** `bc.exit_code` silently read a
global rather than the struct field when the field name matched one (v6.4.24, P1). Field
access and identifier resolution are not as separate as they look.

**5 — The diagnostic must NAME the collision.** Since v6.4.77 the parser names the specific
reserved word you hit; before that, 67 tokens reported a generic `got unknown`, which cost
one consumer a whole 312-assertion suite findable only by bisection.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** Read the table; never extend a list in prose. When a name is rejected and
you do not believe it, compile `var <name> = 1;` and read the diagnostic — the round-trip
through the compiler is the only sound enumeration method.


[[entries]]
name = "any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes_must_know_every_site_in_the_stream"
description = "CLASS — 4 recurrences merged v6.6.1, a 5th at 6.6.5 (a byte classifier whose stages disagreed). The code buffer is not pure instructions: inline switch tables, hand-emitted jumps outside any registry, and fn-boundary offsets all live in or beside it. A scanner that assumes 'instructions only' desynchronises; a mover that repairs only the tables it knows about corrupts the rest — and a length decoder that is ONE BYTE SHORT is worse than one that admits it does not know"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces four entries.** One subject: **the emitted byte stream contains more than
instructions, and every pass over it inherits that.**

**1 — Inline switch tables live IN the code stream.** A table-regime `switch` puts its jump
table among the instructions. Any unaligned byte scanner over a fn range — a peephole, a
compactor, a decoder — will walk into it and read table data as opcodes. A v6.5.20 P1 silent
miscompile came from exactly this, latent for multiple minors; a v6.5.53 adversarial review
of a NEW peephole found the OLD one had the same hole.

**2 — Some sites are in NO registry.** The entry trampoline's `E9` is hand-emitted (`EB(S,
0xE9)` + four raw bytes, patched later with `EPATCH`) rather than going through `EJMP0`, so
neither the per-fn `jump_src_tbl` nor the fixup table knows it exists. It never mattered while
compaction was per-function — its source and target both sit outside any fn body — and became
a SIGSEGV the moment a pass moved bytes program-wide.

**3 — The enumeration people reach for is incomplete.** "The tables the per-fn compactor
repairs" omits fn START offsets (`_fnt_offsets`), fn END offsets (`_fnt_codeend`, recorded
after the per-fn pass so it is already correct), and the trampoline. Widening a pass's scope
means re-deriving the list of stored positions from scratch, not inheriting it.

**4 — A WRONG length is worse than an UNKNOWN one.** A length decoder returning 0 fails safe:
the caller declines. One returning a length ONE BYTE SHORT desynchronises the walk and every
subsequent decode is garbage — and the resulting compiler is the SAME SIZE as the correct one,
so size comparison cannot detect it. `DECODE_LEN` had no case for `0x99` (CQO, before EVERY
integer division) and a `0F`+imm8 class that was short by one.

**5 — A pass that CLASSIFIES bytes must disqualify every form it cannot rewrite (6.6.5).** The
register picker is three stages over the same bytes: a use scan (counts `48 8B 85` / `48 89 85`,
the rax moves), an unsafe scan (disqualifies a slot), and a patch pass (rewrites exact 0x85). The
unsafe scan's trigger read the whole ModRM (`& 0xC7 == 0x85`, any reg) but its EXEMPTION read only
the prefix and opcode — so `48 8B 95`, `mov rdx,[rbp+disp32]`, was filed as a "plain mov the patch
pass will rewrite", and the patch pass never touched it. Every SIMD batch kernel reads its
operands in exactly that form (`_EMIT_LOAD_RDX_FROM_LOCAL`, 55 sites). A slot with two rax refs
was promoted, its store became a register move, and the kernel read a slot nothing wrote — silent
wrong code / SIGSEGV through all 33 batch intrinsics on every x86 picker target. It needed a way
for two uses to share a slot (a nested inline replay frees a param slot the next intrinsic
reuses) and shipped in 16 tagged releases (6.5.63 → 6.6.4) because cycc's own source has no intrinsic, so the fixpoint was
blind. The v6.5.64 comment on that very scan CLAIMED `48 8B 95` was already caught. FIX: one
predicate (`_ra_plain_slot_mov`) that all three stages call — a slot is promotable only if every
`[rbp+disp32]` reference to it is a form the rewriter handles. The byte scan is also disp32-only,
so an rbp-disp8 or SIB operand read would be invisible to all three stages; that dependency is now
a census gate (0 in 180,445 corpus functions) rather than an assumption.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** Before widening any pass over emitted bytes, enumerate (a) every table
embedded in the stream, (b) every stored code position, (c) every site emitted outside the
normal helper. Then mutation-prove that the pass fails when one is omitted. For a pass with
several stages (count / disqualify / rewrite), give them ONE predicate for "a form I handle" —
"disqualify anything not handled" is the only safe default — and check it with an oracle that
does not share the predicate (`objdump` decoding both a pass-on and a pass-off build, as
`simd_intrinsic_operand_slots.sh` axis C does).


[[entries]]
name = "a_stated_cause_or_a_carried_number_is_not_evidence_seven_times"
description = "CLASS — 7 recurrences (4 merged at the first v6.6.1 pass, 3 more on the second). A cause that was confidently NAMED was wrong about cycc's compile time twice, about a regalloc guard, about what a cap was hiding, and about an IR cost that was off by 20x. A carried binary SIZE is not identity. Instrument or count the thing itself"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces seven entries.** The recurrence count IS the lesson: this is the most repeated
*methodological* failure in the project.

**Wrong causes, confidently named:**
- cycc's compile-time superlinearity was blamed on `_fnt_grow` (costs 4–9 ms across an entire
  120,000-fn compile, not the 1,145 ms attributed to one call), on a rehash that DOUBLES and
  is therefore amortized-linear by construction, and on a DCE walk that is OFF by default and
  never ran in any measurement. The real causes: a hash keyed on **LENGTH ALONE** (20,168 ms →
  1,005 ms once the key includes content) and an open-addressed table grown only when **FULL**.
- **A cost jump at a doubling boundary is not evidence the doubling is the cost.** 60,000 fns
  737 ms → 70,000 fns 2,078 ms was read as "one grow costs 1,145 ms". The boundary was where
  LOAD FACTOR crossed.
- The roadmap asserted for months that the cross-BB regalloc defect was "live and localised to
  ONE line". That line was a **deliberate v5.6.22 correctness guard**, and deleting it alone
  fails **69 of 282**. The real blocker was an unnamed second one: a LIFETIME cap of 5.
- **Removing a cap unmasks whatever the cap was hiding.** Lifting the allocator's lifetime cap
  was correct and immediately exposed an O(n²) safety scan the cap had been concealing. A
  correct change can still make things worse; measure after.

**Carried numbers, never re-taken:**
- `CYRIUS_IR=3` was planned around "+3.6 % compile time (670 → 694 ms)" for several releases.
  Measured live: **13,967 ms** vs 672 — off by **20×**. The SIZE half of the same note was
  accurate, which is exactly why the time half survived: a half-right number reads as checked.
- **An identical binary SIZE is not byte identity.** ELF padding absorbs small additions — a
  one-line xlat entry landed while 872,790 bytes moved and the size did not change. Use `cmp`,
  never `ls -l`.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** Instrument or COUNT the thing before designing around a stated mechanism,
and re-take any number you are about to plan against. Several of these survived multiple
releases and two prior investigations apiece.


[[entries]]
name = "agnos_is_an_x86_host_that_is_not_linux_and_the_predefines_do_not_say_so"
description = "CLASS — 3 recurrences merged v6.6.1. `CYRIUS_ARCH_X86` is predefined on EVERY x86-hosted build including the AGNOS ring-3 target, so `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86` alone silently selects Linux syscall numbers on agnos. Add that agnos has no /proc and a stub written against an older kernel ABI rots silently, and agnos needs a precedence check in three separate places"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces three entries.** One subject: **agnos looks like x86 Linux to every mechanism
that asks a coarse question.**

**1 — The arch predefine does not distinguish it.** `main.cyr` unconditionally does
`PP_PREDEFINE(S, "CYRIUS_ARCH_X86")` — the AGNOS ring-3 target included, because agnos IS
x86. So a stdlib file selecting syscall numbers on `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86` /
`CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` **alone** silently takes the Linux branch and emits Linux syscall
numbers, which compile clean and corrupt at runtime. **The fix shape is a PRECEDENCE branch:**
test `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS` FIRST, then fall through to arch. Surfaced v6.2.33 by
sakshi's logger. Still live by design — this is the pattern, not a bug to fix.

**2 — A `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS return 0;` stub rots silently.** Written against an
EARLIER kernel ABI, it keeps returning 0 after the kernel grows the real call. The stub's
comment naming a kernel version is a TIMESTAMP, not an invariant. ⚠ The v6.6.1 clock case is
the sharp version: the v6.2.6 "fix" bound `clock_now_ns()` to `uptime_ms` (#40, `timer_ticks`)
which is **FROZEN with interrupts cleared** — replacing a stub that returned 0 with one that
returns a plausible CONSTANT, strictly harder to spot. Now `uptime_us` (#95, rdtsc).

**3 — agnos has no /proc**, so `getenv()` walks the exec-stack envp from `_agnos_init_rsp`.
Anything that reaches for `/proc/self/*` is a Linux assumption wearing portable clothes.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** Ask "is this check TARGET-specific or ARCH-specific?" — on agnos the two
disagree, and only the target question is safe. Gate with
`tests/gates/platform/folds_agnos_parity.sh` (every folded stdlib that builds for Linux must
also build for agnos) and verify against the live kernel ABI doc, not the stub's comment.


[[entries]]
name = "a_guard_in_the_loop_implies_a_second_pass_somebody_has_to_write"
description = "CLASS — 3 recurrences merged v6.6.1. A correct in-loop guard that DEFERS work to a later pass is only half an algorithm, and the other half gets forgotten: closures dropped every parameter past the 6th, gvar inits were emitted after a pass that never returns, and #derive bodies landed after the user's tail. The guard makes the code look finished"
category = "cyrius"
---
⟳ **Replaces three entries.** One shape: **an in-loop guard that says "handle this later"
where the later never got written** — and because the guard itself is correct, review passes
over it.

**1 — Closures dropped every parameter past the 6th.** The in-loop guard correctly stopped at
the register-argument boundary and implied a deferred stack-param pass. That pass was never
written, so params 7+ were silently discarded. Fixed v6.5.46 by writing the second half.

**2 — Global-var inits were emitted after a pass that never returns.** In `kmode==1` the emit
order puts gvar-init AFTER `PARSE_PROG`, so top-level `var X = <literal>;` got its store
**code-emitted but never executed** — agnos's `XHCI_CMD_TIMEOUT_SPINS = 10000000` read as 0. A
timeout of zero does not crash; it looks like a hardware fault. Fixed with static-init tables
that bake the value into the image, removing the second pass entirely.

**3 — `#derive` must emit generated bodies BEFORE the user's tail.** The remedy has an
ordering requirement: flattening generated bodies onto the struct's closing brace keeps the
construct's expansion line-neutral, but they must precede whatever the user wrote after.

⚠ **`EMIT_GVAR_INITS` is a HAND-DUPLICATED parallel copy of `PARSE_STRUCT_INIT`** — the same
two-pass shape, maintained twice. A change to one that is not applied to the other is the
fork problem (see the seven-forks entry) at function scope.

⭐ **THE HABIT.** When you write a guard that defers, write the deferred pass in the SAME
commit or leave the guard failing loudly. "Correct as far as it goes" compiles, runs, and
produces wrong answers with no diagnostic.


[[entries]]
name = "a_compat_row_that_turns_fail_closed_into_fail_open_ships_with_its_decline"
description = "v6.6.4. A raw x86 syscall number in arch-neutral code is not 'unsupported' on aarch64 — it is a DIFFERENT syscall, and the raw-literal diagnostic cannot see that class by construction (a valid aarch64 number is silent by design). Four shipped: cbt's getppid(110) ran timer_settime and killed every `cyrius run/test` child on native aarch64 for 59 releases; hashseed's time(201) ran listen(0); sigil's uname(63) ran read; dynlib's arch_prctl(158) ran getgroups — which on REAL hardware returns the group COUNT (qemu-user EFAULTs it), so the `rc == 0` caller failed open exactly on group-less processes. Two habits: derive the allowlist from the emitter, and land a decline in the same commit as any ESYSXLAT row that would make an x86-only path start 'working'."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["esysxlat", "aarch64", "raw-syscall", "fail-open", "getgroups", "getppid", "qemu-user", "cross-os", "v6.6.4"]
---
**The class.** `syscall(<x86 literal>, …)` in `lib/` or `cbt/` reaches aarch64-Linux through
ESYSXLAT, which renumbers the x86 numbers it has rows for and passes the rest through
UNCHANGED. A passed-through number is not an error on aarch64 — it is whatever aarch64
assigned that number to. The v6.5.51 raw-literal diagnostic derives its table from the aarch64
PEER's declared `SYS_*` names, so a number the peer declares for a *different* syscall is silent
**by design** (`syscall_xlat_generated.sh` axis 5 pins that 63 = x86 `uname` / aarch64 `read`
stays quiet). The most dangerous class is therefore the one the diagnostic is built to ignore.

**What shipped that way, all found in one sweep (v6.6.4):**
- `cbt/build.cyr`: `syscall(110)` (`getppid`) after `PR_SET_PDEATHSIG` → aarch64 `timer_settime`
  → `-EINVAL` ≠ ppid → child exits 1 before `execve`. EVERY `cyrius run/test/tests/bench/fuzz`
  child on native aarch64, 6.5.19 → 6.6.3 (59 releases). The pi gate self-hosted the COMPILER each
  release and never built or ran the CLI; from 6.5.51 on, the CLI's own aarch64 build printed a
  warning for that line. Note the fix is the SYMBOL, not a row: each Linux peer declares its own
  `SYS_GETPPID` (110 / native 173) and ESYSXLAT carries no 110 row.
- `lib/hashseed.cyr`: `syscall(201, 0)` (`time`) → aarch64 `listen(0, …)` → `-ENOTSOCK` as the
  hash seed.
- `lib/sigil.cyr`: raw 63 (`uname`) → aarch64 `read`.
- `lib/dynlib.cyr`: `syscall(158, 0x1002, tls)` (`arch_prctl(ARCH_SET_FS)`) → aarch64
  `getgroups(4098, tls)`.

**⚠ The qemu trap.** The filing traced under `qemu-aarch64 -strace` and reported every wrong
call as failing closed. qemu-user's `getgroups` range-checks the whole `4098 × 4` destination
before the call and returns `-EFAULT`. The real kernel on pi writes the process's supplementary
GIDs into the TLS block and returns their COUNT — and the caller took only `rc == 0` as success,
so it failed closed BY ACCIDENT on a process with supplementary groups (pi: 2) and failed OPEN
on one with none (`setgroups(0)` daemons, minimal containers): a TLS block the CPU never
installed, handed back as installed. Two lessons in one row: a cross-OS finding traced under an
emulator is a hypothesis about the hardware — run it on the host before writing "fails closed"
into a severity — and "the wrong syscall returned an error" is not the same as "the caller
failed closed": read what the caller does with the return value.

**⚠ The fail-open a fix can create.** The obvious repair — add ESYSXLAT rows for `fstat` 5 and
`lstat` 6 — would have made `_fdlopen_mmap_elf`'s `fstat` start WORKING on aarch64, so an x86-only
ELF loader that had been failing by accident would proceed to `mmap` an aarch64 object and jump
into it. A compat row that turns an accidental fail-closed into fail-open **ships in the same
commit as the decline** (`#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64 return -1`), plus the belt the decline
implies (`e_machine != 62` refused on every arch). The rows alone would have been a regression
that every gate called green.

**The habit that catches the class.** Do not maintain an allowlist; DERIVE it. `cmp x8,#N` on
aarch64 is `0xF100001F | N<<10`, so every `EW(S, 0xF1……1F)` word in ESYSXLAT's ELF arm is a
routed source number. `tests/gates/platform/raw_syscall_literals_routed.sh` decodes them (44
rows) and scans every raw `syscall(<literal>, …)` in `lib/` + `cbt/` (325 sites) against the
set, tracking `#ifdef`/`#ifndef`/`#else` target guards so a literal under
`CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` is judged native. A new row is admitted automatically; a new raw literal
fails until it is routed, spelled `SYS_*`, or guarded. And the cross-OS leg now builds and RUNS
the CLI on pi — a wrapper that compiles on five targets is not a wrapper that runs.

[[entries]]
name = "nested_call_alignment_stack_machine"
description = "A stack-machine codegen is rsp-aligned only BETWEEN statements. cycc padded nothing for values pending inside an expression, so any call nested in an argument or operand entered its callee 8 bytes off — and on PE the base itself was inverted, so it was the statement-level calls that were wrong."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["abi", "alignment", "sysv", "win64", "codegen", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**The trap, in one sentence**: if your codegen evaluates
expressions on the machine stack, "the frame is 16-aligned" is a
statement about the gaps BETWEEN statements, and a call emitted
inside an expression is aligned only by luck.

cycc pushes one 8-byte value per pending operand (`push rax` in
EPUSHR/ESPILL). The only alignment invariant it had was the frame
rounding `fsz = (flc*8+15) & -16`. So:

```cyr
store64(pp, create_buffer(dev, desc));   # arg 2: 1 pending -> rsp 8 off
var buf = create_buffer(dev, desc);      # statement level  -> aligned
store64(pp, buf);
```

Both x86 ABIs require rsp ≡ 0 at the CALL. A C callee that spills
SSE with `movaps`/`movdqa` — what gcc emits for ordinary code —
takes a #GP. Found by mabda 4.1.3 as a SIGSEGV inside NVK's
`create_buffer`; whether it faults depends on the callee, so it
reads as intermittent and vendor-specific.

**Two properties that make the source text a bad guide:**

* The shift is INHERITED. A cyrius fn entered misaligned runs its
  whole body misaligned, so its statement-level calls are off too,
  to any depth. A second misaligned nesting cancels it. So the
  question is never "is this call nested", it is "can anything
  that reaches C be reached from a nested position".
* The same text can have opposite alignment in two positions.
  `fncallN(...)` in expression position was LOWERED (callee
  spilled to a frame slot); as a bare statement it was a plain
  call into lib/fnptr.cyr with the callee pushed as argument 1.

**PE was inverted, which is the part worth remembering.**
Windows (and UEFI firmware) enter an image the way they call any
function: return address pushed, rsp ≡ 8. Linux enters at ≡ 0.
cyrius never accounted for that, so the whole PE base ran one
parity off — every statement-level call entered its callee at
≡ 0 and the ODD-depth nested calls were the aligned ones. It
survived because cyrius code uses no aligned SSE, but the seven
fixed-frame kernel32 reroutes (EOPEN_PE/ECREATEDIR_PE/EDELETEF_PE
0x258, EEXIT 0x28) had each been hand-tuned to that base over
years — so `f(0, open(path))` faulted inside kernelbase's own
`movaps` in CreateFileW.

⭐ **That is also why the v6.0.71 entry seed "destabilised cycc,
don't retry".** It did — because the seed alone leaves those
frames compensating for a base that no longer needs it. Seed and
retune ship together or PE breaks; neither half is testable
without the other.

**Fix shape (6.6.5)**: a compile-time counter `_xdepth` of
pending 8-byte pushes, maintained by every push/pop consumer, and
ONE parity rule at the call site per ABI (SysV n<=6 `push rax` /
`pop rcx`; SysV n>6 `(nextra+d)&1`; PE n<=4 folded into the
shadow sub; PE n>4 `_pe_call_frame(n, d)`), plus the PE/UEFI
landing seed and the retuned reroutes.

**Recognize again**: any "crashes only sometimes, inside a
vendor library, on its first SSE instruction" report; any
`gdb p $rsp & 0xF` = 8 at a callee entry; any fix that works when
you hoist the call to its own statement. And structurally: a new
target whose loader enters with a return address pushed needs a
landing seed, or every alignment fact you have is inverted there.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "counter_model_gate_shares_the_defect"
description = "A gate that asks the compiler's own model to check that model reads GREEN. The 6.6.5 alignment work measured one mutant that produced zero compile-time desync reports and 24 CPU-measured misaligned calls."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["gates", "verification", "methodology", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
6.6.5 added a compile-time counter (`_xdepth`) modelling how many
values are pushed on the expression stack, plus `_xd_check` at the
parser chokepoints to report a desync. That check is useful and it
is NOT a gate for the bug it was built alongside.

**Measured, not reasoned — and the FIRST version of this note got
the mutant wrong, which is itself the lesson.** The mutant that
produces the zero-desync result is: ECALLCLEAN stops EMITTING the
`pop rcx` that releases the alignment pad **but still decrements
`_xdepth`**. ECALLPOPS increments, ECALLCLEAN decrements, the
model balances perfectly, and the emitted code leaks 8 bytes per
padded call:

* compile-time desync reports: **0**
* CPU-measured misaligned call sites in the gate driver: **24**,
  then SIGSEGV on the `movaps` leaf

⚠ Deleting the whole line — the emitted byte AND the decrement —
is a DIFFERENT mutant and DOES desync: **200 reports** on a cycc
self-compile, because ECALLPOPS's increment is then unmatched.
The first draft of this note (and of the gate header, and of the
CHANGELOG) attributed the zero-desync result to that one, without
rebuilding it. Both were then rebuilt and measured. A mutation
ledger you did not run is a claim, not evidence.

The gate that catches it computes `(rsp+8) & 15` inside a
gcc-assembled leaf — the CPU, reading the frame the caller really
built — and compares against the ABI constant 0. Two independent
derivations; the compiler's counter is not consulted.

⚠ This is the SIXTH instance of this file's "a green gate
measuring nothing" class, and it is kept as its own entry only
because the mechanism is new and measured: the check and the
checked share a model, so one edit moves both.

**The general rule this instance supports**: compute the expected
value a DIFFERENT way from the actual one. A model-checking
assertion is a good tripwire for the cases you did not think of,
and it is worthless against a change that edits the model and the
code in one motion — which is what every "fix" to that area will
look like.

Related: the v6.3.26 FFI gate called `fncall4..7` at TOP LEVEL
only, so it proved alignment for the one position this bug never
touched, and its pass was then cited in the guide and in
docs/ffi/fncall-abi.md as proof of the general claim. A gate
proves the position it tests.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "uefi_exit_must_restore_the_entry_rsp_not_adjust_it"
description = "Under UEFI the exit is a bare `ret` to firmware and `ret` pops [rsp] ITSELF, so the exit must RESTORE the entry rsp — an `add rsp, 8` give-back only fixes the one depth it was computed for. 6.6.5 shipped the give-back first: `syscall(60,x)` inside a fn body #GP'd under OVMF and every gate was green over it."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["uefi", "pe", "abi", "alignment", "gates", "emission-order", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
6.6.5 put a 4-byte `sub rsp, 8` at the PE/UEFI landing so that
depth 0 means 16-aligned on every target (Windows and UEFI
firmware enter an image with the return address already pushed,
rsp ≡ 8; Linux enters at ≡ 0). On Windows that is the whole
story — `EEXIT` there is `call [ExitProcess]; int3` and never
returns.

**Under UEFI it is not.** The entry point IS an MS-x64 function
and firmware reads its `rax` as the EFI_STATUS, so `EEXIT` emits
a single `0xC3`. `ret` pops `[rsp]` ITSELF, so it is correct only
where rsp is EXACTLY the rsp firmware entered with.

⛔ **THE FIRST FIX PAID THE SEED BACK (`add rsp, 8`) AND THAT IS
THE WRONG SHAPE.** A give-back corrects one known displacement;
the exit has to be right at every displacement. rsp is 8 low at
top level, and a whole frame plus its locals low inside a fn
body. Measured on OVMF (edk2, q35):

```
var q = 7; syscall(60, q);                      # top level
  6.6.4:        returns cleanly to BdsDxe
  seed only:    X64 Exception Type - 06(#UD)
  give-back:    clean

fn f(){var a=3; syscall(60,a); return 0;} var r=f();
  6.6.4:        survives by luck
  give-back:    X64 Exception Type - 0D(#GP), RIP inside the stack
  (with a ConOut print first, 6.6.4 #UDs too — the class was
   broken on BOTH compilers)
```

**The shipped fix parks and restores.** The landing emits
`lea r13, [rsp]` BEFORE the seed and `EEXIT` emits
`mov rsp, r13; ret`. Depth-independent, so there is nothing
per-site to track. r13 is reserved from the register allocator on
that target (`_ra_cap = 2` in parse_fn.cyr) — a parked register
that regalloc may assign to a hot local is not parked.

⚠ The park is a 4-byte `lea` and not the 3-byte `mov r13, rsp` on
purpose: the landing must stay an EVEN number of bytes or every
inline CHAR16 literal in a hand-written-asm image (efi_probe,
gnoboot) loses its 2-byte alignment.

⛔ **THE SECOND TRAP IS EMISSION ORDER, AND IT IS THE REUSABLE
ONE. fn BODIES ARE EMITTED BEFORE THE LANDING.** The give-back was
gated on a flag set at `EPATCH(jmp_patch)` and cleared per fn body
by PARSE_FN_DEF — and the top-level definition pass runs FIRST, so
that flag read 0 inside every fn body no matter what the clear
did. The per-body clear looked like it was doing the work. Any
"set a flag at the landing, read it while emitting" is wrong for
anything a fn body emits; derive the condition instead. The
shipped EEXIT reads kmode (`L64(S + 0x18FCA0)`) at the point of
emission for its one remaining choice, executable vs
object/shared mode.

FOUR routes reach that firmware `ret` and a fix has to cover all
four:

1. `main.cyr`'s fall-through exit at the end of the image.
2. A top-level `syscall(60, x)`, reaching EEXIT through the PARSER.
3. A `syscall(60, x)` INSIDE a fn body — not exotic: `lib/alloc.cyr`
   and `lib/bounds.cyr` abort that way, so a UEFI image takes this
   route on OOM or a bounds trip with no explicit exit in user code.
4. `programs/efi_probe.cyr`, whose hand-written asm returns from
   its OWN `ret` and never reaches EEXIT — so it carries the
   correction itself, as a deliberately ASYMMETRIC
   `sub rsp,0x20` / `add rsp,0x28`.

⛔ **THE GATE LESSON IS THE BIGGER ONE, AND IT REPEATED.** OVMF
TOLERATES A MISALIGNED CALL BUT NOT A MISALIGNED RETURN, and the
EFI smoke gate grepped serial output for `hello, uefi` — so it
PASSED on an image that printed and then took a #UD one
instruction later. It now also requires the absence of
`X64 Exception` and a post-return marker. **And that was still not
enough**: the only image it boots is `efi_probe.cyr`, which
returns through route 4 and therefore had NEVER tested how the
COMPILER ends a UEFI image. Route 3 shipped broken under a green
smoke, a green byte gate and a code review. There is now a second
boot (`programs/efi_fn_exit_probe.cyr`) that exits from inside a
fn, and a byte axis that COUNTS `mov rsp,r13; ret` in such an
image — two are required, because that image has two firmware
returns and a top-level-only fix produces one.

**Recognize again**: any entry-alignment seed on a target whose
exit is a `ret` rather than a syscall; any fix expressed as "undo
the N bytes we added" where the site can be reached at more than
one depth; any flag set during emission and read by something
emitted earlier; any gate whose only fixture exercises a path that
bypasses the code under test.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "probe_row_reports_only_through_the_returned_argument"
description = "An alignment/value probe row measures nothing unless the callee returns the argument the probe sits in. `snd(probe(), 0)` returns its SECOND argument and is the constant 0 — four rows of the 6.6.5 alignment test and three of its shell gate were dead that way, and the fix's own test scored a perfect pass over them."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["gates", "verification", "methodology", "testing", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
When a test probes codegen by calling a measuring function from
many expression positions, the row only reports if the VALUE it
finally asserts on carries the probe's reading. It is very easy
to write rows that cannot:

```cyr
fn snd(x, y): i64 { return y; }
a(snd(align_probe(), 0), "argument 1");   # constant 0 — DEAD
a(thd(0, align_probe(), 0), "argument 2"); # thd returns z — DEAD
t = 8; if (zero < probe() + 1) { t = 0; } # true for 0 AND for 8 — DEAD
Rec_b(&rec)                                # a derived getter returns
                                           # the FIELD, never the call
```

All four shapes shipped in the first cut of 6.6.5's own alignment
test and gate, and every one of them PASSED — against the broken
compiler as well as the fixed one.

**The audit that finds them is cheap and mechanical**: rebuild
with the probe hardcoded to return the FAILING value (`mov rax,8;
ret` in place of `lea 8(%rsp),%rax; and $15,%rax`) and require
every row to report. Measured at 6.6.5: 63 of 64 tcyr rows and
56 of 56 gate rows report; the one that does not is a deliberate
loop-count control. Run it whenever a row is added — it is the
per-row analogue of the anti-vacuous twin, which only proves the
probe itself is live.

⚠ A single anti-vacuous control is NOT enough. The 6.6.5 file had
one from the start (`align_probe_8`, reading one slot higher, must
return 8) and it was green while four rows were dead: the probe
was live, the rows just threw its answer away.

⚠ Corollary for the thing being measured: some constructs cannot
report at all. A `#derive(accessors)` GETTER returns the field, so
only the SETTER's argument can carry a probe. Say so at the site
rather than leaving a row that looks like coverage.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_top_level_statement_closes_the_declaration_window"
description = "struct / enum definitions and struct-TYPED globals must appear before the first top-level statement. After one, `struct X {` is `unexpected struct`, and a `var g: T = ...` still compiles but silently loses the struct-type annotation operator-overload dispatch keys on."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["frontend", "parser", "language", "testing", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
Hit while writing a test that needed a struct, an enum, an
`impl`, a `#derive(accessors)` struct and a `#naked` asm probe in
one file. Two distinct symptoms, one cause:

* **`enum Tri { ... }` or `struct Pt { ... }` placed after the
  first top-level statement** is a hard error — `unexpected
  struct` / `unexpected enum`, followed by a cascade of
  `undefined variable` for every field name. Definitions are
  collected in a pre-pass over the top of the file.
* **`var g: OpV = 5;` placed after the first top-level statement
  compiles** and is usable — but the struct-type annotation does
  not survive, and `EMIT_OP_DISPATCH` keys on exactly that, so
  `g + 3` silently becomes a plain integer add instead of calling
  `OpV_add`. No diagnostic.

The second one is the dangerous one, and it is how a test row
that looks like it exercises operator overloading exercises
nothing.

**Ordering that works**: includes → enums → probe/helper fns the
definitions need → structs / impls / `#derive` → globals →
top-level statements → `fn main`. In particular, put the fns an
`impl` body calls ABOVE the `impl`: an impl method that forward-
references gets `warning: undefined function`.

⚠ Related, and worth checking before writing any operator-overload
test: `tests/tcyr/lang/advanced.tcyr`'s `operator add` row asserts
`20 + 22 == 42` over a struct-typed LOCAL. A plain integer add
satisfies that too, and a struct-typed local does not dispatch at
all — so that row proves nothing about dispatch. A dispatch test
needs a GLOBAL receiver and a helper returning a value plain
arithmetic could not produce (the shape
`tests/tcyr/frontend/const_chained_multiply_fold.tcyr` uses:
`fn OpV_add(a, b) { return 3000 + b; }`).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "process_entry_sp_parity_varies_with_argv_env_darwin_x86"
description = "A process entry SP parity is a per-KERNEL fact, not an ABI fact. On Darwin x86_64 the initial rsp's 16-byte parity varies with the argv/env string byte count, so the same binary is aligned under one process name and misaligned under another. Never assume a kernel-aligned entry — a landing must ESTABLISH the alignment invariant, not inherit it."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["codegen", "abi", "alignment", "macho", "platform", "verification", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
Every alignment rule in cyrius's x86 backend is stated RELATIVE
to the entry landing — "depth 0 means 16-aligned", and the pad
machinery keeps that true through pushes. So the landing itself
has to be an ABSOLUTE. In 6.6.5 THREE targets turned out to be
inheriting it from an assumption instead:

* **PE/Win64** — Windows enters with the return address pushed,
  rsp = 8 (mod 16). Seeded (`sub rsp, 8`).
* **UEFI** — same firmware entry, plus a `ret` back to firmware.
  Seeded, plus an entry-rsp park in r13.
* **x86_64 Mach-O** — **no fixed parity at all.**

The third is the one worth remembering. MEASURED ON REAL HARDWARE
(ach, Darwin 22.6.0, x86_64): XNU does not 16-align the initial
rsp of a static Mach-O executable. It falls where the argv/env
string area leaves it, so the parity is a function of the
process's own NAME and ENVIRONMENT:

```
./_l            misaligned      ./_ltxx                 aligned
./_lt           misaligned      /tmp/csa2               aligned
PADVAR=x ./_lt  misaligned      PADVAR=xxxxxxx ./_lt    aligned
```

A 20-argv0-length x 16-env-pad sweep of a `#naked` `(rsp+8)&15`
probe splits 160/160, and it tracks the PARKED entry rsp
(`r15 & 15`) ROW FOR ROW — i.e. the misalignment at every call
site WAS the kernel's entry parity, carried through the landing
untouched. It is still 160/160 with the fix in: the kernel has
not changed, the landing absorbs it.

**Fix shape**: `and rsp, -16` (48 83 E4 F0) at the landing. It
rounds DOWN, so it is correct for either parity and free when the
entry was already aligned. AFTER the init-rsp park (`mov r15,
rsp`), never before — the park is what keeps argc/argv reachable
once rsp moves. And in BOTH x86 forks (`src/main.cyr`'s
`_TARGET_MACHO == 1` arm and `src/main_x86_macho.cyr`); only the
native fork is the compiler a Mac actually runs.

**Which targets may inherit, with the evidence:** Linux ELF only
(SysV amd64 3.4.1 guarantees rsp = 0 at `_start`; measured
320/320). aarch64 is safe architecturally (measured 320/320 on
ecb, anti-vacuous control 320/320 at 8). agnos builds rsp from
two 16-aligned terms in its own loader.

⛔ **HOW IT PRESENTS, AND WHY IT COST A ROUND.** It does NOT look
like an entry bug. Every row of the cross-OS alignment test
shifts together, so it reads as a scatter of unrelated shape
failures — capturing-closure callptr at odd depth, method
dispatch, operator-overload dispatch, a derive setter argument,
for-in, while — with nothing naming the entry. The fix for that
is a test row that reads the base EXPLICITLY (a global
initialised with the probe, so it is measured in the gvar-init
block right after the landing) asserted FIRST and labelled
ENTRY.

⛔ **AND A SINGLE RUN IS NOT A VERIFICATION ON DARWIN.** The
cross-OS lib-test runner always names the binary `./_lt`, i.e. it
samples exactly ONE parity. On this host that name is the
misaligned one and the gate went red; one byte longer and the
same broken compiler scores GREEN. Any entry-alignment claim on
Darwin needs the argv0/env sweep, not a re-run.

**What can be checked without the hardware** (and what should
have been): the cross-compiler emits Mach-O from Linux, so the
LANDING BYTES are readable on the dev box — `mov r15, rsp`
(49 89 E7) immediately followed by `and rsp, -16` — with an
anti-vacuous check that the ELF build does NOT carry the pair,
plus a source-level fork-parity check on both forks. Deleting the
helper from the NATIVE fork only leaves the byte axis green,
which is why the source axis is there too.

⭐ THE GENERALISATION, which was available two targets earlier
and was not drawn: **"the kernel aligns the entry" is a claim to
be measured on that kernel, not inferred from another platform's
ABI.** Three targets, three separate discoveries, one rule.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "two_passes_computing_the_same_fact_disagree_make_pass_one_the_authority"
description = "When a compiler computes a fact in two passes, the passes WILL disagree, and the failure is silent. cyrius stamped fn visibility in pass 1 AND recomputed it in pass 2, and both halves were wrong in different ways: pass 1 did not stamp every definition kind (so the check fell open), and pass 2 mis-armed the marker (masked only because the pass-1 bit was never cleared). The fix is one authority — stamp per DEFINITION in pass 1, look the definition up by its own token in pass 2, and take the stamp."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "frontend", "visibility", "two-pass", "private", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
cycc parses twice: pass 1 walks the token stream registering
declarations so forward references resolve, pass 2 emits. Fn
visibility (`private`) was computed in BOTH.

⛔ **PASS 1 DID NOT REGISTER EVERYTHING, and an unregistered
definition is fail-OPEN by construction.** `_vis_check` tests the
callee's private FLAG first and its owner second — an unstamped
callee has neither, so the check returned at its first test. Four
kinds were unstamped: impl methods (all seven `src/main*.cyr`
forks brace-skipped impl bodies), `mod`-scoped fns (a `GMOD == 0`
guard), every fn after the FIRST TOP-LEVEL STATEMENT (pass 1
stops there while `PARSE_PROG` keeps defining fns), and anything
above the `private` line — because the marker was recorded as
pass 1 WALKED PAST it, so a definition written earlier was
prescanned while the file still read public.

⭐ **THE REVEALING PART IS NOT THE FAIL-OPEN, IT IS THE MIRROR
IMAGE.** The filing said "only on an include order normal code
never produces". The same root produced, in ORDINARY order:

* **False refusals.** Pass 1 kept one entry per NAME, so a second
  file's same-named helper overwrote the first file's owner,
  arity and private bit before either body was parsed. Two
  private files each with a `_h` gave `'_h' is private to its
  file` AND `'_h' expects 2 arguments, got 1` on a legal program.
* **A public fn reported private.** The private bit was only ever
  SET, never cleared, so a public definition inherited a stale
  stamp — the language guide's own two-file example was REFUSED
  when the public file was included first.
* **Silent SIGSEGV.** No pass-1 signature means no parameter
  masks, so a forward call passing a >8-byte struct took the
  mask-0 ABI and pushed it BY VALUE into a callee that derefs the
  parameter as a pointer. rc=139, no diagnostic, x86 + aarch64 +
  PE.
* **Diagnostics naming the wrong symbol.** The method-call path
  registered an UNCOMMITTED scratch name (the mangler writes at
  `GNPOS` and deliberately does not advance it), so the next mint
  overwrote it and `q.nosuch()` was reported as
  `undefined function 'Q7_seven'` — a method that exists.

**The shape to remember: a stamping gap does not present as "the
check is too permissive". It presents as a scatter of unrelated
bugs — wrong ABI, wrong arity, wrong symbol name, wrong refusal —
because everything downstream reads the same empty record.**

**Fix shape, generalisable:**

1. Do the file-level marking ONCE, before either pass (a single
   walk at the end of LEX). Anything recorded DURING a walk makes
   the result depend on position in the file.
2. Register every definition kind in pass 1, per DEFINITION, not
   per name. The identity rule ("is this existing entry mine?")
   becomes one shared function both passes call, so they cannot
   drift.
3. Pass 2 finds the entry pass 1 gave THIS definition by its own
   `fn` token and takes the stamp — but only while nothing has
   claimed that entry yet, because two same-file definitions of
   one name legitimately share one entry and it holds ONE bit.
4. CLEAR the bit when a definition is public. A flag that is only
   ever set is a latch, and a latch across a shared entry is how
   a public fn becomes private.
5. Keep a fail-CLOSED-LATER backstop: a reference whose callee has
   no owner AND no body is DEFERRED and re-judged once everything
   is stamped. It reports nothing on legitimately-unknown callees
   (enum constructors, DCE stubs) and it is what catches the NEXT
   definition kind someone forgets to walk. Measured: with the
   relaxed-ordering prescan disabled, the deferral still refuses
   the call — but the program MISCOMPILES, because the ABI comes
   from the masks, not from the visibility check. A backstop is
   not a substitute for the stamp.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_cannot_pack_reason_deserves_the_same_scrutiny_as_the_bug"
description = "'It needs a design decision' is the cannot-pack reason that survives longest unexamined. A cyrius filing deferred a fix on the grounds that it required a name-pool design decision across seven compiler forks; the decision took one 18-line function, and the fork obligation was two one-line calls each. Cost the fix a release."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["process", "deferral", "compiler", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
The filing said, in its own *Why it was not packed* section:

> deciding how the pass-1 mangle coexists with pass 2's — a design
> decision on the name pool, with seed-derive exposure. That is
> the "cannot pack" reason CLAUDE.md names.

It reads like diligence: the reason is named rather than left as
prose, which is what the rule asks for. But nobody had tried it.

**The answer was mechanical.** Mint the mangled name at `GNPOS`
WITHOUT committing; if an fn of exactly that name is already
registered, reuse ITS string and leave the pool untouched;
otherwise commit. Name lookup compares by STRING, so a string
minted in either pass serves both, and pool growth is unchanged
because the second mint is discarded. Eighteen lines, and it
removed a duplicated mangler rather than adding one.

The other two clauses were no better:

* **"seed-derive exposure"** — real, and the reason to RUN
  seed-derive, not a reason to defer. It was GREEN.
* **"a 7-fork obligation"** — two one-line calls into shared code
  per fork, guarded by a static parity axis in the new gate that
  derives the fork list with a glob.

⭐ **The test to apply: can you state the design decision as a
question with a small number of answers?** If yes, it is not a
design decision, it is a thing you have not tried. A genuine one
("what should the language DO here") has a user in it.

⚠ And the cost is not one release of delay. The defect stayed
open long enough that its severity line — "Medium, only on an
include order normal code never produces" — was quoted forward as
if it had been measured, while the same root was refusing
legitimate programs and miscompiling struct arguments in ordinary
include order the whole time.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "routing_a_new_caller_through_a_shared_helper_inherits_its_latent_bug"
description = "The fix for a call path with no ABI handling was 'route it through the shared helper the other paths use'. The helper had its own latent defect — it took a local's ADDRESS unconditionally, which is wrong for a pointer-mode struct — so the new caller traded a loud SIGSEGV for a silent wrong value. Test the helper against the shape the new caller brings, not just against the bug you are fixing."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "abi", "struct", "refactor", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
cyrius's method-call path (`s.m(arg)`) pushed every argument as a
bare value, with no reference to the callee's parameter masks. A
`>8`-byte struct argument therefore arrived BY VALUE at a callee
that derefs the parameter as a pointer: SIGSEGV. The obvious fix,
and the one written in the plan, was "resolve the callee first and
route each argument through `_try_push_struct_addr_arg`" — the
helper `PARSE_FNCALL` and four other paths already use.

⛔ **That helper pushed `&slot` UNCONDITIONALLY.** A struct-typed
local in cyrius is one of two things:

* **inline** — the slot IS the struct, so `&slot` is its address;
* **pointer-mode** — the slot HOLDS the struct's heap address, so
  the callee wants the slot's VALUE.

Taking the address of a pointer-mode slot hands the callee a
pointer-to-pointer, and it reads the address itself as field 0.
Measured on the unfixed compiler with `var b: HB = alloc(24);
b.a = 10; …; f(0, b)`: it printed **140545185808389** where 42 is
right. No crash, no diagnostic. So the method path would have gone
from *SIGSEGV on inline / correct on heap* to *correct on inline /
silent garbage on heap* — strictly worse in the dimension that
matters, since a wrong number travels further than a fault.

⭐ **WHY IT SURVIVED:** the defect is invisible from the helper's
existing callers. They were all reached through code paths whose
own tests used inline structs, and the ONE in-tree corpus (316
tests, 131 programs, 408 raw-compilable ecosystem sources) contains
zero instances of the shape — because it had never worked, so
nobody wrote it. A shared helper's blast radius is measured over
what people DO call it with, which is exactly the set that avoids
its bugs.

**What to do instead of routing-and-hoping:**

1. Write the new caller's shape as a probe FIRST, run it against
   the helper as-is, and read the value — not the exit code. This
   one exits 0.
2. Check the helper against the OTHER polarity too. The rule here
   already existed twice in the same compiler (field access and a
   method's `self` both discriminate on a -1 slot marker plus a
   `<= 8 B` single-slot rule); argument passing was the third user
   and the only one that had never asked.
3. Fix the HELPER, then measure the blast radius on both sides. In
   this case the byte-identity differential over four backends and
   the ecosystem was zero, which is what says the change is a
   strict improvement rather than a behavioural swap.

⚠ And exempt what the rule does not describe. A value-form SIMD
local is always inline and its type tag lives in a descriptor band
where the struct-size table has no entry, so it must SKIP the test
rather than answer it wrong — `_is_simd_any` first, then the rule.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "one_call_syntax_is_not_one_call_path_enumerate_the_masks"
description = "cyrius has FOUR argument-marshalling paths for what a reader sees as one call: PARSE_FNCALL, the method-dot path, the tail-call path and the PE SIMD-return path. Each consults the callee's parameter masks independently, so a mask honoured by one and not the others is a silent wrong value that differs between two spellings of the same call in the same program. Enumerate the masks, not the bug."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "abi", "call-paths", "tail-call", "simd", "str", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
A cyrius callee records what each parameter wants in per-fn bitmask
tables: `_fnt_structmask` (>8-byte struct, pass its address),
`_fnt_strmask` (`: Str`, wrap a string literal in `str_from`),
`_fnt_simdmask` (vector, load into XMM/V *after* the integer args are
popped, never push it), `_fnt_cstrmask`, `_fnt_ovstr` / `_fnt_ovint`
(overload dispatch).

`PARSE_FNCALL` reads all of them. **Three other paths marshal
arguments too**, and each one reads whatever subset its author needed
that day:

* the method path `s.m(a)` (`parse_decl.cyr`) — read NONE until 6.6.5,
  and had no arity check either: `q.one(5, 99)` on a 2-parameter method
  BUILT and returned 6 where the identical free call is a hard error;
* the tail-call path `return f(a);` (`parse_fn.cyr`) — emits its own
  epilogue + `jmp`, so it never enters PARSE_FNCALL at all;
* the Win64 hidden-retptr vector RECEIVES — and that is not one path but
  FOUR loops (`_try_vector_call_assign`, the `_f2c` / `_f4c` receives, the
  PE SIMD-return `_rc` path), each `_TARGET_PE == 1` only. ⚠ They are
  invisible to every x86-Linux run: the same source gave 5 on ELF and 0 on
  PE, under wine, on two consecutive cuts of the same fix.

⚠ **COUNT THE LOOPS, NOT THE PATHS.** "Four paths" is how this was written
down after 6.6.5 round 1 and it was already an undercount — `grep -n
'_try_push_struct_addr_arg(' src/frontend/` lists the argument loops, and
there were seven.

⭐ **THE TELL IS TWO SPELLINGS DISAGREEING IN ONE PROGRAM.** Not a
crash, not a diagnostic — two lines that should be the same value:

    var r = f("abcde");    # 5
    return f("abcde");     # 0   -- no str_from wrap on the tail path

    h.vmix(2, v, 3)        # 927 -- vector pushed as an int arg, so
    free_vmix(0, 2, v, 3)  # 923    every LATER argument shifts a register

The tail path's history is the whole lesson: v6.3.36 added a divert
for plain-struct params, v6.4.53 for value-form SIMD params, v6.5.1
for overload dispatch, v6.5.2 for the integer-literal-to-cstring
error, and 6.6.5 for the `: Str` literal wrap. **Five separate
discoveries of the same structural gap**, each found by a user hitting
one spelling.

**What to do when you touch any of these paths:**

1. List the mask tables from their definition site, not from the path
   you are editing. `grep '_fnt_[a-z]*mask' src/frontend/` is the
   enumeration.
2. For each, write the two-spelling probe (`var r = f(x)` against
   `return f(x)`, free fn against `obj.m(...)`) and compare the
   VALUES.
3. Prefer EXTRACTING PARSE_FNCALL's arm into a shared helper over
   copying it. 6.6.5 lifted `_try_push_str_literal_arg`,
   `_simd_arg_record` and `_simd_arg_second_pass` out precisely so the
   method path cannot drift from the free-fn path again.
4. A divert (`tc_has_addr = 1`, forcing the general path) is simpler
   than teaching the special path a new mask, and it is what the four
   previous fixes did — **but its PREDICATE must match PARSE_FNCALL's
   exactly, and "over-covering is the safe direction" is FALSE here.**
   6.6.5's first cut armed the `: Str` divert on a literal at ANY paren
   depth and wrote "over-diverting costs a tail call" in the comment.
   It does not: losing TCO on a SELF-RECURSIVE tail call turns bounded
   recursion into unbounded stack growth, and a correct program that ran
   on 6.6.4 started SIGSEGVing. Read the predicate off the helper you
   are diverting TO — `_try_push_str_literal_arg` opens
   `if (PEEKT(S) != 30) return 0`, i.e. an argument's FIRST token, i.e.
   paren depth 1 — and gate on that. A predicate copied from the general
   path cannot under-cover by construction, and does not over-cover.

⚠ **AND THE CONTROL CAN BE THE BROKEN ONE.** The 6.6.5 tail-call
defect was found because a new test asserted `obj.m("lit")` equalled
the identical free fn — and the free fn had been written as
`return f(...)`, so it was wrong too. Both sides agreed at a wrong
value until a third expectation (the literal's length, computed in the
test) was added. Compute the expected value a DIFFERENT way from the
actual one, and make sure "different way" is not just a different
spelling of the same path.

⛔ **6.6.6: THE ENUMERATION GREP ITSELF UNDERCOUNTED — IT FINDS ONLY LOOPS
THAT ALREADY CALL A GATE.** `grep '_try_push_struct_addr_arg('` lists the
loops that run *that* gate; a loop that runs NO gate is invisible to it.
PARSE_VAR's two struct-valued receives (`var p: S = f(..)` — the >16 B
retptr `asv` loop and the 9-16 B `asv_pair` loop) were bare
`PCMPE + EPUSHR` per argument and so never showed up: a vector argument
shifted every later int, a struct argument went by value (SIGSEGV), a
`: Str` literal was not wrapped, and the arity check never ran. Enumerate
by the thing EVERY call must emit — `grep -n 'ECALLPOPS(' src/frontend/`
— and then ask each hit which gates it runs. 6.6.6 moved the per-argument
gates into ONE helper, `_call_arg_one`, which the method loop and both
receives now share.

⭐ **A HIDDEN ARGUMENT IS PART OF THE CALL TOO.** The same bite found that
only `var p: S = f(..)` supplied the >16 B struct's hidden RETPTR; an
expression statement, an argument, `x = f(..)` and `return f(..)` (the
TAIL path) marshalled the user args as if there were none, and the callee
wrote its result through argument 1. The matrix to walk is (call syntax) x
(return class: scalar / rax:rdx pair / retptr / vector), not just
(call syntax) x (parameter mask) — and top level, where there is no frame
to give the result, is a syntax too (v6.6.6).

⛔ **AND "CALL SYNTAX" HAS MORE ROWS THAN THE FIX'S DESTINATIONS COULD SEE.**
That first cut gave the retptr a destination at every site that could
RECOGNISE a struct call by its tokens — `IDENT (`, the callee named by
the next token. Two call syntaxes never look like that: a METHOD call
`b.mk(2)` (the callee is resolved from `b`'s TYPE) and an OVERLOADED
OPERATOR `a + b` (the callee `V3_add` is never written at all). Both emit
their own call, so `var r: P3 = b.mk(2)`, `r = b.mk(5)`, `h(b.mk(1))` and
`var c: V3 = a + b` still SIGSEGV'd after the fix, while the identical
`B_mk(b, 2)` worked — and the guide the same commit shipped said the call
"may be written anywhere". The fix that scales is the reverse direction:
the EMITTER (which knows its callee) allocates the temp, passes the retptr
and RECORDS the token span it covered; a destination arms the token it is
about to parse and takes the temp only when the record spans EXACTLY its
expression (`_sc_pre` / `_sc_post` / `_sc_whole`, v6.6.6). Begin AND end
must match — `k + a * b` ends where `a * b` ends. When a fix keys on the
SHAPE of source text, list every syntax that reaches the same codegen and
ask which ones have that shape.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "aggregate_layout_is_recorded_never_inferred_from_a_neighbour_slot"
description = "cycc guessed whether an aggregate LOCAL was inline (the slot IS the struct) or pointer-mode (the slot HOLDS its address) from `is the slot below named -1?`. That marker is not a discriminator: SCOPE_POP writes -1 over every local of a CLOSED BLOCK, and callptr / bitset / SIMD-reserve / closure-env temporaries are registered -1 by construction. So any pointer-mode struct local declared after a closed block or after one of those temporaries read its fields OFF ITS OWN FRAME SLOT — silent since 5.8.17, live in a shipped consumer (stiva let a 512 MB node pass a 1024 MB constraint). Fixed v6.6.5 by RECORDING the slot span at registration (SLAGG/GLSPAN, high 32 bits of the existing per-slot depth word) and giving every consumer ONE reader, `_local_struct_is_ptr`."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["codegen", "layout", "struct-locals", "scope-pop", "silent-miscompile", "discriminator", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**The class, stated generally: a property that is DECIDED at one place and RE-DERIVED at
another will disagree, and the re-derivation is the one that ships wrong.** Five separate
consumers each asked "is this local inline or a pointer?" by reading a NEIGHBOURING slot's
name, and the writer of that name (`SCOPE_POP`, util.cyr) had no idea anyone was reading it:

```cyrius
fn f(c) {
    if (c == 1) { var t = 5; }   # SCOPE_POP renames `t`'s slot to -1 when the block closes
    var p: P = mk();             # ...so `p` "looks inline". It is a POINTER.
    return p.y;                  # 6.6.4: 6. Correct: 22.
}
```

The same guess is defeated by `callptr` (181 for 22), by `bitset` temporaries, by SIMD
reserves and by closure-env slots — all of which legitimately register name -1.

**How to recognise it again.** A predicate whose INPUT is a side effect of unrelated code is
not a predicate. Ask: *who writes the thing I am reading, and do they know I read it?* If the
answer is "several places, for their own reasons", the property has to be RECORDED at the
point where it is actually known — here, at the variable's registration.

**The cheap place to put a recorded bit.** Depths are small non-negative counters, so the
per-slot depth word had 32 unused high bits. Putting the span there means no new table, no
heap-map change, and it CLEARS ITSELF on slot reuse because every named registration writes
`SLDEP` before anything else. A new side table would have needed a per-fork base
initialisation; the high-bits trick needed none.

**Companion trap in the same release:** the aggregate's WIDTH, not just a flag, is what the
coroutine emitters need. A stack frame's slot addresses DESCEND with the index; a coroutine's
heap frame ASCENDS. So `&agg + k*8` walks off a multi-word aggregate in a coroutine, and
`EFLADDR` can only recover a SINGLE-slot local there. Storing the span rather than one bit is
what let that be answered exactly instead of guessed again.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "compiler_generated_temporaries_must_not_be_globals_or_named_zero"
description = "`switch`, `match`, both `?` lowerings and `for ... in` each stashed their subject in a GLOBAL slot registered under NAME OFFSET 0. Three silent consequences: recursion reloaded the outer call's value, threads shared one word, and name offset 0 is the FIRST LEXED WORD of the program — so FINDVAR (a flat last-match table) RETURNED those slots for that identifier and `&setup` changed value after an intervening fn containing a switch. Frame slots named 0 aliased the same way through FINDLOCAL, which matches any `sn >= 0`. Fixed v6.6.5: `_HTEMP`/`_HTSTORE`/`_HTLOAD` — an anonymous FRAME slot inside a fn, a dead-marked global at top level."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parser", "lowering", "hidden-temporaries", "findvar", "recursion", "threads", "name-offset-0", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**It was a HABIT, not a lowering.** Five constructs each open-coded the same four lines —
`GVCNT`, two `S64` writes, `SVCNT` — to get one scratch word, and each passed name offset 0
because "it has no name". Copy-paste made the defect five times over, which is why the repair
shipped with a CENSUS gate (`tests/gates/codegen/hidden_temp_census.sh`) rather than only a
behaviour test: the behaviour test pins the five that exist, the census pins the SHAPE so the
sixth is caught at the source.

**Name offset 0 is not "no name".** The lexer resets NPOS to 0, so offset 0 is the first
identifier the program lexes. `FINDVAR` walks names backward with no scope or owner filter, so
a hidden temporary was a perfectly good match for it. `FINDLOCAL` is worse: it accepts any
stored name `>= 0`, so a FRAME slot named 0 aliases the same identifier — which is how the
slice/u128 high half, the `secret` flag and the `defer` flag each became reachable. The
anonymous convention in this tree is `0 - 1`, and it has to be written that way everywhere.

**The recursion trap when you go to TEST it.** A recursive fn that finishes its loop BEFORE
recursing passes on the broken compiler — the shared bound has already done its job. The
recursive call has to be INSIDE the loop body, and a `match` has to be re-entered from an arm
PATTERN (a call in the pattern position), i.e. after the subject is stashed and before it is
compared. A first cut of the gate asserted the obvious shape and went green on 6.6.4.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_fn_local_static_array_must_not_take_a_global_name"
description = "An array local over the per-fn frame budget (~120 KB), or any array under CYRIUS_STACK_ARRAYS=0, falls back to shared STATIC storage — and was registered with an ordinary GLOBAL NAME, so it captured every later reference to that identifier anywhere in the program. It was also invisible to FINDLOCAL, so an outer LOCAL of the same name won inside its own fn. v6.6.5 keeps the storage static (raising the budget is a heap-layout change) and scopes only the NAME, and turns the `note:` into a WARNING that says the storage is shared across calls and threads."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parser", "arrays", "storage-class", "stack-arrays", "secret", "scoping", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**Two separable questions, and conflating them is what made this hard to fix for two years:**
*where does the memory live* (a heap-layout decision, expensive) and *what does the name mean*
(a scoping decision, cheap). The filing framed it as one. Only the second had to move.

**The `secret` half is the sharper lesson.** `secret var buf[N]` decided local-vs-static from
the stack-arrays FLAG rather than from where the array ACTUALLY went, so an over-budget
`secret var` was refused outright — and, with an outer local of the same name in scope,
`FINDLOCAL` found the wrong thing, the zeroise ran against the wrong storage, and the buffer
was never cleared. A security feature that silently does nothing is worse than one that errors.
Ask the SLOT-COUNT DELTA (`GFLC` before vs after), which is the fact, not the intention.

**The diagnostic class matters.** `note:` reads as informational; the consequence here is
semantic — ONE buffer for every call and every thread — so it is a `warning:` that says so.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "one_variable_two_frames_the_half_fixed_address_of"
description = "6.6.5. A coroutine's locals live in a heap frame, not the stack. `EFLADDR`/`EFLADDR_X1` (field access, method self) were taught that frame; `ELOAD_LOCAL_ADDR` (the `&name` path) was not — so `q.v = 11` stored to `r11+0x28` and `peek(&q)` read `rbp-0x40`, and the variable silently answered 0. The un-fixed half had a recovery written for it at v6.5.70 that HAD NEVER ONCE FIRED, because it was guarded on `_cur_fn_regalloc == 0` and an async fn body reserves the five callee-saved slots like any other fn."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["coroutines", "async", "codegen", "x86", "regalloc", "address-of", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**The general shape: when one VARIABLE is reachable through two emitters, fixing one of them
is worse than fixing neither.** Before the fix both halves addressed the stack, which was
wrong but CONSISTENT — and it read as correct for years because a resumed coroutine frame
usually lands back on the same bytes. The moment one half learned the heap frame, the
variable split down the middle and the answer went from accidentally-right to silently 0.
Grep for every emitter that can produce an address for the same storage BEFORE changing one.

**The dead guard is the second lesson.** v6.5.70's recovery read:

    if (_cur_fn_regalloc == 0) { if (_cur_fn_ret_stash == 0) {
        var _ai = (0 - adisp) / 8 - 1;

`_cur_fn_regalloc` is 5 in every coroutine body, so the branch was unreachable — and the
displacement inside it did not subtract the shifts either, so it would have computed the
wrong slot if it had run. A comment above it asserted "both of which are ZERO in a coroutine".
**A guard nobody can reach is not conservative, it is untested code that will one day run.**
Subtract the shifts instead: `(0 - adisp - _cur_fn_regalloc * 8 - _cur_fn_ret_stash) / 8 - 1`.

**A multi-word aggregate needs its LOW word, and refusing it was a capability regression.**
The stack descends with slot index and a coroutine's heap frame ASCENDS, so basing a struct
at its NAMED slot puts word k on a different local. The first cut of the fix made that a hard
error — but `var q: P2;` in a suspending fn, and even a struct used only BEFORE the first
suspend, compiled on the previous release and returned the right answer, so the diagnostic
took working code away. Base at the aggregate's LOWEST-INDEX slot instead: the block owns the
same `n` contiguous words in both frames, and the internal word ORDER being reversed is
invisible because every consumer either addresses the block through that one base or walks
BOTH sides by slot index (a permutation applied identically to source and destination copies
the same word to the same word).

**A diagnostic is the right answer only for a gap that cannot be closed.** Check which of the
refused shapes compile on the PREVIOUS release before shipping a refusal.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "tightening_one_side_of_a_symmetric_operation_creates_an_asymmetry"
description = "6.6.5. `_try_aggregate_copy_assign` opened `if (GINFN(S) != 1) return 0;` and then FINDLOCAL'd both sides, so `B = A` between two inline struct GLOBALS copied EIGHT BYTES while the identical assignment between two locals copied the whole struct. The local half had just been tightened onto the recorded layout; the global half had never been reachable at all."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parser", "codegen", "globals", "aggregates", "struct-copy", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**When a fix tightens one operand class of a symmetric operation, enumerate the other classes
in the same change.** `dst = src` has four arms — local←local, local←global, global←local,
global←global — and only the first was implemented. Fixing it made the SAME source line answer
differently depending on where the two variables lived, which is worse than both being wrong,
because the wrong one is now the one nobody is looking at.

**A global slot index is NOT a word index.** `SVCNT` advances by ONE per variable while
`_vars_base` holds its BYTE size, so `EVLOAD(gvi + 1)` reads the NEXT VARIABLE. Every other
global-aggregate consumer goes through `EVADDR(gvi) + byte offset` for exactly that reason,
and the first cut of this fix did not — it produced 0 for a two-word copy and looked like a
different bug entirely.

**Globals are packed by EXACT byte size** (`x86/fixup.cyr` sums `_vars_base` with no round-up
below 8), so a 12-byte struct global is followed at +12. A word-granular copy of
`ceil(sz/8)` words clobbers 4 bytes of its NEIGHBOUR — the same overrun v5.9.36 fixed for the
literal's own field stores. Copy the tail at 4/2/1. The gate row that catches this is a
neighbouring global asserted to still hold its own value, not the copy's own fields.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "metadata_recorded_after_the_code_that_reads_it_splits_one_variable_in_two"
description = "6.6.5. `_STRUCT_LIT_LOCAL` recorded the aggregate's slot span (SLAGG) at the BOTTOM of the fn, after the zero-fill and every initialiser store was already emitted. On the stack that is invisible; inside a coroutine the emitters consult the span to find the block's base, so the literal's own stores based one place and every later read based another — and the stores ran past the block into the next local."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parser", "codegen", "coroutine", "aggregates", "struct-literal", "registration-order", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**Record a fact about a slot BEFORE emitting any code that consults it.** The registration
order here was: reserve the slots, emit the zero-fill, parse and emit the initialisers, THEN
name the slot and record its span. Naming last is deliberate and correct (`var cfg = P { cfg, 2 };`
must see the OUTER `cfg`) — but the SPAN is not the name, nothing resolves through it, and
recording it late made the emitters answer two different questions about the same variable:
the stores went to `EFLADDR_X1(named)` with span 0 and the reads to the same call with span
`n`, which in a heap coroutine frame is a different base. `var p = P2{5,6}` across a suspend
read 5 where the previous release read 56, silently.

**A typed declaration and a literal are DIFFERENT registration paths.** `var p: P2;` records
its span at registration and was correct throughout, so a gate written only against the typed
form passes over a broken literal. When a feature has two spellings that reach the same
emitters by different routes, the gate needs a row for each — the first cut's axis had typed
rows only and read GREEN.

**The visible half is the overrun, not the wrong value.** A block based `n-1` slots off writes
past itself over whatever local follows, so the cheapest gate row is a `guard` local declared
after the aggregate and asserted to still hold its seed — it catches the neighbour damage even
when the aggregate's own fields happen to read back plausibly.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "moving_a_symbol_to_a_new_resolution_rung_drops_whatever_the_old_rung_set"
description = "6.6.5. Making `var a = Num{1}` a frame local moved it off the global rung and onto the closure-CAPTURE rung, which emitted the right value but never called SESTYPE — so `|x| a + x` compiled to an integer add where the same source had dispatched `Num_add`, and with the operator fn DELETED it compiled clean where the previous release refused the program."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parser", "closures", "operator-overloading", "capture", "storage-class", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
**A storage-class change is a RESOLUTION change, and every rung sets different side data.**
`PARSE_TERM`'s identifier ladder has four rungs — closure local, closure capture, enclosing
local, global — and each one emits a value AND records expression metadata (`SESTYPE`,
`SESVAR`, `SEXW`, `SPSC`). Only three of them set the struct type. Moving a declaration from
global storage to a frame slot silently re-routed every read of it to the rung that did not.

**The tell is not the wrong number, it is the LOST DIAGNOSTIC.** `3` instead of `102` looks
like arithmetic. Delete the operator fn and compare: the old compiler refuses the program
(`reachable undefined function`), the new one compiles it. A fix that turns a compile error
into a silent answer is a capability regression however right the number would have been.

**Read the capture metadata from the SNAPSHOT, not the live tables.** By the time a closure
BODY is parsed, the shared per-fn slot tables hold the closure's own locals, so slot index
`esl` no longer means the enclosing variable. `_cl_snap_span` / `_cl_snap_type` read the
saved copy; anything reading `GLSPAN(esl)` there is reading a different variable.

**When you add an encoding band, check every decoder of the old one.** The capture operand is
encoded `-1000000 - capi` and a local is `-2 - li`; a capture is therefore ALSO `<= -2`, so
`_OP_OPERAND_ADDR`'s local arm silently accepted it as a nonsense slot index until the capture
band was tested first.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_warning_raised_after_its_subject_was_consumed_names_the_next_statement"
description = "6.6.5. The over-budget array-local warning fires once `]` and `;` are consumed, and WARN reads the parse CURSOR — so `var bigbuf[200000];` on line 3 printed `:4:5:` with the excerpt and caret under the following statement, while the release note told downstreams the warning carries a file:line for the declaration."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["diagnostics", "parser", "warnings", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`WARN` / `ERR` / `_err_excerpt` all read `GTI(S)`. That is right for a parse error — the cursor
IS the problem — and wrong for anything raised after the parser walked past its subject. The
repo already had the fix pattern twice (`_err_head_at`, and `_priv_per_item_err`'s save-cursor /
`STI` / restore), so the rule is: **a diagnostic about a construct saves that construct's token
index when it enters and reports against it**, rather than growing a second excerpt path.

**Pin the LINE, not just the text.** A gate that greps the message body passes over a warning
pointing at the wrong statement. Put the declaration in a probe and derive the expected line by
grepping the probe file — never by writing the number in the gate.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_generated_tables_two_halves_drawn_from_one_source_cannot_intersect"
description = "6.6.5. The raw-syscall diagnostic's better clause — 'on ELF-aarch64 that number is `ptrace`' — was DEAD CODE for 13 minors. Both halves of the generated table were derived from the same file, and the emit predicate for one half was the exact complement of the other, so the two lookups could never both hit. 43 numbers warned; 0 ever carried the text."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["diagnostics", "codegen", "generated-code", "aarch64", "syscalls", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`src/common/syscall_xlat.cyr` is generated from the two stdlib syscall peers. `_SYSX_MEANT(n)`
answers "what x86 call is n", and `_SYSX_ACTUALLY(n)` answers "what does n do on aarch64". The
generator emitted a `_SYSX_MEANT` row only when `n` is **not** a value the aarch64 peer declares,
and built `_SYSX_ACTUALLY` from **exactly those declared values**. Two sets, one complement of
the other. `parse_expr.cyr` then asks both, and the second could not answer for anything the
first admitted — so the clause that names the call the number really invokes never printed once
between v6.5.51 and v6.6.4.

⭐ **THE TELL IS THE PREDICATE, NOT THE OUTPUT.** Nothing looked wrong: the table was large, every
row plausible, the warnings well-formed. It surfaced only from compiling a probe containing all
43 rows and grepping for the second clause — 43 warnings, 0 matches. **When a generator emits two
lookups that a consumer chains, compile a probe with every key and count how many reach the
second one.** A row count proves the table exists; it does not prove any row is reachable.

**The fix is a DIFFERENT SOURCE for the second half.** The peer cannot name calls the stdlib has
no wrapper for, which is most of the interesting ones. `_SYSX_ACTUALLY` is generated from the
committed kernel table (`tests/data/syscalls/aarch64.tbl`) now, restricted to the `_SYSX_MEANT`
keys so every row is reachable by construction: 35 of 43 carry the text, the other 8 are
genuinely unassigned on aarch64 and the message says so. The table SHRANK by 47 lines, because
~82 of its rows had been unreachable.

⚠ **A generator that reads an external fact must HARD FAIL when the fact is missing**, never emit
an empty table: an empty table compiles, links, answers 0 for everything, and re-derives cleanly
against itself.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "an_arch_guarded_native_syscall_number_is_a_shadow_anywhere_not_just_in_the_peers"
description = "6.6.5. ESYSXLAT's shadow class (a compat row stealing a NATIVE aarch64 number) had three gates watching it, all reading the same two stdlib peer files. `lib/yukti.cyr` declared `SYS_STATFS = 43` under `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` and the `43 -> 202` socket row made every call `accept()`."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["aarch64", "syscalls", "gates", "vendored-stdlib", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
The class was found twice before (v6.5.36, v6.5.37) and each time the sweep that found it was
written against `lib/syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr`. But the defect is a property of an
**arch-guarded syscall NUMBER**, not of that file — and vendored sibling stdlibs declare their
own. Measured under `qemu-aarch64 -strace`: `yukti_filesystem_usage` issues
`accept(6293618, 0x7f254c000000, [0]) = -1 errno=14` and reports "statfs failed: errno 14" on
every ARM host. Older than the release that found it.

⭐ **WHY THE OTHER FOUR SYSCALL GATES COULD NOT SEE IT, and this is the generalisable part.**
`raw_syscall_literals_routed.sh` EXEMPTS arch-guarded literals **by construction** — writing the
native number under a guard is the supported spelling, so it cannot flag them. The other three
parse the two peer files. Every gate was doing its job; the union of their scopes had a hole
shaped exactly like a vendored fold.

**The discriminator has to come from outside the repo.** "Is this guarded value a row source?" is
not the question — the peers deliberately declare x86 numbers the chain renumbers
(`SYS_TRUNCATE = 76`, routed `76 -> 45`). The question is whether the row's DESTINATION really is
that call on aarch64, answered against a committed kernel table. That separates yukti's
`43 -> 202 = accept` (wrong) from the peers' `76 -> 45 = truncate` (right).

⚠ **A sweep whose real corpus is legitimately tiny needs a POSITIVE CONTROL, not a floor.** Six
guarded declarations exist across 658 in-tree files, so "at least N found" means nothing. The gate
runs its scanner over a fixed synthetic buffer every time and requires it to flag exactly the
known-bad lines. That control caught the first cut anchoring its regex at line start, which a
one-line `enum M { SYS_PPOLL = 73; }` walked straight through.

⛔ **CORRECTION (same release, review round 2): that sentence was half right and the half it got
wrong let the gate read green over an EMPTY WALK.** A positive control proves the scanner; it
says nothing about whether the corpus was visited at all. See
`a_gate_can_have_a_positive_control_and_still_read_green_over_an_empty_corpus` — the device that
actually closes it is re-deriving a KNOWN live hit a different way, with corpus floors behind it.
The same round added axis 3, because this axis's `SYS_*` regex could see neither consumer shape
the release names (`GWL_NR_FTRUNCATE = 46`, `n = 83;`).

⛔ **And do not fix it in the fold.** A patch to cyrius's vendored `lib/<dep>.cyr` evaporates at
the next `cyrius deps`. The gate names the live instance on every run instead, so it cannot go
quiet while the upstream fix is pending.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_tracked_cross_compiled_binary_no_gate_rebuilds_goes_stale_then_wrong"
description = "6.6.5. `build/cycc-native-aarch64` is committed, shipped into every install slot, and rebuilt by nothing — not install.sh, not the release gate, not CI. It was 60 releases stale, and a syscall renumber in the same release turned stale into actively wrong: `sys_unlink` traced as `fanotify_mark`."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["bootstrap", "aarch64", "release-gate", "install", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
The binary exists because ARM hardware self-host needs a compiler that already runs on ARM, so it
cannot be regenerated by the x86 build. `install.sh`'s case for it is `: ;` with a comment
claiming the binary stays "in lockstep"; the pi gate leg builds its compilers **fresh from
source**, so nothing in the gate ever touches the tracked one. Meanwhile
`install.sh --refresh-only` copies it into `versions/<v>/bin/` and `verify-store.sh --restore`
writes it from the tag. It had been 940,536 B from three months earlier against 1,505,480 B from
the tree.

⭐ **STALE IS TOLERABLE; DISAGREEING WITH THE STDLIB IS NOT.** The release moved the aarch64
peer's `SYS_UNLINKAT` from 35 to 263 and added the matching `263 -> 35` ESYSXLAT row. The old
emitter has no such row, so `sys_unlink` compiled against the new stdlib traced as
`fanotify_mark(-100, <ptr>, 0, 0)`. **Any release that touches `src/backend/aarch64`, the
ESYSXLAT chain or the syscall peers must regenerate it** — and it is deterministic and local,
no ARM hardware in the loop:

    ./build/cycc < src/main_aarch64.cyr > build/cycc_aarch64
    build/cycc_aarch64 < src/main_aarch64_native.cyr > build/cycc-native-aarch64

`qemu-aarch64` is enough to self-host the result to a byte-identical fixpoint as a local check
(an emulator, not the pi). ⚠ Generalise it: **a tracked artifact that no gate rebuilds has no
freshness signal at all** — its mtime is the only evidence, and nobody reads an mtime.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "an_x86_hosted_cross_compiler_takes_the_hosts_arch_ifdef_arm"
description = "6.6.5 review round 2. Changing code under `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` in cyrius's own source produced ZERO byte delta in all SEVEN forks, including `main_aarch64.cyr` — because those forks are x86-HOSTED cross compilers and take the x86 arm. Only `build/cycc-native-aarch64` moved, by exactly two bytes."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["bootstrap", "aarch64", "verification", "cross-compile", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`cat src/main_aarch64.cyr | ./build/cycc` produces an **x86 binary that EMITS aarch64**. The
arch macros come from the COMPILER doing the compiling, not from the fork's name — `build/cycc`
predefines `CYRIUS_ARCH_X86`, so every `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` inside the compiler's own
source is dead in all seven of those builds. The only build where it is live is the NATIVE ARM
compiler, produced by running `build/cycc-native-aarch64` on `src/main_aarch64_native.cyr`.

⛔ **THE TRAP IS THE REASSURANCE.** Deleting an `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` arm from
`src/backend/common/runtime.cyr` gave `ctl == new, IDENTICAL` for all seven forks. Read
naively that says "the change is a no-op, nothing to verify". It says the opposite: the
verification that matters had not run yet. Under qemu the native compiler differed in exactly
two bytes (`cmp -l` → offsets 144529/144530: `0x0E20` → `0x1C80`, i.e. the `movz` immediate
113 → 228) and self-hosted to a byte-identical fixpoint.

⭐ **The rule.** A compiler-source change under an ARCH guard is verified by building the
native compiler for that arch, not by the x86 fork sweep. Corollary: an x86-hosted fork-parity
check can never regress on an arch-guarded change, so it is not evidence about one.

The companion check is cheap and direct — compile a probe with the cross compiler and read the
kernel's own answer:

    cat probe.cyr | build/cycc_aarch64 > probe.a64 && qemu-aarch64 -strace ./probe.a64

which printed `clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, …) = 0` and settled that raw 228 reaches
`clock_gettime` on ELF-aarch64 through the `228 -> 113` ESYSXLAT row.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_gate_can_have_a_positive_control_and_still_read_green_over_an_empty_corpus"
description = "6.6.5 review round 2. A sweep gate had a synthetic positive control for its SCANNER and floors on its inputs (rows, peer declarations, kernel facts) — and still printed PASS and exited 0 when its tree walk matched ZERO files, taking the one live defect it names out with it."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["gates", "testing", "vacuity", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`aarch64_syscall_shadow.sh` axis 2 walks the tree for arch-guarded syscall declarations. It had
every anti-vacuity device the repo knows: a floor on decoded ESYSXLAT rows, a floor on parsed
peer declarations, a floor on kernel-table rows, and a **positive control** running the scanner
over a fixed synthetic buffer and requiring it to flag exactly the known-bad lines.

Measured: change the file-extension tuple so it matches nothing and the gate prints

    PASS: … axis 2: 0 arch-guarded declarations over 0 files, 0 known / 0 new

and exits 0. The `⚠ KNOWN LIVE DEFECT lib/yukti.cyr:58` line — the only thing keeping a real,
unfixed, cross-repo bug audible on every run — vanishes with it.

⭐ **WHY THE CONTROLS MISSED IT.** A positive control proves the SCANNER works. Floors on inputs
prove the INPUTS parsed. Neither says anything about the CORPUS in between. Three separate
guards, all green, over a walk that visited nothing.

⭐ **WHAT ACTUALLY CLOSES IT: re-derive a known live hit a DIFFERENT WAY.** The gate now opens
each entry of its KNOWN table by that entry's own path and regexes it directly. If the
declaration is still in the file, the walk MUST have reported it; a broken walk fails loudly
instead of going quiet. It needs no threshold, and it retires itself the day the upstream fix
lands and the declaration disappears. Corpus floors sit behind it as a second line, not as the
answer.

⚠ Generalise: **"my check has a positive control" is not the same claim as "my check ran over
the thing".** Ask separately what proves each of scanner, inputs, and corpus.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "assert_lte_zero_on_a_syscall_wrapper_accepts_every_failure"
description = "6.6.5 review round 2. `assert_lte(sys_sched_yield(), 0, …)` is true for success (0) AND for every negative errno, so the only assertion covering that wrapper could not fail: mis-declaring its syscall number still scored 50/50, exit 0."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["testing", "syscalls", "vacuity", "crossos", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
The wrapper returns 0 on success and `-errno` on failure, so a `<= 0` assertion is a tautology
over its entire range. Measured: set the aarch64 peer's `SYS_SCHED_YIELD` 124 → 300, rebuild the
aarch64 fork, run the cross-host test under qemu → **50 passed, 0 failed, exit 0**. The wrapper
was in the release's headline list of new names and had a line in the test with its name on it.

⭐ **The tell is the shape, not the subject.** A bound that a failure satisfies is not an
assertion. `assert_lte(x, 0)`, `assert_gte(x, 0)` on a value that is `-errno` or a fd,
`assert_ne(x, <a value it never takes>)` — all of them read like tests and cover nothing.

The fix is `assert_eq(…, 0)` per target: Linux and Darwin both return exactly 0 (Darwin through
the zero-timeout `poll` the wrapper composes), and PE declines with `-38` by design, which is a
separate `assert_eq` in the Windows group. Two exact assertions instead of one that spans both.
The same mutation then scores 51 passed / 1 failed, exit 1.

⚠ A per-target exact value is more work to write than one loose bound that "covers every
target" — and the loose bound is how a wrapper ships with no test at all.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "bench_min_over_windows_net_of_a_mean_floor"
description = "6.6.5. A MEAN clock cost subtracted from every window, whose MINIMUM is then reported, reads low by the clock's own jitter and reaches 0 for real work — and subtracting the minimum read cost instead does NOT fix it, because an empty window is not a lower bound on in-window clock cost."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["benchmarking", "statistics", "clock", "stdlib", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`lib/bench.cyr` calibrated the **mean** cost of one `now_ns()` read, subtracted that one scalar
from every timed window, clamped at 0, and fed the result into `min`. The minimum over windows
of `op + c_i - mean(c)` is `op - (mean(c) - min(c_i))`: low by the clock's window-to-window
spread, and **0** as soon as that spread reaches the op. Measured on an hpet box: `getpid` timed
one per window reported `min=0` in 3 of 3 runs while its mean (370/373/581 ns) and its 128-op
batched figure (262/309/320 ns) agreed. mabda filed it; cyrius's own `bench_tagged` and
`bench_float` were printing `min=0ns` on 5 of 14 rows at the time.

⛔ **The obvious repair is refuted by measurement.** "Subtract the MINIMUM single-read cost when
computing minima" re-nets the same raw windows to 70 / 0 / 0 ns against a true ~280. The reason
is in the data: the minimum raw `getpid` window (907 ns) came out EQUAL to the minimum EMPTY
window (907 ns) in 2 of 3 runs. Clock cost inside a window that does work is not drawn from the
same distribution as an empty window, so it dips below any empty-window minimum. **No scalar
floor rescues a per-window minimum.**

⭐ **The fix is RESOLUTION, not a better floor.** A window may claim a per-op extreme only if its
net duration is at least 100 x (one read + one TICK) — i.e. only when the instrument can be
wrong by at most 1 % of it. Short windows still count toward the total and the mean. When
nothing resolves, report the MEAN and expose a `resolved` flag; never 0, and never `n/a` if
consumers parse the field as a number.

Three corollaries that each cost their own bug:
  1. **Net at READ time, not per window.** A per-window clamp at 0 biases the MEAN upward (88 to
     3,584 of 20,000 windows clamped) and freezes rows against a later floor correction.
  2. **Error is floor PLUS tick.** They are different numbers and on three of five targets the
     tick is the larger one. Floor-only sizing sized for 0 on a host whose floor calibrates to
     0 against a 15 ms tick.
  3. **The adaptive path's PILOT and TAIL windows are short**, so through 6.6.4 they set the
     extremes for every `bench_run` row: `bench_run(noop, 1e5)` read `min=0` in 91 of 200 runs.
     Under the resolution rule they are excluded automatically, with no special case.

⚠ Generalise: **do not subtract a mean from a quantity whose minimum you then report.** If the
instrument's error is comparable to the measurement, the honest answer is the mean plus a flag,
not a smaller number.

⛔ **And do not answer a review finding by widening the promise.** The first cut of this fix
wrote "never 0 for real work" into four places. It is false: when a row's windows do not in
total outlast the clock reads that bracketed them, the netted total clamps at 0 and the mean —
and with it the unresolved min and max — goes to 0. Measured at `bench_run(noop, 16)`: min=0 in
69-317 of 500 reps. That case cannot be repaired by arithmetic (reporting the raw mean would
report the CLOCK as the op, which is the inflation the release before had removed), so the
answer is to NAME it: a `sub_floor` predicate, the words "below the instrument" in the report
line, and a closed-form test leg beside a control that must NOT be flagged. A measurement that
the instrument cannot make should say so in its own vocabulary; it should not be rounded into a
number that looks like the bug you just fixed.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "gate_a_statistics_defect_with_a_scripted_clock_not_a_measurement"
description = "6.6.5. Three attempts to gate a timer defect with live host-jitter statistics: one unsatisfiable by construction, one passing two runs in three, and one that measured the defect and declared it correct. A seam that makes the clock return scripted time gives closed-form expected values on every host."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["testing", "benchmarking", "gates", "vacuity", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`tests/tcyr/crossos/bench_timer_floor.tcyr` has carried three gate shapes for one subject:

  1. `bench_min_ns(b) * 2 < raw` where raw was a MIN over 200 no-op windows — unsatisfiable on
     any host whose tick exceeds a no-op (`0 < 0`), RED on ecb and ach on every run.
  2. A difference of totals across two separate executions — signal `N x floor`, noise the
     run-to-run spread of the same loop, and the noise was larger: 3 of 20 runs came out
     NEGATIVE on correct code.
  3. A one-sided `_btf_agree`, loosened until it stopped failing — and its own comment then
     recorded `min via bench_run : 0 0 3 22 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 (0 in TEN of twelve)` on real pi
     and concluded **"That 0 is not a defect."** Five weeks later a consumer filed exactly that
     number.

⭐ **A statistic whose noise exceeds its signal cannot be rescued by moving the threshold** —
loosen it until it stops failing and it has stopped discriminating. Each repair made the gate
looser, and the third repair wrote the defect down as correct behaviour, which is worse than no
gate: it is a standing instruction not to look again.

⭐ **The shape that works: a seam.** `var _bench_clock_fp = 0;` checked first in `now_ns()`, so a
test hands the library scripted virtual time — a stated per-read cost, jitter, op and tick — and
every expected value is CLOSED FORM from those four numbers. Identical on ecb, ach, cass, pi and
x86 Linux, at any load, in 0.1 s. Nine mutants (min over all windows, per-window clamp,
integer-ns per op, no recheck, recheck adopting a higher floor, one calibration round, error
without the tick, truncating round, unresolved min of 0) each turn it RED; the same nine ran
GREEN against the three live shapes above.

⚠ **Uninstalling the seam is more than clearing it.** Dropping `_bench_clock_fp` leaves the
CALIBRATION CACHE holding the script's numbers — the 15 ms axis leaves a floor of 0 and a 15 ms
tick — and every live axis after it then measures the host against those. Measured: two live
axes went RED on a correct library the first time the scripted axes were added. Force a real
recalibration and ASSERT it happened.

Keep live assertions, but one-sided and property-shaped ("a real op never reports a zero
minimum"), never as the primary gate.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_stdlib_module_that_calls_a_wrapper_it_does_not_include_stops_being_self_sufficient"
description = "6.6.5. Routing lib/chrono.cyr's and lib/bench.cyr's Windows clock through lib/syscalls_windows.cyr's sys_qpc_ns() broke every PE consumer that declared only \"chrono\" — and no in-repo test could see it, because every in-repo consumer pulls lib/syscalls.cyr."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["stdlib", "self-sufficiency", "windows", "pe", "deps", "vacuity", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`lib/chrono.cyr` includes only `atomic.cyr`; `lib/bench.cyr` includes NOTHING. Every platform
arm in both files is a bare `syscall(...)` builtin, which is what makes them stand alone. The
6.6.5 Windows-clock repair replaced `syscall(228)` with a call to `sys_qpc_ns()`, defined two
files away in `lib/syscalls_windows.cyr` — and a consumer whose `[deps] stdlib` lists
`"chrono"` without `"syscalls"` stopped cross-building for Windows:

    undefined function 'sys_qpc_ns'

⛔ **The defect is invisible in this repo by construction.** Every in-repo consumer includes
`lib/syscalls.cyr`, so the whole corpus, the whole gate set and the whole cross-OS leg build
fine. It only appears one repo over, at the pin, in the shape nobody here compiles.

⛔ **And the vendoring closure cannot rescue it.** `_distlib_union_declared_stdlib`
(`cbt/commands.cyr`) derives `dist/<lib>.deps` from the declared `[deps] stdlib` plus literal
`include "lib/X.cyr"` lines in the bundled sources. Neither file has such a line for syscalls,
so there is no chrono→syscalls edge to derive and nothing adds one.

The fix is to spell the route raw the way the module's other arms already do —
`syscall(61496, &c)` / `syscall(61497, &c)` in a private `_chrono_qpc_ns` / `_bench_qpc_ns` —
not to add an include. (PE reroute numbers ≥ 0xF000 are exempt from
`raw_syscall_literals_routed`, which judges arch-neutral ELF numbers.)

⭐ **The gate lesson is separate and sharper.** The first anti-vacuous check for the new gate
axis read the PE import table: "the binary must import QueryPerformanceCounter/Frequency".
It passed over a mutant whose Windows arm had been reverted to GetTickCount64 outright —
because the now-unreachable raw helper STAYS IN THE BINARY and keeps its imports. An
import-table assertion measures what was LINKED, not what is REACHED. The check that kills
that mutant derives the arm's callee from the source and requires it to be defined in the same
file; the import check is kept only as the second axis.

Rule of thumb: when a module's header says "Requires: X" but the module compiles standalone
today, that header is a wish, not a constraint — the constraint is whether a consumer that
declares only this module can still build, on every target. Cross-build a single-module
consumer in the gate; it is one `cycc` invocation.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_check_that_reads_the_thing_it_guards_is_not_a_check_git_diff_index"
description = "6.6.5 / CVE-43. cyrius deps verified a dep cache with `git diff-index --quiet HEAD`, which answers from that cache's own index and config. It refused 138 of 138 untouched checkouts after one `cp -a`, and accepted seven shapes of real tampering. The fix hashes the tree into a private throwaway index."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["deps", "security", "git", "cve", "supply-chain", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`cbt/deps.cyr` proved a cached dep checkout matched its tag with:

    git -C <cache> diff-index --quiet HEAD

Every input to that answer lives **inside the cache being judged**: the index's cached stat
data, its `assume-unchanged` / `skip-worktree` bits, and `core.fileMode` / `core.trustctime` /
`core.checkStat` / `core.fsmonitor` / `refs/replace`. Anything that can write the cache can
write the evidence.

⛔ **It was wrong in both directions at once, which is why one half hid the other.**

  * **False refusal** (what got filed, by a consumer): `diff-index` never refreshes the stat
    cache, so `touch`, `cp -a`, or a `git status` run under `unshare -r` makes every entry
    mismatch. One `cp -a` of `~/.cyrius/deps` refuses **138 of 138** real checkouts. It looks
    intermittent because any porcelain command (`git status`, `git diff`) rewrites the index
    and silently "fixes" it — the first repro attempt hid the bug that way.
  * **Silent accept** (nobody filed it, because nothing surfaces): an in-place edit with the
    mtime restored, `assume-unchanged`, `skip-worktree`, a local commit with no lock,
    `refs/replace`, a missing `.git`, and a `core.fsmonitor` hook all vendored the tampered
    bytes at exit 0. Untracked files are invisible to `diff-index` entirely.

⭐ **The rule.** A check must compute its answer from something the subject cannot write. The
fix keeps using git, but builds the evidence itself: `read-tree HEAD` into a **private
throwaway index** (a fresh index holds no stat data, so `update-index --refresh` HASHES every
file), then `diff-files`, `ls-files -o`, and a populated-gitlink scan. The cache supplies
objects; it no longer supplies the verdict.

Five git facts this cost, each measured rather than read:

  1. **`--connectivity-only` does not catch a forged object.** `read-tree` never verifies that
     an object hashes to the name it is stored under, so a forged subtree spliced in under the
     original tree's name is read back whole and `diff-files` then agrees with the planted
     bytes. `fsck --connectivity-only` exits **0** on that repo. It appeared to catch it only
     because the cache's own `.git/index` carried a cache-tree naming the mangled object —
     delete the index (a state that is otherwise perfectly benign) and the detection vanishes.
     Only a FULL `git fsck` hashes objects: 11 ms on a typical dep, 70 ms on a 392-object one.
  2. **An explicit `--git-dir` SKIPS git's `safe.directory` ownership check.** Naming the
     repository to stop discovery therefore turns OFF a security check. Use
     `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES=<parent>` instead: discovery stays on, ownership is still
     enforced, and it cannot climb out of the cache.
  3. **Discovery keeps CLIMBING past a `.git` that exists but is invalid.** A gutted `.git`
     directory is not "not a repo, stop" — git walks up to the next candidate. With
     `CYRIUS_HOME` inside a git workspace that means an ENCLOSING repo answers `rev-parse
     HEAD`, and its commit gets written into `cyrius.lock` as the dependency's pin.
  4. **Git exports `GIT_INDEX_FILE` into every hook** (an absolute path to `.git/index.lock`),
     and `GIT_DIR` too in a linked worktree. A tool that forwards its environment to `git`
     children and is run from a pre-commit hook will operate on the USER'S commit index. Here
     the cold-cache `git clone` rewrote it: the hook reported success and the user's
     `git commit` died with `invalid object … Error building trees`. The scrub list is
     `git rev-parse --local-env-vars` (plus `GIT_NAMESPACE`) — and `GIT_CONFIG*` is
     deliberately NOT on it, because CI injects credentials that way and a command-line `-c`
     outranks it.
  5. **`core.fsmonitor` is a program git RUNS.** Reading a config value out of an untrusted
     directory can be code execution, not just a wrong answer. Measured: two executions per
     status check, zero with `-c core.fsmonitor=false`.

⚠ And the shared-cache constraint ruled out both remedies the filing proposed: `update-index
--refresh` and `git diff --quiet HEAD` each WRITE the cache's index, and `scripts/check.sh`
aliases the live `~/.cyrius/deps` into its staged home — the "fix" would have corrupted the
maintainer's cache on every suite run. Verification must be able to run against a directory it
may not touch. Proof is a filesystem snapshot (`find .git -printf '%p %T@ %s'`) before and
after, not a git query.

Gate: `tests/gates/toolchain/deps_git_cache_verified.sh` (65 axes, 34 mutants — DERIVE those
from the file, they grew twice during review). See
`docs/audit/2026-09-03-security-audit.md` CVE-43.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "two_axes_that_exist_because_the_ledger_could_not_prove_their_check"
description = "6.6.5. A 39-axis gate looked complete until the mutation ledger ran: two of the guarantees it claimed — GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES and a FULL fsck — reddened NOTHING when removed. An unprovable check is either dead weight or an untested one."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["gates", "mutation-testing", "vacuity", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`deps_git_cache_verified.sh` shipped its first green run with 39 axes covering, among other
things, a refusal caused by a forged git object and a refusal caused by a cache inside an
enclosing repository. Both passed. Then the ledger ran, and two mutants reddened **no axis at
all**:

  * removing `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES` — because the axis that was supposed to need it
    (`.git` deleted) is stopped one step earlier by the `.git`-is-a-real-directory check;
  * downgrading the full `fsck` to `--connectivity-only` — because the forgery axis left the
    cache's own index in place, and that index's cache-tree names the mangled object, so
    connectivity-only exits 8 for a reason that has nothing to do with the check.

Two guarantees in the shipped code, neither one measured. The remedy is not to delete the
mutants: it is the two axes each mutant needed in order to be provable.

  * **R19** — a `.git` that EXISTS but is gutted (git's discovery keeps climbing past an
    invalid one), inside a git-repo home. Only the ceiling stops that.
  * **R20** — the same forgery with the cache's index removed, a state another (benign) axis
    already proves is normal. Only a full fsck stops that.

⭐ **The ledger is not a report card you write after the gate is done; it is the last design
step.** A mutant that reds nothing is telling you either that the code is dead or that your
fixture is proving the right answer for the wrong reason — and both of those get written down
as "verified" if the ledger is skipped. Run the ledger, and treat every NO-AXIS-RED row as a
missing axis until proven otherwise.

**UPDATE (same release, review round 2): four such axes, and one deliberate exception.** Two
more checks turned out to be unprovable as first written — `.git/info/attributes` (the other
laundering axes also carried a config key and refused on that) and a `core.worktree` hidden in
`.git/config.worktree` (a `--local` config scan cannot see that file at all). Both got an axis.
The exception is worth the precedent: dropping the `GIT_WORK_TREE` the same release added reds
NOTHING, because every work-tree redirect we could build is ALSO refused by the config scan. It
is kept anyway, with the ledger line saying so in the gate header, because it is the layer that
does not depend on having enumerated the right config key — which is precisely the assumption
the previous cut got wrong. "Treat NO-AXIS-RED as a missing axis **until proven otherwise**" has
a second answer besides "write the axis": two layers that genuinely overlap, where the redundant
one is the one that survives a wrong assumption. Say which it is in the ledger, or someone will
delete it on the strength of a green line.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "overriding_an_untrusted_config_with_dash_c_is_not_enough"
description = "6.6.5 / CVE-43, the SECOND cut. Having moved the dep-cache verification into a private index, the fix pinned five `-c` values on the cache's own git config and considered the config handled. Review then found eight more shapes that still vendored tampered bytes at exit 0 — every one of them a knob `-c` cannot reach."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["deps", "security", "git", "config", "cve", "supply-chain", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
The private-index rewrite fixed WHERE the evidence came from. It still let the subject vote,
through its own `.git/config`, because the code overrode a fixed list of keys:

    -c core.fsmonitor=false -c core.hooksPath=/dev/null -c core.fileMode=true
    -c core.symlinks=true  -c core.untrackedCache=false

A fixed list can only override keys whose NAMES you know. Measured against that build, each of
these resolved at **exit 0 with the tampered bytes vendored**:

  1. **`core.worktree`** — points every content command at a different directory. Park a
     pristine copy beside the tampered cache, and `diff-files` compares the copy while the
     resolver vendors from the cache. The code unset `GIT_WORK_TREE` and never SET it, so
     discovery took the work tree from this key.
  2. **`filter.<name>.clean`** — a program git RUNS over the working tree while hashing it, and
     whose output is what the comparison sees. A driver printing the honest bytes made an
     edited file compare equal. ⛔ `-c` cannot neutralise this one **in principle**: the
     driver name is chosen by the attacker, so there is no fixed key to override. It also ran
     twice per resolve — the untrusted cache choosing a binary for us to execute, the same
     class as the `core.fsmonitor` hook the first cut DID gate.
  3. **the same filter behind `include.path`** — git follows includes when it reads the config,
     but `git config --local --name-only --list` does **not** print what they pull in (measured:
     the listing showed only `include.path`, and the included filter laundered the edit anyway).
     A scan that greps the listing for `filter.` misses it.
  4. **`.git/info/attributes` alone** — no filter needed: `* text eol=crlf` makes git convert
     while hashing, so a CRLF-only edit compares equal.
  5. **`core.autocrlf` in the cache's config** — same laundering, and it CANNOT be forced off:
     a user whose GLOBAL autocrlf is on has a legitimately CRLF working tree, so forcing `lf`
     would refuse an honest cache.
  6. **`extensions.worktreeConfig` + `.git/config.worktree`** — a SECOND config file whose keys
     `--local` does not list either. Anything from 1-5 can hide there.
  7. **a `--depth 1` clone of a DIFFERENT repository carrying the same tag** — the cache
     directory is keyed on dep NAME and TAG only, so it is simply reused, and the foreign
     commit is written into `cyrius.lock`.

⭐ **The rule: partition the knobs.** What the command line CAN override, override. What it
cannot, REFUSE by name — and refuse it *before* running the command that would honour it,
because by the time a filter has laundered the bytes it has also already executed. Refusing
needs a cost check, not a guess: all 138 live checkouts carry exactly the six keys `git clone`
writes (`core.repositoryformatversion`, `core.filemode`, `core.bare`, `core.logallrefupdates`,
`remote.origin.url`, `remote.origin.fetch`), so the deny list costs nothing.

⚠ **An allowlist is the tempting inversion and it is wrong here**: git writes platform-dependent
extras at clone time (`core.ignorecase`, `core.precomposeunicode`, `core.symlinks` on Windows),
so an allowlist derived on Linux false-refuses on macOS — which is the very defect the release
was fixing. Useful fact for either shape: `git config --name-only --list` lowercases the section
and variable halves of a key (`core.workTree` prints `core.worktree`), so prefix matching on
lowercase is exact; only the subsection keeps its case.

⚠ **And narrow the claim afterwards.** Even with all of that, the check proves the checkout is
internally consistent with the tag it CLAIMS — not that the objects came from the declared
remote. Commit the tamper locally, `git tag -f` onto it, and nothing offline can tell (measured).
The lock file's commit pin is the real bound, and it is trust-on-first-use. The audit entry, the
guide and the gate comments all said "proves the checkout IS the tag"; all three were corrected.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "hardening_that_forces_a_setting_must_ask_what_the_tool_was_telling_it"
description = "6.6.5. Two ways the dep-cache hardening could itself have bricked a legitimate dependency permanently, both found in review: reading `git fsck`'s POLICY complaints as integrity failures, and forcing `core.fileMode=true` over git's own filesystem probe. Both are the same mistake — overriding a tool's answer without asking what the answer meant."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["deps", "git", "false-refusal", "portability", "gates", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
A check that refuses is only as good as its ability to refuse the right thing. Two instances,
one release, same shape:

**1. `git fsck` exits 1 for things that are not corruption.** Measured on git 2.55, all on an
otherwise perfect repository: `missingEmail`, `badEmail`, `missingSpaceBeforeEmail`,
`missingSpaceBeforeDate`, `badDate`, `zeroPaddedDate`, `missingNameBeforeEmail`,
`missingAuthor`. A dependency whose tag commit predates modern git hygiene would have been
refused as "object-store damage" **for ever** — and the recovery the message printed
(`rm -rf <cache>`) re-clones the same objects and refuses again. Fix: pass those ids as
`fsck.<id>=warn`; leave everything that bears on whether an object hashes to the name it is
stored under as an error. ⚠ An id a git version does NOT know is `fatal: Unhandled message id`,
exit **128** — which would brick every resolve on that git — so the run falls back to a
policy-free fsck on 128. (`fsck.missingName` is exactly such an id on 2.55. The list has to be
probed, not copied out of documentation.)

**2. Forcing `core.fileMode=true` overrides git's own filesystem probe.** On vfat / exfat /
NTFS-3g / 9p — a USB stick, a WSL `/mnt/c`, a VM share — `git clone` writes
`core.filemode=false` because the exec bit cannot be stored. Forcing `true` made every 755 entry
in the tag read as mode-changed, so the dep refused permanently and `reset --hard` could not
repair it: it cannot set a bit the filesystem has no room for. 104 of 138 live checkouts here
carry a 755 file.

⭐ **The fix is not "trust the config" — it is to ASK THE SAME QUESTION YOURSELF.** Probe the
filesystem the way git probes it (create a 0600 file, `chmod` it 0700, `stat` it) and force the
probed answer. That keeps the security half — an attacker's `core.fileMode=false` is still
overridden on a filesystem that CAN store the bit — while a mode-only difference stops bricking
the dep on one that cannot. ⚠ Use `stat`, not `access(X_OK)`: they disagree on a `noexec` mount,
where the bit IS stored, and `stat` is what git itself asks.

⚠ **The probe's own first cut then walked into the neighbouring trap**: it read `st_mode` at a
hand-written offset, which is byte 24 on x86-Linux, **16** on aarch64-Linux and **4** on
macOS-arm64's stat64 — the exact thing `lib/io.cyr`'s `is_dir` carries a warning about, after it
once reported every directory as not-a-directory on ARM. Measured under `qemu-aarch64` (not
hardware): with the hand-written 24 the ARM build read st_uid and **accepted** a mode tamper,
and through `xstat` + `STAT_MODE` it refuses. If the stdlib has a wrapper for a struct field,
using it is not style — the offsets differ per ABI and the host suite cannot see the difference.

⚠ Both of these are refusals that only fire on other people's machines, so the gate has to reach
for them deliberately: a tag commit built with `hash-object --literally` for the fsck policy, and
— since a vfat mount needs root — driving the probe-impossible path by making the probe's own
directory read-only, then asserting BOTH halves (a mode-only difference is accepted, content
tampering in the same state is still refused).
"""

[[entries]]
name = "one_identifier_two_legal_spellings_is_not_a_string_compare"
description = "6.6.5 / CVE-43, review round 2. The dep-cache check compared `remote.origin.url` against the manifest with a plain string equality. `…/x` and `…/x.git` are ONE repository on every forge, so it refused 19 live declarations across 11 repos — and the refusal had no fixed point."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["deps", "git", "false-refusal", "url", "cve", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
The hardening added "the cache must CLAIM to come from the url the manifest declares", because
the cache directory is keyed on dep NAME and TAG only and a foreign checkout parked there was
silently reused. Correct. The comparison was `_cstr_eq(cache_origin, declared_url)`.

A forge serves `https://host/o/x`, `https://host/o/x.git` and `https://host/o/x/` as the SAME
repository, and both spellings are in normal use. Measured across the 126 `cyrius.cyml`
manifests on this box against the 138 live caches: **19 declarations in 11 repos** differ from
their cache by exactly that suffix — one dep+tag (tyche 1.0.1) is declared both ways by eight
different consumers.

⛔ **The shape that makes it worse than a one-off refusal: there is no fixed point.** The message
says `rm -rf <cache>`; do that and the next consumer re-clones with ITS spelling, and now the
first project refuses. Two consumers alternate for ever. A false refusal that the advertised
recovery converts into a different false refusal is not a bug you can tell a user to work around.

⛔ **And the SAME comparison sat one function over, where the failure mode is worse.** The
CVE-21 commit pin is looked up by `(name, url, tag)` from `cyrius.lock`; an exact compare there
does not REFUSE, it silently TOFU-RE-PINS — respell the url in your manifest and the
moved-tag check the pin exists for is skipped for that resolve. The first fix normalised only
the cache's origin, because that is where the reviewer had measured it. ⭐ When you fix a
comparison, grep for the SHAPE (`_cstr_eq(<a url>, …)`), not the site.

⭐ **Rule: an identity check between two things written by different people needs the identity
relation, not `==`.** Normalise what the domain says is insignificant (here: one trailing `/`,
then one trailing `.git`), then compare — and prove the check still refuses a genuinely
different url, or normalisation has just deleted the check.

⚠ **And the CLAIM about it shared the blind spot.** The release note said "every origin url
matches what the manifests declare", because it had been checked against the CACHES (138 of
them) rather than against every DECLARING manifest (210 rows). The subject and the verifier were
the same population — the v6.6.2 lesson (`a check that shares a defect with the thing it checks
reads GREEN`) applied to a sentence in a CHANGELOG. Compute the claim from the other side.

⚠ Related, unfixed and now written down instead: two consumers on one box declaring the same dep
NAME+TAG from genuinely DIFFERENT urls (a fork) still alternate refusals, because the cache path
carries no url component. The refusal is correct in each direction; the recovery fixes one
project by breaking the other. Keying the directory on a hash of the url is the fix when someone
needs it.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "could_not_measure_is_not_measured_false"
description = "6.6.5 / CVE-43, review round 2. Three fail-opens and one hard-fail in one release, all the same shape: a check that could not ASK its question treated the failure to ask as an answer. An unwritable directory read as 'this filesystem cannot store the exec bit'; a fork that does not exist on Windows read as 'git says your cache is broken'."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["deps", "security", "windows", "probe", "fail-open", "cve", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
The dep-cache hardening probes its environment in several places. Each probe has three possible
outcomes and the code only had two.

**1. The exec-bit probe.** To decide whether `core.fileMode` comparison is meaningful, it writes
a 0600 file beside the cache, chmods it 0700 and stats it. When the file could not be CREATED it
returned *not storable* — i.e. "this filesystem has no exec bit" — and memoised that per PROCESS
from the first dep's parent directory. So a read-only `~/.cyrius/deps/<name>` silently disabled
mode comparison for every dep in the run: measured, a `chmod -x` tamper accepted at rc 0, no
warning, no attacker control of the cache config needed. Fix: a third answer, UNKNOWN, which
forces nothing and falls back to the value git's own clone-time probe wrote into that cache —
the other measurement of the same filesystem — and a memo keyed on the directory probed.

**2. `sys_fork` on Windows.** `lib/syscalls_windows.cyr` defines it as `return 0 - 1`. The
verification runs git through `fork`+`execve`, so on PE **every** git command returns -1. The
release had just closed a fail-open by making "no HEAD" a refusal — which on Windows turned into
a hard refusal of a pre-populated cache (the only way a git dep can exist there), naming a reason
that is false ("its .git is missing") and a recovery that cannot run. Measured under wine: rc 1
with the module missing, where 6.6.4 vendored it. Fix: ASK the platform (`_git_exec_available`,
one `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN`), warn once per dep that the cache was not verified, and stay out
of the check — rather than inferring a verdict about the cache from the failure of the tool.

⭐ **The rule: every probe has three outcomes — yes, no, and I-could-not-ask.** Collapsing the
third into "no" is a fail-open when the check is a security check, and collapsing it into "yes"
is a permanent false refusal. Name the third state and decide it deliberately; the right
fallback is usually another measurement of the same thing (here: what git itself recorded), not
a guess.

⚠ The gate axis for the first one had encoded the WRONG semantics and passed: it asserted that
an unprobeable directory accepts a mode difference. It now asserts both halves against the
cache's own recorded value — `false` (a vfat clone) accepts, `true` (this box) refuses — which
is a property, not a behaviour the implementation happens to have.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "an_argument_parser_per_verb_is_a_defect_per_verb"
description = "6.6.5. A filing about ONE flag on ONE verb (`cyrius lint --strict-deferrals`) was ~60 spellings across every verb and every delegated tool, because each branch hand-rolled its own argument loop. Several were SILENT AND MUTATING: a typo'd flag rewrote a file, deleted build/, or ran a real resolve, all at exit 0."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cli", "cbt", "arguments", "fail-open", "dry-run", "windows", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
mabda filed two rows against `cyrius lint`: the flag-first spelling handed cyrlint the FLAG as
its file (usage text, exit 1), and the flag-last spelling DROPPED the flag and exited 0 with the
gate off. The second is the one that costs you — it prints the same notes as a plain lint and
nothing says the flag was ignored, so three untracked deferrals shipped in three mabda releases
behind a green CI step.

**It was never a lint bug.** ~35 verb branches in `cbt/cyrius.cyr` each had their own loop, and
no two agreed. The shape has four faces:

1. an undeclared `-` token becomes the OPERAND (`lint --strict-deferrals f` opens
   "--strict-deferrals"; `build s --strict` writes the binary to a file NAMED `--strict`);
2. a flag after the operand is dropped (`fmt f --chekc` REWRITES the file, exit 0);
3. extra operands are dropped (`lint a b` lints `a` and reports its verdict for the pair —
   ranga's CI linted 1 of 41 files that way);
4. the branch indexes from a literal `argv(2)` rather than `cmd_idx + 1`, so a global `-q`/`-v`
   shifts every operand (`cyrius -q clean --dry-run` DELETED `build/`).

⭐ **The severity is not in the flag, it is in what the verb does when it proceeds anyway.**
Measured on 6.6.4: `clean --dryrun` deleted; `lib sync --dry` synced 110 files; `deps --dry-run`
and `deps --verfy` really resolved; `pulsar --dry-run` did the real rebuild-and-install. Sorting
the ~60 rows by SILENT vs LOUD and by MUTATING vs not is what turns "low severity, there is a
working form" into the actual picture.

⭐ **`--dry-run` was set too late to mean anything.** It was assigned inside the build loop,
which runs AFTER `_auto_deps`, so a dry-run build in a `[deps]` project printed "dry-run:
compile" having already created `lib/`. And the deps branch never set it at all — which means
`cbt/deps.cyr`'s dry-run early return had existed for releases and had **never once been
reached from the CLI**. A branch no caller can reach is not a feature; grep for the flag that
selects it before believing it works.

⭐ **A cap in the plumbing silently shapes the caller.** `run_tool` forwarded at most 3
arguments, so `cmd_lint` was *written* to forward only `--strict` and the file: three flags plus
a path is four. The caller looked like a policy decision and was an arity limit. Same shape one
level down in `lib/flags.cyr`, whose positional array stopped at 128 and returned SUCCESS —
kriya re-implemented operand counting by hand (`src/lib/args.cyr:781`) rather than being told.

⚠ **`run_tool` used fork/waitpid, which are -1 STUBS on PE.** `cyrius lint/fmt/doc/vet/deny/
api-surface` therefore never spawned their tool on Windows at all; under wine the PE CLI did not
return in 15 s. The Windows path is `_win_create_process`/`_win_wait_close` with each argument
quoted (a PE command line is ONE string). If a CLI feature delegates to a helper binary, check
how it spawns before assuming the feature exists on every target.

⚠ **`var argv[8]` is 8 BYTES, one slot.** `run_cx` stored its NUL terminator at `+8`, which
landed on the next local's stack slot (a probe measured `&argv + 8 == &fd`). It had worked by
luck since the cx arc.

**The fix is one classifier, not 35 repairs** (`cbt/cli_args.cyr`): flags in any position, an
undeclared `-` token is an error that NAMES it, operand counts enforced, indices from
`cmd_idx + 1`, effects applied before `_auto_deps`, and `--help` printed from the SAME spec
tables the parser reads so the two cannot drift. Adding a flag to a table registers it with both.

⭐ **What the round-2 review found in the FIX — the same family, one level out:**

* **A wrapper that re-implements its tool's semantics will disagree with it.** The first cut
  spawned cyrlint once per file and took the MAX exit; cyrlint itself SUMS. On three
  100-warning files `cyrlint a b c --exit-with-count` exited 255 and `cyrius lint a b c
  --exit-with-count` exited 100. The fix is not a second summing loop — it is ONE invocation
  over every file, so the tool's semantics are the only semantics.
* **When the exit code IS the answer (a count), a hard failure needs its own carrier.** The new
  multi-file tail returned the summed count and threw away `rc`, the only variable holding
  "could not read file 2" — so `cyrlint good.cyr missing.cyr --exit-with-count` printed the
  read error and exited **0**. A fail-open written by the fix for a fail-open. `_lint_hard`
  now records it separately.
* **A per-target arm applied to one binary out of nine.** `_ccname`/`_sn` had resolved
  `cycc.exe` on Windows since v6.0.85; every OTHER tool resolved as `make_path(_tools_dir,
  "cyrlint")` with no suffix, and `run_tool_vec` refuses on `file_exists` before CreateProcess —
  so on the shipped Windows layout every delegated verb said `tool not found`. The new spawn
  worked (measured under wine by copying `cyrlint.exe` to `cyrlint`) and was unreachable.
  `_tool_path` now carries the `.exe` probe for all of them, and the gate fails on any tool
  lookup that bypasses it. Two of the tools were not even in the Windows tarball.
* **Forwarding arguments to something that ignores them is a drop.** `cyrius run p.cyx a b c`
  handed `a b c` to cxvm, which reads nothing but the program on stdin (cx has no guest argv
  ABI), and exited with the guest's code. Rejected by name now.
* **The fix's own new integer parser wrapped.** `_cli_int` existed to stop `atoi` coercing
  garbage and accumulated past 63 bits: `soak 99999999999999999999` became
  7766279631452241919, passed the `> 0` guard and never terminated.
* **Sweep every binary with a dispatcher, not the one in the filing.** `cyriusly` (a shipped
  `[release].bins` entry) had the filed shape verbatim — `cyriusly use 6.6.4 --globl` DROPPED
  the typo and took the LOCAL-pin path, exit 0 — and `cyriusly install --dry-run` installed a
  version literally named `--dry-run`.

⭐ **Round 3 found the family again, in three new places:**

* **A count-as-exit mode must still carry the verdicts.** `cyrlint --exit-with-count` returned
  the warning count BEFORE the strict checks ran, so `--exit-with-count --strict-deferrals` on a
  file with 0 warnings and an untracked deferral exited **0** — the filed flag, finally
  forwarded, switched off by its neighbour. The exit code is now max(count, 2). When one
  channel carries two answers, decide which wins before the first early return.
* **"Last one wins" is a drop.** The classifier's value accessor kept the last occurrence, so
  `--features a --features b` resolved `b` alone while `--features a,b` resolved both, and
  `--target=js --target=cx` silently meant cx — the FLAG_LIST defect fixed in `lib/flags.cyr`
  the same week, rebuilt one directory over. Single-valued repeats are refused by name now;
  `-D` and `--features` accumulate. Same shape in `cyrius-init` (`init alpha beta` scaffolded
  beta alone), `ts_test_runner` (last path walked), `cyrld` (a second `-o` replaced the first)
  and `cyrius_api_surface` (`--snapshot` twice).
* **A dry run must fail where the real run fails.** `deps --dry-run`, reachable for the first
  time, returned before the manifest check: in an empty directory it said "would resolve deps
  from cyrius.cyml" and exited 0 while `deps` exited 1.
* **Windows, three layers deep.** The spawn fix made `lint` reach its tool, and then the
  shipped layout (cycc.exe present) failed CLOSED: the private temp dir was a literal `"/tmp"`
  plus a raw `syscall(39)`, which is not a PE reroute and returns -38 on every call, so every
  run raced for `/tmp/cyrius--38[-N]`. Under the temp dir sat `run_binary_timed`, still
  fork/waitpid, so `run/test/tests/bench/fuzz` never ran the program on Windows and decoded an
  UNINITIALISED status (`run argc a b c` → 111). And a CreateProcessW with no application name
  appends `.exe` to an extensionless program, so the temp binary had to be named `run.exe`.
  Measure a Windows fix on the SHIPPED layout — the round-2 claim was measured with cycc.exe
  absent, which is the one layout where the pre-pass never needs a temp dir.
* **Plan change, recorded:** the fix plan said the delegated tools would move onto
  `lib/flags.cyr`. They got in-place loops instead, and the loops had already drifted (cyrsign
  printed `unknown option` without the token). What is enforced is the RULE, by one gate over
  every binary — not which parser implements it; `lib/flags.cyr`'s header had claimed those
  tools used it and was corrected.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "gate_a_cli_with_a_hash_the_shell_computes"
description = "6.6.5. How to gate argument handling without the gate sharing the defect: a tree hash the SHELL computes, a second oracle from a different binary, and the defect in the LAST operand so a fix that only handles operand 0 cannot pass."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["gates", "cli", "anti-vacuous", "mutation", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
`tests/gates/toolchain/cli_args_never_dropped.sh`, 579 assertions after the round-3 review (478
after round 2, 310 in the first cut). The techniques are reusable for any CLI-shaped gate:

⛔ **Round 3 found this gate passing on the 6.6.4 CLI in three ways — each worth a rule:**

* **Grep for the ERROR CLASS, not the token.** Axis 2 checked that stderr contained
  `--zz-cli-probe`. On 6.6.4 every verb whose operand is a path printed `no such file:
  --zz-cli-probe` — the flag-taken-as-a-file defect itself — and the row read green. It now greps
  `unknown option '--zz-cli-probe'`.
* **A refusal row is vacuous on a fixture where the real operation fails anyway.** `deps
  --verfy` in a dir with no manifest, `fuzz --poisn` with no harness, `pulsar --dry-run` outside
  a cyrius tree: 6.6.4 ran all of them for real and they failed for unrelated reasons, rc != 0,
  green. Each typo row now runs where the real operation SUCCEEDS, proven by an anti-vacuous
  twin without the typo, and hashes CYRIUS_HOME as well as the working dir (pulsar/lsp/update
  write the store, not the project).
* **A throwaway CYRIUS_HOME is not hermetic if the compiler reads $HOME.** cycc's include
  fallback reads `$HOME/.cyrius/versions/<ver>/lib` and ignores CYRIUS_HOME, so five fixture
  rows compiled against the LIVE store; with an empty HOME they went red. The gate now points
  HOME at a throwaway whose `.cyrius` is the staged home.
* **A Windows leg under wine needs its own prefix.** wine overrides `TEMP` from the prefix
  registry (a `TEMP=` in the environment does not reach the process), so every PE run that makes
  a temp dir writes into `~/.wine`. The leg runs under `WINEPREFIX=$T/wine` (winemenubuilder,
  mono and gecko disabled), then `wineserver -k` and removes its `/tmp/.wine-<uid>/server-…`
  socket dir. It SKIPs, loudly, when wine is absent; cass runs the same rows on hardware.

**Never ask the tool what it did.** Side effects are measured with
`{ find . ; find . -type f | xargs sha256sum ; } | sha256sum`, taken before and after each
probe. ⚠ A `find . -type f` alone is NOT enough: it misses `cyrius deps`' symlinks and a bare
`mkdir lib`. That exact blind spot hid a real defect during development — `lib sync --dry-run`
mkdir'd `./lib` and the hash did not move, because an empty directory has no files in it. The
NAME list is what caught it.

**Two oracles for the filed row.** Axis 1 asserts exit 2 both against the literal and against
the rc of `cyrlint --strict-deferrals` run directly — a different parser in a different binary.
And the `--exit-with-count` expectation is parsed out of STDOUT's `<n> warnings` line, a
different channel from the exit code it predicts.

**Put the defect in the LAST operand.** Axis 4 lints/formats N files where only the last is
bad. With the defect in the first file, a mutant that iterates only operand 0 passes every row —
which is precisely the mutation (M3) this arrangement exists to catch. It also runs 130 files so
a silent cap anywhere in the chain shows up.

**A census axis, so a verb added later cannot escape.** The gate derives the verb list from
`cbt/cyrius.cyr`'s own `streq(cmd, "…")` dispatch sites with awk and FAILS on any verb with
neither a fixture row nor a reasoned exemption. Mutation M11 (adding a `streq(cmd, "zz")`
branch) turns it red. ⚠ **"Has a row" must MEAN "is probed".** The first cut kept COVERED as a
second hand-typed list beside the probe loop, and it drifted at once: `build`, `run` and
`--version` passed the census and were never probed, under a comment claiming every verb was.
COVERED is now the probe list itself (mutation M19: drop `build` from it and the census fails).
The same census runs over `cyriusly` and `ark` from their own dispatch functions.

**Equivalence needs an absolute anchor.** "`V F x` == `V x F`" compares a binary with itself: a
flag dropped in BOTH positions keeps it green (mutation M23 — `doc --check` ignored everywhere —
passed every equivalence row). Each verb also needs one row whose expected value comes from
outside the tool: `doc --check` over a fixture of N undocumented fns must exit N, where N is the
shell's loop counter.

**Anti-vacuous rows next to every assertion.** `plain lint exits 0` beside `lint --strict-
deferrals exits 2`; `build without --dry-run DOES write` beside the seven dry-run rows; `a real
clean DOES remove the file` beside `-q clean --dry-run leaves it alone`; `argc()==1 with no args`
beside `argc()==4 with three`.

**Record the mutations as MEASURED, not as intended.** The ledger in the header carries the fail
COUNT per mutation. One row is honest about not doing what was planned: restoring `lib/flags.cyr`'s
128-positional cap leaves this gate GREEN and turns `tests/tcyr/crossos/flags.tcyr` red, because
the CLI does not use the stdlib parser — two parsers, two gates. Writing that down is worth more
than quietly dropping the row.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_line_scanner_forgets_what_the_language_carries_across_lines"
description = "6.6.5. cyrlint ran every rule on ONE physical line, and four things in cyrius cross a line break: a comment a formatter wrapped, an initializer that continues, a string with a raw newline, a call whose arguments wrap. Each produced a miss — one of them a real silent zero with no warning — and widening the unit of MATCHING must not widen the unit of EVIDENCE."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cyrlint", "lint", "deferral", "init-order", "strings", "measurement", "v6.6.5"]
content = """
mabda filed one row: `# The immediate-offset form is a later` / `# bite.` passed
`cyrlint --strict-deferrals` while the same words on one line exited 2. Three untracked
deferrals shipped that way. The same design produced three more misses nobody had filed:

1. **init-order** — the initializer scan stopped at the line end, so `var A = 1 +` /
   `    B;` over a later `var B = g();` drew no warning while the binary read B as 0
   (exit 1; the same program in declaration order exits 3). Prove a silent zero at
   RUNTIME before asking the linter — the gate compiles both orders and checks the exit
   codes first, so the lint assertion rests on a fact, not on the linter's own opinion.
2. **brace depth** — cyrius strings may hold a raw newline (src/main.cyr has two). Three
   hand-rolled trackers reset "inside a string" at every line end, so every main*.cyr fork
   drew a false `unclosed braces`, and a `}` inside such a string took a real top-level
   `var` out of the init-order rule's sight. One shared helper (`_lx_line`) now carries the
   state; three copies had already drifted.
3. **call notes / enum members** — `sys_open(p,` / `0, 0)` and one-line enums were missed.
   The enum grammar (`PARSE_ENUM_DEF`) makes the `;`/`,` separator OPTIONAL, so "a member
   follows `{` or `,`" — the plan's rule — would have missed sankoch's `;`-separated enum.
   Read the parser, not the plan.

⭐ **Join the TEXT, keep the EVIDENCE line-granular.** The filing proposed accepting a
tracking pointer "anywhere in the joined block". Measured over a corpus before choosing: that
hid one of the filing's OWN three cases (a `docs/` path three lines up "tracked" a `for` /
`now` it had nothing to do with) and silently un-flagged 98 of 179 existing notes. The rule
shipped: a pointer counts only on a physical line the occurrence touches — every single-line
verdict is unchanged and all three filed cases are caught. When a fix widens what a matcher
SEES, check separately what it lets EXCUSE.

⭐ **A per-occurrence re-scan is quadratic on a hostile file.** The first join rescanned the
pointer vocabulary per occurrence: 11–27 s on 1 MB inputs. A per-line cached verdict makes it
76–263 ms, and the gate pins that with `timeout 10` on a 960 KB tracked line (mutating the
cache away turns it red). The same applies to cross-line CALL walks: one walk per LINE with a
line cap, not one per `syscall(` (3 s → 0.25 s on 90K unclosed calls).

⭐ **A classification nothing can observe is dead weight.** The plan made `#`+letter lines a
"directive" class that breaks a paragraph. Measured: removing it changed 0 notes across
24,241 ecosystem files — a directive's keyword (`ifdef`, `derive`) always sits between the
phrase halves — and its only visible effect was hiding `#now`-style wraps. It was dropped.

Oracles in the gate: literal tables typed from the fixtures, plus an awk re-implementation of
the OLD per-line rule for the single-line fixtures (invariance, checked by a different program
in a different language), plus the runtime exit codes. The cyrlint under test is compiled in
the gate from the tree — `build/cyrlint` and the store can both be stale.

⭐ **Enumerate the SHAPES the grammar allows, not the ones you happened to write.** Review
round 2 found six more statement shapes the first cut had not tried, each confirmed by
compiling it: `var A` / `= 1 + B;` (the `=` opens the next line, past comment lines — a silent
zero, exit 1), `pub var` / `public var`, a second decl on a line as a forward-ref TARGET,
`sys_open (p, 0, 0)` and `syscall` / `(…)` — and the snake_case rule, which matched a
line-initial `fn ` only, had never checked one of the ecosystem's 28,652 `pub fn` lines. Every
one was measured at 0 live instances
across 24,241 files — and so had the shape the bite was filed for, until mabda wrote it. Ask
the parser what it accepts (`var A` with no `=` and no `;` is an error, so the look-ahead is
safe to take at the first significant byte) and cover each shape the rule claims.

⭐ **A `#` is not always a comment.** The lexer turns ten byte prefixes (`#naked`, `#inline`,
`#pure`, `#io`, `#alloc`, `#must_use`, `#regalloc`, `#deprecated`, `#assert`, `#pe_import`)
into TOKENS and keeps lexing the line, so `#naked fn isr() {` opens a real brace. cyrlint read
the line as a comment (15 false `unmatched closing brace`, and a negative depth that blinded
init-order to a real silent zero after it), and cyrfmt had the SAME reading: `cyrius fmt`
rewrote that fn's body flush left. Both now mirror the lexer's own table. The lexer matches
with no word boundary, so `#ioctl numbers` does not compile — a lexer defect the tools mirror
on purpose until it is fixed (issue 2026-09-19-lexer-attribute-prefix-swallows-comments).
When a tool re-implements the lexer's idea of "comment", copy the lexer's table, not the
one-byte rule everyone remembers.

⭐ **A check that expects "nothing" must prove the tool RAN.** The first gate compared
cyrlint's stderr against `''` / `0` and discarded its exit status, so a cyrlint that SEGFAULTED
on all seven src/main*.cyr forks read PASS 77/77 (a crash prints nothing, and nothing matched).
Every run now goes through one wrapper: a timeout, a signal death or a missing `<n> warnings`
trailer is a hard failure, and after the first timeout every later run fails at once, so a
looping build costs one timeout instead of hanging check.sh. Mutation-prove the harness too:
a crashing mutant and a looping mutant are ledger rows now.

⭐ **Round 3: the unit is the STATEMENT, not the line — and order is by OFFSET.** Round 2's
look-ahead accepted an `=` only when it OPENED the next line; the grammar lets the header wrap
anywhere: `var` / `A = 1 + B;`, `var A:` / `i64 = …`, `var A` / `: i64 = …` — each a silent
zero (exit 1, twins 3). Two more statement STARTS were invisible: a `var` after a `}` that
closes a fn on the same line, and a `var` after a `;` that ends a WRAPPED initializer. And a
line-number compare cannot order two declarations on one line: `var A = 1 + B; var B = g();`
reads B as 0 and drew nothing. The rule now walks the header across lines to its `=`/`;`,
takes the name from the next line after a bare `var`, continues the `;` chain across lines,
tries a declaration after every `}` that returns to depth 0, and compares declaration
OFFSETS. Every walk ends where the next declaration can begin and a "frontier" skips any
keyword inside an already-walked statement, so the walks never overlap: 50K lines of
`{} var vN = (` finish in 0.16 s (without the frontier each line scans to EOF — quadratic).
0 changed notes over 24,241 ecosystem files; every new shape is pinned with a runtime twin.

⭐ **A buffer sized for the INPUT is not sized for the OUTPUT.** cyrfmt's output buffer was the
input cap (1 MB) with no bound check, and formatting can multiply size: 20K lines of an
unclosed `syscall(a,` indent line i by 2·i spaces, so `--check`/`--write` SEGFAULTED (at 6.6.4
too) and stdout mode streamed >100 MB. Bound the writer, stop the indent loop at the cap, and
fail LOUDLY — the per-line quadratic work disappears with the output.

⭐ **A silent truncation makes a gate judge a PREFIX.** cyrdoc read every file into a fixed
64 KB buffer. sigil's CI requires "0 undocumented" in its 1,106,698-byte dist bundle: cyrdoc
saw 69 of its 1,168 fns (6 %) and passed; read whole, 429 are undocumented. cyrius's own
stdlib doc gate had judged three >64 KB files on a prefix. The buffer now grows until a read
comes up short of it. Grep every fixed-size read in a checking tool: `file_read_all(p, buf,
N)` returning exactly N is a truncation, not a file of length N.

⭐ **The PREPROCESSOR had the same defect one layer down — fixed 6.6.6.** A line inside a
multi-line string that began with `#ifdef` / `#endif` / `#define` was EXECUTED: `"ab` /
`#ifdef NOPE` / `cd` / `#endif` / `ef"` compiled to the bytes `ab`, four newlines, `ef`, exit 0,
no diagnostic. Three separate passes each carried an inlined copy of a state machine that reset
`in_string` at every newline, justified in a comment by the premise "cyrius source uses
single-line strings" — false about `src/main.cyr`'s own error messages. ⚠ **The reset was a
blast-radius bound, so deleting it is not the fix.** It bounded a `"` the machine MIS-READ, and
both real sources of that had to become states first: a `"` in a `#` comment and a `"` in a char
literal (`var q = '"';`). Remove the reset without the char state and one apostrophe poisons the
whole buffer. Fixed as `PP_LEXST`, one machine for all the line-oriented passes.

⛔ **COUNT THE INSTANCES BY SHAPE, NOT BY NAME — there was a FOURTH pass.** The first cut
fixed the three passes that HAD a state machine and said so in the commit, the CHANGELOG, the
gate header and the issue's resolution. `PP_IS_HOST_ONLY`, 2,300 lines up in the same file,
was missed because it had NO state machine to find: just `var bol = 1` and a column-0 byte
match. Its own comment named the column-0 requirement as the safeguard ("so prose that merely
mentions the directive cannot trip it") — and a raw LF inside a string is exactly what puts an
arbitrary byte at column 0, so an included file holding `var doc = "intro` / `#host_only` /
`end";` made every bare-metal build refuse it. Grep the SHAPE (a `bol` flag, a first-byte-of-line
decision), not the names you already know; a pass with no state is the one a "which passes carry
this state?" search cannot return.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "two_sites_that_rederive_one_mapping_drift_record_it_where_it_is_decided"
description = "6.6.6. PARSE_FN_DEF homed register params INSIDE the parameter loop (with each param's true int ordinal and frame slot) and stack params in a SECOND pass after the loop that re-derived ordinal, slot AND the caller's int-class total from `pc`, the all-class param count. A value-form vector next to 6+ ints, or an x86 retptr with 6+ params, desynchronised it on every backend — silently. v6.4.44 had already patched ONE input of that pass (the Win64 retptr ordinal) and left the other two. Fix: record (ordinal, slot) where the loop classifies the param; the pass only executes what was recorded."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["abi", "calling-convention", "stack-args", "simd", "retptr", "param-homing", "rederivation", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**Shape.** A deferred pass cannot run where the facts are known (SysV/aarch64 stack slots
count DOWN from the caller's int-class TOTAL, which is only known after the whole list is
parsed), so it was written to RE-DERIVE the facts afterwards — from `pc`. Three inputs, one
proxy:

| input | re-derived as | true value |
|---|---|---|
| stack total | `pc` | int-class params + retptr (`int_pc + _pl_shift`) |
| arg ordinal | loop counter `spi` | that param's `int_pc + _pp_shift` |
| frame slot  | loop counter `spi` | that param's `li` (a vector owns 2-4 slots) |

The proxy is exact for the common case (every param int-class, one slot, no retptr), so
323 `.tcyr` and 99 programs never exercised a difference — the fix moved ZERO corpus bytes.
`n6(v: f64v2, 1..6)` read `e` as 0; `mk6(1..6): P3` read its sixth param from the return
address; x86, aarch64, Win64 and cx were all wrong, each differently.

**Why a patch on one input is not a fix.** v6.4.44 found the Win64 retptr case of this
same pass (vidya `win64_retptr_shifted_deep_stack_param_homing_...`) and taught it the
ordinal shift — the one input that had a symptom on the host in hand. The total stayed
`pc` (wrong on SysV with a retptr) and the vector's ordinal/slot stayed `spi`. Any pass
that recomputes what an earlier site already decided will drift again the next time the
earlier site learns a new case; the durable fix is to RECORD at the deciding site
(`_stkp_defer(ordinal, li)`) and have the pass consume the record (`_stkp_flush(total)`).
Recording inside the loop's own guards also stopped the pass homing params the loop had
refused (a coroutine impl's).

**How to find the next one.** Grep for a second pass over the same list keyed by a
counter the first pass did not use as its key: `while (spi < pc)` next to a loop that
tracked `int_pc` / `li`. The caller paths the filing exercised — plain call, method call,
call in `return` position — were already right on every target (and the filing's "a
per-target convention change" was wrong) — bisect caller vs callee by disassembling the
prologue homing store against the body's FINDLOCAL read first. ⚠ Do not generalise that to
"every caller": the struct-valued-assign paths (`var p: P3 = f(v, ...)`, parse_decl.cyr
`asv` / `asv_pair`) push a value-form vector as an INT at any arity — a separate caller-side
defect, filed at 6.6.6 (`2026-09-19-struct-valued-assign-call-pushes-simd-args-as-ints`).
Name the caller paths you measured; "all of them" is a claim nobody enumerated.

**Gate.** Differential gates (method arm vs free fn) cannot see a defect on the shared
callee half — both arms agree while both are wrong. `stack_param_homing_matrix.sh`
GENERATES the shape matrix (class x position x 5..9 ints, retptr) with expectations built
as digit strings by awk while the callee computes them arithmetically.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "redeclared_name_new_slot_orphans_per_slot_data_and_name_replays_drift"
description = "6.6.6. A top-level `var` redeclaration was registered as a SECOND slot while every reference bound to the last one (FINDVAR is last-match). Anything written PER SLOT for the first declaration — its baked static-init value — was orphaned, so `var a = 5; var b = a; var a = 5;` read b as 0, and the collision warning's '(last definition wins)' described a semantics nothing implemented. A replay that re-resolved the name at emit time (instead of using the slot recorded at declaration) could land on a LATER declaration in kernel builds. Fix: one slot per name in the declaration zone, value rule stated and gated, replay entries carry their slot."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["globals", "static-init", "symbol-table", "last-match", "redeclaration", "diagnostics", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**Shape.** Two mechanisms each correct on their own: (1) static-init bakes a constant
into the image AT THE SLOT INDEX being registered; (2) name lookup returns the LAST slot
with that name. Put together, a redeclared name's first value lives in a slot no reference
can reach. The v5.11.64 static-init even had a "shadow opt-out" — but it opted out the
LATER declaration (to stop an earlier runtime store clobbering it), never the earlier one,
whose value was the one being orphaned.

**The warning was the spec nobody tested.** `CHKDUPVAL` printed "(last definition wins)"
since v6.2.11, and no gate ever read the value back. A diagnostic that states a semantics
is a claim: gate the claim (read the value from an earlier initializer, a fn, and after
init), not just that the diagnostic fires.

**Replay by name drifts.** EMIT_GVAR_INITS replayed each deferred initializer by
re-resolving the name. In a normal build the replay runs before the top-level program, so
it happened to find the declaration's own slot; in a kernel build it runs AFTER, and found
the program's later `var` of the same name. Record the slot when the entry is registered
(`_gv_ent_set`) and replay by the record — the same lesson as the stack-param homing entry
above: record where it is decided, do not re-derive.

**Where the rule stops.** After the first top-level statement a `var` is a statement and
redeclaration is lexical shadowing (in-tree tests re-declare a scratch buffer at a new
size). Merging there would change a relied-on semantics; the defect was the declaration
zone. Measure the corpus before widening a fold: a probe that printed every shadowing
registration found 53 in-tree, all same-shape — but it had not instrumented the top-level
array path, and the full `.tcyr` old-vs-new loop is what surfaced the two size-changing
script-zone arrays the first cut (which folded both zones) broke.

**Review round (same release) — three more instances of "resolved by name at the wrong
time".** (a) Turning a deferred store into a static one REORDERS it against every other
startup store: the enum pass-2 init (`PARSE_ENUM_DEF(S, 1)`) runs before the deferred
replay and resolves the variant name last-match, so once a `var` over an enum constant
became static the enum's store overwrote it (`enum E { K = 5; } var K = 7;` read 5 for
every read). Before making anything static, list every OTHER store that can target the
slot and when it runs. (b) Recording the replay's STORE target fixed half of the kernel
case: names the replayed initializer READS still went through FINDVAR after the program
had registered its own globals — the fix ranks those below declaration-zone matches while
the replay runs (`_fv_hidden`). A "record, don't re-derive" fix has to cover every lookup
the replayed code makes, not just the one that motivated it. (c) An opt-out whose hazard
you have just removed is now only a restriction: the v5.11.64 shadow opt-out existed
because the replay re-resolved its store onto the later slot; with recorded slots it only
kept a different-owner shadow on the runtime path, where an earlier read saw 0.
And a real consumer was hit: agnos declares one register index twice with the same value;
in kernel mode the second (deferred) store is never reached, so it read 0 for the life of
the kernel.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "declaration_zone_skip_stops_at_first_semicolon_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius parses a top-level `var` TWICE (pass 1 registers it, pass 2 steps over it) and both passes used to find its end by scanning to the FIRST `;`. That was correct only while no expression could contain a `;` — a premise closures broke in v6.3.7. `var f = |x| { return 7; };` before the first top-level statement therefore stopped BOTH passes inside the closure body, at its `}`, which ends every top-level loop: the rest of the program was silently dropped and the binary exited with the closure's address. Fixed at 6.6.6 by one shared brace-aware skip (`_skip_gvar_decl`) used by PARSE_GVAR_REG's two arms and by all seven `src/main*.cyr` forks."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parser", "declaration-zone", "closures", "token-skip", "silent-failure", "forks", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The class, which is the reusable part: a lexical SKIP encodes an assumption about the
grammar, and a later syntax extension can falsify it without touching the skip.** The
skip-to-`;` in `PARSE_GVAR_REG` (and the copy in every fork's pass-2 `var` arm) was written
when a `var` initializer was an expression and an expression could not contain a `;`. Block
closures (v6.3.7) made that false. Nothing failed at the closure — it failed three releases
later, in unrelated code, as "the second half of my file isn't in the binary".

**What made it invisible for so long.** The failure has no error path: the top-level loops
END at `}`, so a skip that stops mid-body looks to both passes like a well-formed end of
input. A program with no `main` exits with the last global's value, so the status was the
closure's ADDRESS (186 = 0x…ba) — a plausible-looking non-zero number, not a crash. And
the two positions people actually test were both fine: an expression-bodied closure
(`|x| x + 1`) carries no `;`, and a closure AFTER a top-level statement is parsed by
`PARSE_PROG` as a statement. `tests/tcyr/lang/closures.tcyr` keeps every closure inside a
fn, so the corpus never covered the broken position at all.

**Two consequences worth separating, because a partial fix hides one of them.** Pass 2
stopping early drops the program; pass 1 stopping early drops the REGISTRATION of everything
below the declaration — globals, structs, enums. Fix only pass 2 and the program compiles
but a `var` declared after the closure does not exist. That asymmetry is why the gate row
for the destructure arm needed a global declared BELOW it to detect a pass-1-only revert:
without it the mutant passed every row.

**Search the shape, not the name.** `grep` for the loop shape — `while (… PEEKT(S) == 5 …)`
— found NINE copies: two in `PARSE_GVAR_REG` (the plain arm and the `var a, b = f();`
destructure arm) and one in each of the seven `src/main*.cyr` forks. Seven forks means a
fix applied to six of them is broken on exactly one target, with no local gate to notice;
the gate therefore carries a static parity axis that derives the fork list from
`src/main*.cyr` rather than listing it.

**Only BRACE depth may carry a skip past a `;`.** Counting parens and brackets too looks
more thorough and is worse: a malformed `var x = f(1;` then swallows the rest of the file,
and every diagnostic below it disappears — trading a silent miscompile for a silent
mis-diagnosis. `_prescan_skip_stmt` (6.6.5) tracks all three because it skips STATEMENTS,
where `for (;;)` is legal at paren depth; a DECLARATION never has one.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "unhandled_flag_falls_through_to_compile_stdin_6_6_6"
description = "cycc's fall-through for an invocation it does not recognise is COMPILE STDIN. So a driver missing a `--version` arm does not error — it compiles EMPTY input and writes a runnable binary to stdout with exit 0. Four of the seven `src/main*.cyr` forks were in that state at 6.6.6 (`cycc --version` on an ARM install wrote a 65,888-byte ELF; `cycc_cx` wrote 24 bytes of .cyx), and the starship prompt segment `scripts/install.sh` installs runs `cycc --version | awk '{print $2}'`, so inside any cyrius project an ARM or cx install showed a field of that binary as the version in the shell prompt. Same release: `src/main_cx.cyr` predefined no `CYRIUS_TARGET_*` macro at all, so every per-target `#ifdef` arm in the stdlib compiled to NOTHING on cx."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cli", "forks", "silent-failure", "empty-input", "preprocessor", "ifdef", "cx", "macos", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**Two different mechanisms, one shape: the failure mode of "unhandled" is SILENT SUCCESS,
so the absence of a feature looks exactly like the feature working.**

**1. An unrecognised flag is not an error — it is an empty compile.** cycc reads stdin and
compiles it; a flag it does not match is simply ignored. With nothing piped in, stdin is
empty, an empty program is valid, and cycc emits a runnable binary and exits 0. Nothing in
the pipeline can tell that apart from a successful `--version`: exit code 0, output on
stdout, no stderr. This is why four forks could go years without the arm. ⚠ The general
rule: when a tool's default action is "do the thing", a missing flag handler is a SILENT
WRONG ACTION, and the gate has to assert the OUTPUT SHAPE (here: no ELF/MZ/Mach-O/CYX magic
on stdout, exactly one line, field 2 == the VERSION file), not just the exit code.

**2. A per-target `#ifdef` stdlib compiles to NOTHING on a target that predefines no
macro.** `lib/alloc.cyr` and `lib/syscalls.cyr` are `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_{WIN,MACOS,AGNOS,
LINUX}` arms. `src/main_cx.cyr` predefined only `CYRIUS_HAS_VAL_SIMD_PARAMS` — so on cx NO
arm matched, those modules contributed no functions, and `include "lib/assert.cyr"` failed
with `undefined function(s) called (cx backend): alloc, vec_get, alloc_reset, sys_exit`. The
consequence is the one worth remembering: **no `.tcyr` had ever run on that target**, and
the five cx gates that existed all proved codegen SHAPES rather than that the stdlib works.
"Compiles on five targets is not runs on five targets" applies to the STDLIB, not only to
syscall wrappers.

**Why the fork axis has to be DERIVED.** A gate that lists the forks it checks is a gate
that an eighth fork walks straight past. `tests/gates/toolchain/fork_version_parity.sh`
derives the list from `src/main*.cyr` with a floor, and then RUNS each fork's built
compiler where the host can run it — x86 and the host cross stages directly, PE under wine,
aarch64 under qemu. A static grep alone is not enough and the mutation ledger records why:
flipping the `--ve` test byte 118 -> 119 leaves the `_VERSION_STR_` write in the source, so
the grep row stays green while the binary goes back to emitting an ELF.

**macOS forks cannot use the `/proc` arm, and the dead copy looks alive.**
`main_aarch64_macho.cyr` carried a verbatim copy of the Linux `/proc/self/cmdline` scan from
v5.11.63. On Darwin the `open()` fails and returns a POSITIVE errno in rax (no carry check),
so the `if (_vn > 0)` guard written for Linux's negative-errno convention FIRES on the errno
and the walk runs harmlessly over a zeroed buffer — matching nothing, reporting nothing. So
`--strict` and `--allow-undef` had never worked there either. argv on macOS comes from the
entry stack the driver parks (x28 on arm64, r15 on x86 — the base `_read_env` has used since
v6.0.33/v6.4.59); at 6.6.6 that reader moved to `src/common/util.cyr`, because util.cyr is
the ONE module all seven drivers include and `main_cx.cyr` deliberately does not include
`backend/common/runtime.cyr`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "argv0_is_not_a_self_path_on_windows_6_6_6"
description = "On Linux a tool finds its own install root with readlink(\"/proc/self/exe\"); on macOS with fcntl(F_GETPATH). Windows has neither, and the tempting substitute — argv(0) — is NOT a self path: on Windows argv[0] is whatever the PARENT wrote on the command line, so it can be relative or a bare name resolved through PATH. programs/cyrius-init.cyr derives its templates directory from its own path's GRANDPARENT, so an argv0 fallback yields a directory that does not exist. The real answer is kernel32!GetModuleFileNameW, added at 6.6.6 as PE reroute 0xF03A. The trap is that the argv0 version COMPILES, ships, and passes a --dry-run."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["windows", "pe", "reroute", "self-path", "argv0", "packaging", "silent-failure", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The class: a portability arm that compiles is not a portability arm that works, and the
cheapest-looking fallback can be wrong in a way no build-time check can see.**

`cyrius-init` did not cross-compile to PE at all until 6.6.6 — `_self_path` reached an
unguarded `sys_readlink` and the port driver an unguarded `sys_rename`, neither defined in
`lib/syscalls_windows.cyr`, so the build refused with two reachable undefined functions and
a 0-byte file. `cyrius init` and `cyrius port` simply did not exist on a Windows install.

The filing offered two repairs. One was the reroute; the other was "fall back to the
`argv(0)` the function already has". **Measured, the argv0 route fails**, and it fails in
the worst possible shape:

  * it compiles clean, so every build gate is green;
  * `--dry-run` passes, because a dry run never resolves the templates;
  * a real `init` launched the way a test launches it — absolute path, cwd next to the
    binary — also passes;
  * and it fails the moment the exe is invoked by a relative path, or off PATH, from some
    other directory. Then the grandparent walk lands somewhere that does not exist and the
    tool scaffolds `error: missing template` for every file.

So the gate (`tests/gates/toolchain/cyrius_init_builds_for_pe.sh`) invokes the binary as
`..\\bin\\cyrius-init.exe` from an unrelated working directory. That one detail is the whole
difference between a gate that proves the fix and a gate that proves the mutant.

**Two reusable mechanics from the reroute itself:**

1. **A wide-string Win32 reroute should return the RAW UTF-16 and let cyrius narrow it.**
   `syscall(0xF006)`/GetCommandLineW already worked this way — `lib/args.cyr`'s
   `_args_w2u8` does the conversion, where it is pure, host-testable and shared. Following
   that split made `EGETMODFILENAME_PE` a plain 3-register Win64 call (rcx=NULL,
   rdx=buffer, r8d=capacity) with no hand-assembled conversion loop at all. Reroutes that
   widen inline (EOPEN_PE, EMOVEFILEEX_PE) each carry ~25 instructions of loop that only
   real Windows can test; prefer the split when the API hands you a buffer.

2. **The PE reroute chain in `_PARSE_FACTOR_IMPL` is at cybs's per-function global/call
   reference limit.** New arms go in their own small helper fn (`_PE_ROUTE_MODULEPATH`,
   next to `_PE_ROUTE_PERFCOUNTER`) with ONE call from the chain. cybs fails SILENTLY past
   that limit and losing seed → cybs → cycc is days of repair.

**And the half nobody lists in an acceptance criterion: the installer.** Shipping the
binary in the tarball was not enough — `scripts/install.ps1` copied only `bin\\` and
`lib\\`, so `programs/cyrius-init-templates` would have reached the tarball and stopped
there, and the binary would have shipped, run, and reported `missing template` for
everything. When a tool resolves data relative to its own install root, the packaging
question is not "is the binary in the tarball" but "does the installer put the DATA beside
the binary, at every level the binary can be invoked from" — here both `versions\\<v>\\`
and the active home, because Windows copies rather than symlinks.

**Postscript, and it is the same class as the `-1` fork stub in the next entry: the first
cut of this fix also added `sys_readlink` to the Windows peer as an honest `-38`/ENOSYS,
and review removed it.** With the scaffolder's call moved behind `#ifndef
CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN`, the stub was measured to change nothing about the PE build — same rc,
same `MZ`, same 155,648 bytes, zero undefined functions — so it bought no capability. What
it cost was the only thing that made this bug findable: the v6.3.2 reachable-undef HARD
ERROR. `readlink("/proc/self/exe")` is the precise shape of this entire filing, and with a
stub present that line COMPILES for PE and then silently degrades to argv0 — the exact
half-fix the gate's relative-path axes exist to catch, waved through one step earlier.
**Define a POSIX name for a target only where the definition is USED; a stub whose only
effect is to let the wrong code build is a diagnostic you deleted.** Callers that genuinely
want a degrade have the portable façade (`lib/io.cyr`'s `xreadlink`, `-1` behind its own
`#ifdef`); the gate now pins the ABSENCE, with the mutation "put the stub back" reddening
it while leaving every build axis green.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_minus_one_fork_stub_succeeds_wrongly_on_pe_6_6_6"
description = "lib/syscalls_windows.cyr defines sys_fork and sys_waitpid as `return 0 - 1` WRAPPERS, not as unrouted syscalls returning -ENOSYS. So a POSIX fork/wait on PE does not FAIL, it SUCCEEDS WRONGLY: pid is -1, the parent takes the `pid != 0` branch, sys_waitpid answers -1 without touching the status buffer, and the caller decodes an UNINITIALISED STACK SLOT as the child's exit status. Whenever that garbage reads as WIFEXITED with status 0, the code reports success for a child that never existed. Measured on real cass at 6.6.5: `cyrius self` printed its header and exited 0 having compiled nothing, and stayed exit 0 with the compiler source deleted. The cyrius-guide had written this down as safe — \"code paths that are dead on Windows (e.g. POSIX fork/execve) can compile without routing them\" — and that sentence licensed twelve unguarded sites in cbt/."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["windows", "pe", "fork", "waitpid", "silent-failure", "uninitialised", "per-target-source", "gate-design", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The class: a stub that returns a sentinel is not a stub that stops you.** -ENOSYS from an
unrouted `syscall(n, ...)` is loud enough to notice; a wrapper that returns `-1` is not,
because the POSIX idiom around fork does not test the pid before using it. `pid == 0` is
the CHILD test, and `-1` is not 0, so the parent branch runs. `sys_waitpid` then returns -1
having written nothing, and `WIFEXITED(load32(&status_buf))` reads whatever was on the
stack. The failure is not a crash and not an error code — it is a *plausible verdict*.

Two lessons that generalise past Windows:

1. **Do not reason about a platform arm from the syscall table.** "That number is not
   routed, so the path is dead there" is only true when the call site really reaches the
   raw `syscall()`. Everything in `lib/syscalls_<target>.cyr` is a named wrapper that may
   answer with a value. Grep for the WRAPPER, not the number.

2. **A per-target verb must use the per-target SOURCE.** The Windows arm of `cyrius self`
   first self-hosted from `src/main.cyr`, the Linux fork, because that is what the POSIX
   shell script names. `cycc.exe` compiles it happily — the frontend does not care — and
   produces a PE built from Linux syscall numbers, which page-faults the instant step two
   executes it (`Unhandled page fault on write access ... at 0000000140001060` under wine).
   The right source is `src/main_win.cyr`. The same rule the cross-OS legs already follow
   for ecb (`src/main_aarch64_macho.cyr`).

**And the gate-design half.** A "no silent nothing" property is satisfied by
`#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN return 0; #endif`, which is *the defect*, reading greener than
the bug. So `tests/gates/platform/cbt_fork_sites_have_pe_arm.sh` derives the fork-site list
from `cbt/*.cyr` (a new site cannot be added without an arm), requires each arm to NAME
what it does, and — the half that turned out to matter — requires every `fn _win_*` DEFINED
in `cbt/` to be REACHABLE in the cross-built PE binary, from cycc's own DCE listing. The
arm-derived list cannot see a helper go dead, because an arm that stops calling its helper
drops the name from that list; a mutation that replaced a spawn call with a constant read
GREEN until the definition-derived half existed.

⚠ Its own detector had two defects that each made a mutant pass: an awk comment strip
(`sub(/#[^i].*/, "", code)`) ate `#endif` as a comment, so every arm stayed open and the
scan found 1 site of 17; and a shell glob `*_win_*` matched a LOCAL named `cap_win_rc`,
admitting an emptied arm. Both were found only because the mutation ledger was actually
run. Match a helper as a CALL (`name` followed by `(`), never as a substring.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_guard_inside_one_copier_covers_only_that_copier"
description = "v6.5.21 closed the `#@file` forgery hole INSIDE PP_PASS's copy loop; three other routes carried source bytes to the preprocessor output and stayed open for four minors (CVE-44). Where a defence goes decides what it covers — put it at the ENTRY POINT of the untrusted bytes, not in one of the things that moves them."
category = "cyrius"
---
THE HOLE. `private` visibility is decided from the preprocessor's
`#@file "NAME" BASE` markers, and `FM_BUILD` accepts one at ANY offset —
no byte-0 rule, no beginning-of-line rule, unlike `#@incdir`. So any
bytes that reach the preprocessor's output can mint a file span and claim
to be another file, which is a direct route to that file's private items.

v6.5.21 saw that and fixed it — inside `PP_PASS`'s copy loop, with a
careful comment explaining that the loop was the right place because it
is the one that knows about string literals. The reasoning was sound and
the code was correct. It covered one route of four.

MEASURED AT 6.6.5, each one a clean build and `exit 42` where the honest
program is refused with `'SECRET_ADD' is private to its file`:

  1. an INCLUDED file  — `READFILE` writes it STRAIGHT into `out`, in
     both passes. It never meets the loop at all.
  2. a `#define` macro BODY — the `#define` line is consumed by the
     directive handler (so it never meets the loop either) and the stored
     body is written out later by `PP_EXPAND`.
  3. a `#derive` line's TAIL — `PP_COPY_TAIL` copies it verbatim.

⚠ THE PASS THAT DOES (2) CARRIED A 17-LINE COMMENT HEADED "v6.5.21 —
NEUTRALISE a user-authored `#@file` marker", describing in detail a guard
that had never been in it. A reader auditing the route would have read
that and moved on. A comment that describes a defence is not a defence,
and a copied comment is the cheapest way to make a hole look closed.

THE FIX SHAPE. Neutralise at the ENTRY POINTS — one pass over the raw
source before anything reads it, plus one call at each include's
`READFILE` — instead of at one of the places that move bytes around.
There are two ways into the pipeline and four ways through it; guarding
the two is complete by construction, guarding one of the four is not.
The v6.5.21 inline guard was REMOVED rather than left alongside: two
mechanisms for one invariant is how the first came to be believed
complete (the same failure the `source_marked` one-shot caused for the
file map at v6.5.3).

MECHANICAL NOTE worth copying: the neutraliser OVERWRITES the `@` with a
space rather than inserting a byte, so the region keeps its LENGTH and no
line or column shifts. The v6.5.21 version inserted, and had to carry a
caveat about shifted columns inside comments.

[[entries]]
name = "a_degrade_becomes_a_permission_once_rules_are_written_around_it_6_6_6"
description = "lib/io.cyr's xrmdir returned -1 on PE for four minors because no RemoveDirectoryW reroute was wired, and the -1 was documented as an honest degrade. By 6.6.6 THREE separate rules had been written around it: a crossos test asserted its own xrmdir only `if (rd == 0)`, the same test kept a FIXED directory name under #ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN because a per-pid one could never be cleaned up, and a tree-write gate carried a `win_guarded` allowlist RULE whose stated reason was the gap. Each rule made the gap cheaper to live with and the fix look bigger; the one target where the call could fail was the one target not asserting it."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["windows", "pe", "reroute", "rmdir", "degrade", "allowlist", "gate-design", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The class.** A `-1` / no-op degrade on one target is honest exactly while nothing depends
on the call succeeding. The moment something does — here `_cbt_tmpdir_cleanup`, which had
to remove the CLI's private temp directory — the degrade is a defect, and it has usually
been a defect for a while without anybody noticing, because the workarounds accumulated
quietly and each one looked local and reasonable.

Watch for these three shapes; they are what a live degrade grows:

1. **A conditional assertion.** `var rd = xrmdir(d); if (rd == 0) { assert_eq(...); }` —
   written so the one target that cannot succeed does not turn the suite red. It also
   means the only target where the call can fail is the only one not asking, so the gap is
   structurally invisible to the test that covers it.
2. **A per-target fixture.** The same test kept a FIXED directory name on PE while every
   other target used a per-pid one, because a per-pid name that can never be removed turns
   one reused leftover into one per run. Perfectly sound reasoning — about a defect.
3. **An allowlist RULE, not just an entry.** A gate grew a whole `win_guarded` rule
   (detector, self-test, ledger row) whose written justification was the missing reroute.
   A rule is more durable than the defect it excuses: entries get re-checked for staleness,
   rules get re-used.

**The fix is all four places, in one go.** Wiring the reroute (`syscall(0xF03A)` →
kernel32!RemoveDirectoryW, an exact copy of `EDELETEFILEW_PE` with a different import —
same 1-argument shape, same inline UTF-8→UTF-16 widener, same rbx-anchored `and rsp,-16`
frame, same BOOL→0/-1) is the small part. Deleting the three permissions is the part that
stops it coming back, and it is the part a reviewer will not ask for, because each one
reads as a sensible local accommodation.

⚠ When a fix removes a carve-out, delete the RULE, not only the entry. A rule with no
users is a standing permission waiting for its next user.

**Measured, so the premise is not a story.** On real cass (Windows 11) the pre-fix CLI
took `%TEMP%`'s `cyrius-*` count from 6 to 8 over five verbs and the fixed one left it at
6; the crossos test went 6/7-with-one-skipped to 7/7 with nothing left behind. A gate row
that only asserts "no directory was left" is satisfied by a CLI that never made one, so
the companion row probes the primitive directly — five `xrmdir` behaviours, each its own
exit code.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "grep_the_shape_not_the_name_a_rule_applied_inline_once_is_the_tell_6_6_6"
description = "A filing named ONE defective predicate (#io matched as a byte prefix, so `#ioctl numbers` was parsed as code). Fixing the ten it listed left NINE more instances of the same shape in the same subsystem, found only across three review rounds: PP_IS_HOST_ONLY, three ISDERIVE* probes, a fourth reader in cyrius_api_surface.cyr, and ISENDIF/ISENDPLAT/ISSRCLINE — the last of which let a COMMENT close an #ifdef so that skipped code was compiled in silently. ISELSE had carried the boundary inline all along, which was the tell that the invariant was already known and applied exactly once."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["lexer", "preprocessor", "word-boundary", "audit-method", "review", "silent-failure", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The method.** When a filing names a defective predicate, the name is a sample, not the
population. Grep the SHAPE — here "a `#`-directive name compared byte by byte and then
acted on, with nothing checking that the name ENDED". One `grep -n '^fn IS'` over
`src/frontend/lex_pp.cyr`, printing each predicate's LAST byte comparison, separated the
bounded ones (the comparison is `!= 32`, a space, so the name ends by construction) from
the unbounded ones in about a minute. That one command found three defects three review
rounds of prose had not.

**⭐ A rule applied INLINE exactly once is the strongest tell there is.** `ISELSE` carried
this, in the source, since long before 6.6.6:

    # Terminator: whitespace, newline, or EOF. Reject identifier
    # continuation so "#elseif" (hypothetical typo) doesn't match.

Someone understood the invariant, wrote it out, applied it to the predicate in front of
them, and did not generalise. Everywhere you find a hand-written guard with a comment
explaining WHY it is needed, ask which siblings did not get it. That comment is a defect
report about its neighbours.

**Severity inverts as you go down the pipeline.** In the LEXER the defect was loud — a
comment failed to compile with a diagnostic pointing at its second word. In the
PREPROCESSOR the same shape is silent and worse:

- `#host_onlyish note` marked a module host-only and refused every bare-metal build
- `#derive(Serialize)x note` ARMED the derive machinery: 4472 B vs the twin's 4456 B, rc=0
- `#endifoo note` CLOSED a conditional, so code inside a skipped `#ifdef` was compiled in
- `#@srclinex 10` shifted every diagnostic in the file by one line

So "the compiler errors, so we would have noticed" does not transfer from one pass to the
next. It is a property of where the predicate sits, not of the bug.

**Score the comment against its SPACED TWIN.** Never ask the compiler what a comment should
produce. Compile `# ioctl numbers` — which no prefix rule can ever read as a directive —
and require the unspaced form to produce a BYTE-IDENTICAL binary. The oracle is a different
program, and it catches the case where both forms compile but to different bytes, which is
precisely the silent half. Pair every such axis with an ARM axis: the wrong fix is "make
every `#` a comment", and it passes every comment axis.

**One deliberate exception, written down.** `#assert(8 == 9)` is rejected out loud today
(`#assert: expected constant expression`). Making `(` a non-boundary there would have made
it a silent comment — a DROPPED compile-time assertion. It keeps its paren boundary, and a
gate axis pins the exception so a later "tidy up the special cases" pass cannot quietly
remove it. A loud error on rare prose beats a silent hole in a check.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_test_whose_oracle_lives_in_the_file_the_defect_damages_cannot_fail_6_6_6"
description = "The preprocessor's macro pass expanded a macro name inside a STRING LITERAL, rewriting the program's own data. The obvious test — assert_streq(lit, \"MIB(5) text\") — passes on the BROKEN compiler, because the expected literal sits in the same file and is mangled identically. Every oracle for a defect that damages source text has to be a control the defect CANNOT reach: a different spelling, a different file, or a value the program computes at runtime. The first cut of this coverage was a .tcyr whose binaries came out byte-identical from the fixed and unfixed compiler, and it took a deliberate old-compiler run to notice."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["preprocessor", "macro", "testing", "vacuous-gate", "oracle", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The trap.** A defect that rewrites SOURCE TEXT damages the test's expected value as
readily as it damages the thing under test. `#define ID(a) (a)` plus

    assert_streq(lit, "ID(5) literal")

is green on the broken compiler: both strings are mangled to `"(5) literal"` and they still
match. The assertion is not wrong, it is UNREACHABLE — there is no compiler for which it
fails.

**The rule.** The oracle must be something the defect cannot touch:

- a CONTROL SPELLING the predicate cannot match (`XIB(5) text` against `MIB(5) text`, same
  length, not a macro) — then compare LENGTHS, not contents
- a TWIN PROGRAM with the trigger renamed, required to compile BYTE-IDENTICALLY
- a value the program derives at RUNTIME (a `while (load8(s + n) != 0)` loop), never a
  number written into the test

**And always run the unfixed compiler against the new test.** `CYRIUS_CC=<old> sh
<gate>` is two seconds and it is the only thing that distinguishes "my fix works" from "my
test cannot fail". Here it turned a 7/7 green .tcyr into an obviously vacuous one: the
fixed and unfixed compilers produced the SAME BYTES for it, which no correct test of a
codegen-visible fix ever does.

**A corollary about mutation ledgers.** Mutating the FIX is not enough on its own —
every mutant is built from a tree that still contains the rest of the change. Run the
whole pre-fix compiler too, and record what it scores (here 4 of 7 axes red); that is the
one run that proves the axes are about the defect and not about the shape of the patch.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_safety_argument_about_what_an_operation_writes_says_nothing_about_what_it_reads_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius's macro pass expands inside `#` comments. The written justification for that — 'a #define body cannot contain a newline, so an expansion inside a comment cannot change the emitted binary' — was reasoned about, believed, and copied into six places in one release. It is a claim about the bytes an expansion WRITES. The bytes it READS were unbounded: PP_EXPAND scanned to the matching `)` wherever that was, so `# TODO: fix M(` swallowed every statement up to the next `)` and the program compiled to exit 0 with an empty stderr. Fixed by BOUNDING the scan to the comment's line, never by excluding comments — the exclusion regresses attribute lines, which the state machine also reads as comments."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["preprocessor", "macro", "safety-argument", "unbounded-scan", "doc-rot", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The shape.** Any transform has two sides: what it EMITS and what it CONSUMES. A safety
argument that only covers one side reads as a complete argument, and it is the kind of
wrong that survives review — the sentence is *true*, it just does not cover the case.

    "a `#define` body cannot contain a newline, so an expansion inside a
     comment cannot change the emitted binary"

Every clause of that is correct about emission. Consumption was never mentioned, and
consumption was unbounded:

    #define M(a) 0
    # TODO: fix M(
    var r = 42;
    var t = 1;
    syscall(60, r + t);        → exit 0, empty stderr. 43 is correct.

**How to check one.** For a transform, ask the two questions separately: *what can this
write?* and *how far can it read?* If the answer to the second is "to the next delimiter",
name the delimiter and ask what happens when it is missing, or when it is somewhere the
author did not mean. Here the answer — "to the matching `)`, anywhere in the file" — makes
the defect obvious in one sentence.

**BOUND, do not EXCLUDE.** The reflex fix is "then do not expand inside comments at all".
Measured, that regresses real code: the preprocessor's lexical state has no notion of
ATTRIBUTES, so `#inline fn f(): i64 { return N(5); }` is "a comment" to it and that macro
legitimately expands. Telling the two apart needs a list of attribute names kept in sync
with the lexer's if-chain, and the direction that list fails in re-opens the defect. The
narrow rule — *an expansion that starts in a comment must also END in it* — needs no list
and leaves the legitimate case alone. The over-correction is worth a mutation of its own
in the gate; here it is the one mutant that turns exactly the attribute axis red.

**And a note on where the claim lived.** It was in a source comment, a gate header, the
gate's registration string, the CHANGELOG, the language guide and the doc-health ledger —
six copies, all written in the same release, all propagated from the first one. Re-reading
any of them would never have caught it, because they agreed. Only running the shape did.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_gate_that_discovers_loops_by_shape_judges_the_fn_you_inline_into_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius 6.6.6's self_host_src_per_target.sh DISCOVERS self-host loops instead of naming them: any fn that both asks `_self_host_src()` and runs something, and it then requires that fn to be one of two shapes — ask and name no fork, or pin build/cycc. Bite 24b fixed `cyrius capacity`'s per-target default by inlining `var s = _self_host_src(); if (file_exists(s)) ... elif (file_exists(\"src/main.cyr\")) ...` into cbt/cyrius.cyr's main(). main() also runs a compiler (the pinned-toolchain re-exec), so the change made main() a self-host loop that both asks AND hard-codes a fork, and a gate from an adjacent bite went red on a correct behavioural fix. The resolution belongs in a named helper; the helper is load-bearing, not tidiness."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["gates", "cbt", "self-host", "shape-detection", "per-target", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The shape.** A gate that finds its subjects by NAME can only ever judge what it was told
about; a gate that finds them by SHAPE judges everything that fits the shape — including
code you add later for an unrelated reason. That is the whole point of the shape-detector,
and it is also its one surprise: *where you put a line changes whether a gate is about it.*

The detector (`self_host_src_per_target.sh` axis 6) admits a fn when

    (asks _self_host_src()  AND  runs something)   OR   (names a fork  AND  compares bytes)

and then demands one of two legitimate shapes:

  (a) it ASKS and writes no fork path of its own — the loop runs THIS HOST's compiler;
  (b) it is pinned to `build/cycc` — the tracked x86-64 Linux compiler, whose own fork IS
      `src/main.cyr`, so naming that fork is correct and ASKING would be wrong.

`main()` in `cbt/cyrius.cyr` runs a compiler — the `_try_redirect_to_pinned()` re-exec —
so the moment a per-target default was inlined into it, it satisfied the left-hand prong
and failed both shapes at once: it asked, and it named `src/main.cyr` as a fallback. The
behaviour was right. The placement was not.

**Why the fallback could not just be dropped.** `capacity`'s literals are not a leftover:
a NON-cyrius project has no per-target fork, so on ARM the host-fork test simply misses and
`src/main.cyr` / `src/lib.cyr` must still answer. Deleting them to satisfy the gate would
have broken `cyrius capacity` for every ARM and macOS project — a far bigger blast radius
than the bug. The mutation that deletes them is in the bite's own gate for that reason.

**The rule.** When a per-target question needs both an ASK and a literal fallback, it goes
in a fn of its own that asks and returns — no execs, no verdicts — so the shape-detector
classifies it as a resolver rather than a loop. Check the neighbouring gates BEFORE
choosing where to put the code, not after the commit: this was caught by running
`self_host_src_per_target.sh` one bite later, and it had already shipped in between.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_reroute_existing_is_not_a_reroute_being_used_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius 6.6.6: cycc.exe saw NONE of its 20 CYRIUS_* knobs because _read_env fell through to `return 0` on PE — while the GetEnvironmentVariableA reroute it needed had existed since v6.0.85 and the comment above it still said the Windows lookup was 'queued for v5.5.x tail'. The stdlib hit the SAME shape at v6.5.45, that half was fixed, and the compiler's own copy one call away was not. The import table is the check that cannot be fooled."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["windows", "pe", "reroute", "env", "audit-by-class", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The shape.** A backend reroute is *capability*, not *use*. `syscall(0xF015)` →
`GetEnvironmentVariableA` has been emittable since v6.0.85, and for the whole of that time
`_read_env` in `src/backend/common/runtime.cyr` — the compiler's own environment reader —
ended its Linux and macOS arms and fell straight to `return 0` on PE. So `cycc.exe` could
not see `CYRIUS_STATS`, `CYRIUS_DCE`, `CYRIUS_SYMS`, `CYRIUS_MONOMORPH` or any of the other
20 names, and every one of them failed the way a knob you did not set fails: silently, with
the default.

**Why nobody noticed.** The comment directly above the function said the Windows lookup was
"queued for v5.5.x tail" — written before v6.0.85, never revisited after it, and therefore
read for five minors as a statement about the present. A stale *plan* comment is worse than
no comment: it answers the question you were about to investigate.

**⭐ The class, which is the actual lesson.** `lib/io.cyr`'s `getenv` hit this EXACT defect at
v6.5.45 and its fix note says so in as many words: *"Windows has the reroute but NOTHING IN
THE STDLIB CALLED IT."* That fix stopped at the stdlib. The compiler's own copy of the same
mechanism, in the same repository, one call away, stayed broken for another minor — because
the two were investigated as two bugs rather than as one class. When you find "the primitive
exists, the caller never called it" anywhere, the next move is to grep every OTHER caller of
that primitive in the tree before closing the bite. Same family as the macOS `_read_env` gap
that caused the v5.3.13→v6.0.32 self-host rot.

**The check that cannot be fooled.** Do not grep the source for the arm and do not `strings`
the binary — cycc carries the literal `"GetEnvironmentVariableA"` inside its own
reroute-warning text whether or not it imports it, so a strings scan reads 2 hits on the
BROKEN compiler. Read the PE **import directory**:

    objdump -x cycc.exe | sed -n '/DLL Name/,$p' | grep -c GetEnvironmentVariableA
    # 0 on 6.6.5, 1 on 6.6.6

An import entry only exists if the emitter actually emitted a call, so this distinguishes
"the code is written" from "the code is reached" — which a source grep cannot.

**Scope correction worth keeping.** The filing assumed `cyrius capacity` was dead on Windows
"for the same reason". It is not: `cmd_capacity` spawns the compiler with `sys_fork()` +
`/bin/sh -c`, and `sys_fork` returns **-1** on PE. Wiring `_read_env` changes nothing there.
Verified by building `cyrius.exe` and running the verb: `error: no stats output from
compiler`, with the fixed compiler in place. Two defects that produce one symptom.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "cmd_set_var_1_ampersand_puts_the_space_in_the_value_6_6_6"
description = "Verifying an env-gated compiler knob on cass: `cmd /c \"set VAR=1 & prog\"` sets VAR to \"1 \" WITH the trailing space. cyrius knobs test load8(v)==49 && load8(v+1)==0, so the value fails to match and a correctly fixed binary reads exactly like the unfixed one. Write `set VAR=1&& prog` — no space before the ampersands."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["windows", "cass", "verification", "cmd", "gotcha", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The trap.** `cmd.exe`'s `set` takes everything to the end of the token as the value,
including whitespace that looks like it belongs to the shell:

    cmd /c "set CYRIUS_STATS=1 & prog"     REM -> CYRIUS_STATS = "1 "   (trailing space!)
    cmd /c "set CYRIUS_STATS=1&& prog"     REM -> CYRIUS_STATS = "1"

**Why it bites here specifically.** Every `CYRIUS_*` knob in the compiler is tested as
`load8(v) == 49 && load8(v + 1) == 0` — the byte `'1'` followed immediately by NUL. `"1 "`
fails the second half. So the variable IS set, `GetEnvironmentVariableA` DOES return it, and
the knob still does nothing.

**Why that is expensive.** It is indistinguishable, from the outside, from the bug you are
there to verify. During bite 18b's hardware check on cass the first run of a correctly fixed
`cycc.exe` produced a zero-byte stderr — exactly what the broken 6.6.5 binary produces —
and the reading "still broken on hardware" was one step away from being written down. The
same command with `&&` and no space printed the full meter block.

This sits alongside the other two cass gotchas already in CLAUDE.md
(`cmd /c "x & echo %errorlevel%"` lies because `%errorlevel%` expands at parse time; a
`prog < in > out 2>nul &` chain corrupts the redirect). All three are the same underlying
thing: **cmd.exe parses the whole line before running any of it**, so anything that looks
like shell punctuation may already have been folded into a value or an expansion.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_pass_that_reads_a_buffer_the_previous_pass_stopped_copying_back_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius's preprocessor passes share two buffers under a documented 'write to preprocess_out, copy BACK to input_buf' contract. PP_IFDEF_PASS dropped the copy-back for a good reason and said so in its own header; PP_MACRO_PASS still read input_buf. Result: one function-like #define anywhere in a file put stripped #ifdef arms back into the build and truncated the source at 1 MB. The dropped-contract pass and the assuming pass were 50 lines apart and each was individually correct."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["preprocessor", "buffers", "lex_pp", "pipeline", "gotcha", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The shape, which is the reusable part.** A pipeline stage is optimised to skip a copy that
the pipeline's contract requires. The optimisation is correct *for that stage* — nothing it
produces is wrong — and its header documents the deviation honestly. A LATER stage, written
earlier or by someone reading a different file, still relies on the contract. Neither stage
is wrong in isolation; the pipeline is.

**The instance.** `src/frontend/lex_pp.cyr`. `PREPROCESS` runs PP_PASS -> PP_IFDEF_PASS ->
PP_MACRO_PASS. The heap map (`src/main.cyr`) says of input_buf@0x7400000: *"Passes write to
preprocess_out and copy BACK here."* PP_IFDEF_PASS's own header says the opposite, with a
reason: *"NO copy-back to S+0 at the end — LEX reads from S+0x459D000, and PP_MACRO_PASS
short-circuits for programs without macros."* That last clause is the whole bug. It is true
only while `_pp_macro_count == 0`, which is the pass's entry condition — so the moment a file
contains ONE function-like `#define`, PP_MACRO_PASS reads input_buf (PP_IFDEF_PASS's INPUT,
truncated to its 1 MB helper window) and writes it over preprocess_out. The `#ifdef` filtering
is undone and everything past 1 MB is gone.

**What it looked like from outside.** Nothing to do with macros:

    #define M(a) (a)              <- never used
    include "lib/alloc.cyr"
    -> error: lib/atomic.cyr:94:20: unexpected identifier 'x0'

`x0` is an aarch64 register inside `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64`, in an x86 build. With
`lib/assert.cyr` instead it was `expected '}', got end of file` at a line number from the
wrong buffer. An OBJECT-like `#define` was fine — it never sets `_pp_macro_count`. So the
feature worked in every small test that had no `#ifdef` beneath it, and the failure was
content-dependent on which stdlib you happened to include.

**How to recognise it again.** When a stage documents "I skip X because the next stage
doesn't need it", the claim is about the NEXT STAGE'S CODE, not about this stage's, and it
rots the moment that code changes — or, as here, the moment its short-circuit does not fire.
Grep the consumer before believing the comment. The fix is one call (`PP_SYNC_SRCB`), placed
behind the consumer's own short-circuit so a program with no function-like macro pays nothing.

**The neighbouring lesson, recorded because it cost time.** The gate for this is a
BYTE-FOR-BYTE binary differential: the same program, with and without an unused
function-like `#define`, must compile to identical bytes. That axis needs no knowledge of
which stdlib file carries an `#ifdef`, and it survives the stdlib changing. A row that greps
for `unexpected identifier 'x0'` only works until atomic.cyr is rewritten.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "two_fields_of_a_foreign_api_constrain_each_other_and_the_emulator_does_not_enforce_it_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius 6.6.6 mapped POSIX open() flags onto CreateFileW by deriving dwDesiredAccess and dwCreationDisposition INDEPENDENTLY — a table per field, which is how such a map is naturally written. Win32 ties them: TRUNCATE_EXISTING is refused with ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER unless the access word carries GENERIC_WRITE (FILE_WRITE_DATA does NOT satisfy it). wine does not enforce that rule, so the gate's wine axis read 30/30 while real Windows ran 25/30 — inside a bite whose whole subject was Windows file semantics."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["windows", "pe", "createfilew", "wine", "cross-os", "translation-table", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The shape.** When you translate one API's argument onto another's, the natural structure is
one mapping table per field. That structure silently assumes the target's fields are
independent. A foreign API's fields very often are not, and the coupling is not in the
argument's own documentation — it is a sentence under the *other* argument.

Measured on real cass (Win 10.0.26200), `CreateFileW` with an existing file:

    acc=GENERIC_READ                    disp=TRUNCATE_EXISTING  -> FAIL err=87
    acc=GENERIC_READ|FILE_WRITE_DATA    disp=TRUNCATE_EXISTING  -> FAIL err=87
    acc=GENERIC_READ|FILE_APPEND_DATA   disp=TRUNCATE_EXISTING  -> FAIL err=87
    acc=GENERIC_WRITE                   disp=TRUNCATE_EXISTING  -> OK, size 0
    acc=GENERIC_READ                    disp=CREATE_ALWAYS      -> OK, TRUNCATED to 0

Note the last row: `CREATE_ALWAYS` truncates happily with no write access at all, so the
coupling is not even uniform across the dispositions — exactly one of them carries it. And
`FILE_WRITE_DATA`, the minimal bit you would reach for, does not satisfy it; kernel32 tests
for `GENERIC_WRITE` itself.

**What it cost in cyrius.** `open(p, O_RDONLY|O_TRUNC)` and `open(p, O_WRONLY|O_TRUNC|O_APPEND)`
— both `O_TRUNC` WITHOUT `O_CREAT`, i.e. both `TRUNCATE_EXISTING` — returned fd = -1 on
Windows where Linux truncates. The fix is one rule, not two special cases: when the ladder
selects `TRUNCATE_EXISTING`, force `GENERIC_WRITE` into the access word.

**⭐ The part worth carrying to any other target.** *wine accepted all three refused forms.*
The local behaviour gate passed the broken compiler 30/30 under wine; real cass ran 25/30.
An emulator implements the *operations*; the cross-field VALIDATION is precisely the kind of
thing it skips, because skipping it makes more programs work. So for a foreign-API argument
map, a green emulator run is weaker evidence than usual — weaker than it is for codegen,
where wine at least executes the same instructions. Two practical consequences:

1. **Probe the target API directly before writing the map.** A PowerShell `Add-Type`
   P/Invoke matrix over the argument pairs costs one SSH round-trip and produces a table you
   can paste into the emit function's comment. Deriving the map from documentation and then
   testing it under an emulator produces a map that is wrong in exactly the places the
   documentation is thin.
2. **Give the shape axis a signature for the coupling.** cyrius's
   `pe_open_flag_translation.sh` now greps the disassembly for `or $0x40000000,%r9d` and
   requires it once per open reroute — an emitter-shape check is an odd thing to lean on,
   but when the behaviour axis structurally cannot see a defect class, the shape axis is the
   only LOCAL guard, and the mutation ledger says so in the row where the mutant passes wine
   and fails hardware.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "an_integrity_check_that_is_conditional_on_an_input_you_never_ship_verifies_nothing_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius's macOS install pillars scp'd a release tarball to a real Mac and ran the real install.sh against it for 30+ releases without ever verifying a hash. install.sh DOES verify a local tarball — fail-closed, on a mismatch it aborts — but only IF a `.sha256` sidecar sits beside the file. The builders wrote the sidecar; the scp never copied it. Both gates were green the whole time, and the Intel-Mac pillar had verified a hash on no release since it was added."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["install", "checksum", "cross-os", "macos", "gates", "fail-open", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The shape, and why it survives review.** An integrity check written as

    if [ -f "$ARTIFACT.sha256" ]; then verify_or_abort; fi

reads as *strict* — the body is fail-closed, aborts on a mismatch, refuses when no
SHA-256 tool is present. Every line you look at is correct. The defect is not in the
branch; it is that **nothing on the calling side guarantees the branch is ever taken.**
A caller that does not ship the sidecar silently selects the no-verification path, and
there is no output to notice: a skipped optional check prints nothing, so a verified
install and an unverified one differ only by one line that is *absent*.

**What it cost here.** `_cross_os_selfhost()` (cbt/commands.cyr) carries the ecb
(Apple Silicon, v6.0.38) and ach (Intel, v6.4.59) install pillars inline — the gates whose
entire purpose is "the published artifact installs on a real Mac". Both `scp`'d the
tarball alone. Measured at 6.6.6 on both real Macs, with a tarball one byte longer than
its sidecar says:

  * sidecar shipped -> `checksum mismatch … aborting`, rc 1, nothing unpacked
  * sidecar NOT shipped -> **zero** mentions of a checksum, and `binaries installed`
    **from the tampered file** before an unrelated later failure

The sidecar had been sitting in the build output directory the whole time
(`sha256sum "$STAGE.tar.gz" > "$STAGE.tar.gz.sha256"`, in every tarball builder since
they were written). One `scp` argument.

**The three-line rule this earns.**

1. **Grep the CALLERS of every conditional check, not the check.** When the condition is
   "an optional input is present", the question is not "is the body right" but "who
   supplies the input, and does anything fail if they don't". Reading the check teaches
   you nothing about how often it runs.
2. **The far side must REQUIRE the input.** Shipping the sidecar is not enough: an `scp`
   that silently drops it puts you straight back on the unverified path with everything
   green. The remote command now starts `[ -f …tar.gz.sha256 ] && …`, so an absent
   sidecar is a RED gate rather than a quiet skip.
3. **A gate for this needs the negative case.** The static "does the scp name a sidecar"
   axis is a string check and proves nothing on its own. The load-bearing axis runs the
   real installer three ways — intact+sidecar, tampered+sidecar, tampered+NO sidecar —
   because only the third measures what the old pillars were actually doing. Without it
   a reviewer has to take "install.sh verifies only if the sidecar is there" on trust,
   which is the same kind of unchecked claim as the bug.

⭐ Related but distinct from "a check that shares a defect with the thing it checks reads
GREEN" (v6.6.2). Here the checker is correct and the *feeder* is missing — which is
harder to see, because auditing the checker is the thing a reviewer naturally does.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_runner_that_grades_by_exit_code_cannot_tell_nothing_ran_from_everything_passed_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius 6.6.6: the cross-OS lib-test runner judged tests/tcyr/crossos/ by exit code alone. A binary that executes NO user code exits 0, so a compiler bug that dropped the whole top-level program scored a PASS on real ecb/ach/cass/pi — over the very defect the test was named for. Measured: the 6.6.5 compiler turned macro_expansion_with_include.tcyr into a 43,512-byte binary that printed nothing and exited 0. The seeded-non-zero trick inside the test cannot help, because the seed is part of the text that vanished."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["testing", "cross-os", "gates", "exit-code", "silent-pass", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The class.** cyrius already knows the empty-CORPUS version of this: `cycc` on empty stdin
exits 0 and emits a runnable binary, so an unmatched glob scored a fake PASS (CLAUDE.md's
`tests/` note, v6.5.11). This is the same trap one level down — an empty PROGRAM rather than
an empty corpus — and it survived the first fix because the corpus was no longer empty. The
file was there, it compiled, it produced a binary, the binary ran, and it exited 0.

**Why in-file defences do not reach it.** The natural guard is what the test already had:

    var mei_rc = 92;          # seeded non-zero
    mei_rc = assert_summary();
    syscall(60, mei_rc);

That catches a build which emits a binary but drops SOME of the program. It cannot catch one
that drops ALL of it, because the seed, the summary call and the exit syscall are all in the
text that disappeared — and with nothing left to run, the process exits 0 by default. There is
no statement you can put in the file that survives the file being removed. **The judgement has
to come from outside the binary.**

**What that looks like.** Every `.tcyr` ends in `assert_summary()`, which prints
`N passed, M failed (T total)` to stdout. So the runner captures stdout and, for a test whose
SOURCE calls `assert_summary`, requires that line with N >= 1. Two properties are worth
copying:

* **Derive the requirement from the artifact under test, not from a list.** `grep -q
  'assert_summary(' "$t"` self-scopes and cannot rot when a test is renamed — the failure mode
  of the `vr01_` prefix all over again. Measured at 6.6.6: 332 of 333 `.tcyr` call it and all
  332 print a line with N >= 1, so the check is not selective in practice, and the single
  opt-out opts ITSELF out by not calling it.
* **N >= 1, not an exact count.** A test may assert inside a loop or behind a platform guard.
  The claim being enforced is "user code ran and reported", which is exactly what a dropped
  program cannot fake, and nothing more.

**The recognition rule.** Whenever a harness reduces a run to one number, ask what that number
is for the degenerate case — no input, no output, no work. If "did nothing" and "succeeded"
map to the same value, the harness is reporting on a set that includes the failure it exists
to find. Exit code 0, an empty summary line `__LIBTEST_SUMMARY__ 0 0`, and `0 warnings` from a
linter that never parsed the file are all the same bug.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_synthesized_comparison_that_is_accidentally_right_for_rhs_zero_hides_forever_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius 6.6.6: cx has no lte/gte opcode, so ESETCC synthesized `(a<b)|(a==b)` — and wrote the strict half into the wrong register with its operands swapped. For a right-hand side of 0 and a clean scratch the wrong sequence is ACCIDENTALLY CORRECT, and `if (x <= 0)` is the commonest shape in the tree, so it survived cx's entire life. It surfaced as three unrelated-looking bugs: assert_gte always failing, vec_get aborting on a populated vec, and a `size <= 0` guard flipping after an unrelated `g * 8` — which reads as the CALLER corrupting argument 1."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cx", "codegen", "comparison", "silent-miscompile", "emulated-opcode", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**What was emitted.** `lte` had to be built from two opcodes and an `or`:

    lt  r2, r0, r1      # the strict half, into the scratch the `or` reads
    eq  r0, r0, r1
    or  r0, r0, r2

What was actually there was `lt r0, r1, r0` on the first line — destination `r0` instead of
`r2`, operands swapped. So the strict half was computed backwards, immediately overwritten by
the `eq`, and the `or` folded in whatever `r2` held from an EARLIER emit.

**Why nobody noticed for the target's whole life.** Work the wrong sequence through with a
right-hand side of `0` and a zero `r2`:

    (0 < a) == 0   ==   a <= 0        # correct, for this one operand

`if (x <= 0)` and `if (x >= 0)` are the commonest shapes in the corpus, so the emitter looked
right on exactly the cases anyone tried. It went wrong for every other right-hand side, and
for `0` too once anything had left the scratch non-zero — the shift-by-immediate emitter
parks its shift amount there, so a `g * 8` anywhere earlier in the program poisoned every
later `<=`.

**The three symptoms were one emitter.** This is the part worth keeping. They were filed as
three separate defects and two of them pointed at the wrong subsystem:

* `assert_gte` / `assert_lte` failed for every input while `assert_gt` / `assert_lt` passed —
  which reads as a bug in the assert library.
* `vec_get` aborted `index out of bounds` on a populated vec (`0 >= 1` scored TRUE) — which
  reads as a bug in the vec library, or in its length bookkeeping.
* `zalloc(64)` returned 0 because its `if (size <= 0)` guard flipped after an unrelated global
  multiply — which reads as **the caller corrupting the callee's first argument**, and sends
  the reader into the call ABI, where nothing is wrong.

**The recognition rule.** When a target lacks an opcode and the compiler synthesizes it from
two or three others, check the synthesis against an operand that is NOT the common one. An
emulated operator is a small program, and a small program that is right for the argument
everybody passes is indistinguishable from a correct one. Two cheap tests catch it:
a non-zero right-hand side, and running the same comparison after something that dirties the
scratch register the synthesis reads. Both are one line in a gate; neither had ever been
written for cx (`tests/gates/codegen/cx_relational_or_equal.sh` now holds them, with every row
cross-checked against the same source through the x86 backend).

**And the corollary for triage:** three consumer-visible symptoms in three different
subsystems, all appearing on one target, is evidence of ONE thing in that target's backend —
not three. Look for the shared emitter before fixing any of them.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "a_refusal_added_at_the_bottom_of_a_multi_arm_parse_fn_only_covers_what_reaches_the_bottom_6_6_6"
description = "cyrius 6.6.6: PARSE_RETURN has THREE arms that handle a `return` themselves and `return 0` out of the function before the bottom of the fn is reached — the tail-call path, the multi-return (tuple) arm, and the vector `return IDENT;` branches. A new type refusal was added near the bottom; it covered one of the three. The bite found the tail-call arm (because a test happened to use that spelling) and shipped with the tuple arm open, while the source comment said 'EVERYTHING ELSE' and the CHANGELOG said 'refuse anything the ABI cannot carry'. Both were false. Grep for the EARLY RETURNS, not for the shape you are refusing."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["parse-return", "refusal", "early-return", "multi-arm", "incomplete-fix", "silent-miscompile", "simd", "v6.6.6"]
content = """
**The shape.** `PARSE_RETURN` in `src/frontend/parse_fn.cyr` is ~700 lines and is not one
decision. It is a sequence of arms, each of which recognises a `return` spelling, emits the
whole thing itself, and `return 0`s out:

    if (GINFN(S) == 1) { ... tail call: epilogue + jmp ... return 0; }     # line ~613
    if (PEEKT(S) == 10) { ... multi-return: ret2/ret3 ... return 0; }      # line ~909
    if (_cur_fn_ret_sid > 0 ...) { ... big-struct retptr copy ... return 0; }
    if (_cur_fn_ret_pair == 1) { ... rax:rdx pair load ... return 0; }
    if (_is_simd128(...)) { ... return IDENT; byte copy ... return 0; }
    if (_is_simd256(...)) { ... same, 32 bytes ... return 0; }
    <the new refusal>                                                     # line ~1242
    <the PE retptr arm, then the generic scalar PCMPE path>

A refusal placed at `<the new refusal>` is not a refusal for the language. It is a refusal for
the spellings that fall through six arms to get there.

**What that cost.** v6.6.6 bite 21e refused everything the vector return ABI cannot carry, and
correctly noticed the tail-call arm — it diverted `return f(args);` to the normal path so the
refusal could see it. It did not notice the multi-return arm, so:

    fn bad(): f64v2 { return (41, 7); }

still compiled clean, exit 0, no diagnostic, and the caller read 0 — on f64v2, f32v4, i64v2 and
the arity-3 form into f64v4. The ret2/ret3 convention is rax:rdx[:r3], which is the ABI of a
9-16 byte STRUCT, not of a vector on any target. Meanwhile the legacy spelling of the same
thing, `ret2(41,7); return 0;`, WAS caught — because the trailing `return 0;` reaches the
bottom. Two spellings of one construct, opposite outcomes, and the one that worked is the one
that made the fix look complete.

**Why the tail-call arm got found and the tuple arm did not.** The tail-call arm was found by a
test that happened to be written as `return sc();`. Nobody wrote `return (a, b);` because it is
not a shape anyone uses for a vector — which is exactly the property that makes an unrefused
shape dangerous rather than harmless. A refusal exists for the code nobody writes.

**The recognition rule.** Before adding a check to a long parse function, enumerate its EARLY
RETURNS, not its inputs:

    awk '/^fn PARSE_RETURN/,/^fn [^_]/' src/frontend/parse_fn.cyr | grep -n 'return 0;'

Every one of them is a path your check does not see. Decide for each whether it can carry the
thing you are refusing; the ones that cannot need the check at their own head (or a divert to
the common path, which is what the tail-call arm got). Then write ONE gate row per arm and
mutate each guard SEPARATELY — a mutant that reddens every row proves only that you have one
check, not that you have the right number of them. The 6.6.6 gate
(`tests/gates/codegen/simd_return_shapes.sh`) has m2/m3/m4 for exactly this: revert the
PARSE_RETURN refusal and the tuple rows stay GREEN, which is the signal that they are a
different site.

**The corollary for prose.** The same review found the source comment claiming 'EVERYTHING
ELSE' and the CHANGELOG claiming 'refuse anything the ABI cannot carry'. A completeness claim
in a comment is a claim about control flow, and it is cheap to check and cheap to get wrong.
Do not write one until the early-return enumeration above is done.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "macho_arm64_unrouted_syscall_reran_stale_x16"
description = "On arm64-macOS an unrouted syscall number did not fail: ESYSXLAT's Mach-O chain writes x16 (Darwin's syscall register) only on a MATCH and nothing defaulted it, so the svc silently re-ran whatever x16 last held — normally the PREVIOUS syscall — with the new arguments, returning a plausible value. The class shipped at least eight times and was fixed row by row until cycc 6.6.8 defaulted x16 to 0xFFFF at the chain head (xnu → nosys: SIGSYS, or -78 with SIGSYS ignored). A translate-by-chain emitter needs a DEFAULT for the no-match case, not more rows."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["codegen", "aarch64", "macho", "darwin", "syscall", "esysxlat", "silent-failure", "cross-target", "v6.6.8"]
content = """
**Symptom.** `getpid()` then `syscall(<unrouted>, …)` on ecb returned the PID again. No
crash, no errno — the wrong call ran. `tests/tcyr/crossos/syscall_ioctl_x86_compat.tcyr`
passed on ecb for releases because its raw 16 had no row and re-ran ioctl out of x16 = 54,
so the test that was supposed to prove the 16 row existed proved nothing.

**Why.** `ESYSXLAT`'s `_TARGET_MACHO == 2` arm is a chain of `cmp x8,#linux; b.ne; movz
x16,#bsd`. Each row writes x16 only when it matches. ESYSCALL preserves x16 across `svc`
(the pipe/fork fixups rely on it), so after any syscall x16 holds that call's BSD number,
and an unmatched number simply inherits it. Other x16 writers exist too (large-offset
scratch, every `__got` reroute's `adrp/ldr/blr x16`), so "stale" means "whatever last wrote
x16". x86-macOS never had the hole: an unmatched rax reaches Darwin UNCLASSED and SIGSYSes.

**Fix (6.6.8).** One word at the chain head, guarded on query mode like every row:
`movz x16, #0xFFFF`. xnu maps a number >= nsysent to nosys. The compiler's "syscall N not
routed … will fault on macOS" warning had promised that all along; it was false on this
backend until the default existed.

**x86-macOS had a quieter twin.** With SIGSYS IGNORED, an unclassed number came back with rax
unchanged — `syscall(4000)` returned 4000, a positive "success". EMACHO_SYSXLAT now ends with
`_msx_tail`, classing a leftover number as Unix 0xFFFF, so both Macs answer -78.

**The general lesson.** A translation table implemented as a chain of conditional writes has
an implicit DEFAULT — whatever the destination held before. If that default is "the last
value", every missing row is a silent wrong answer instead of a loud one, and each one gets
found late, on hardware, and fixed by adding one more row. Give the chain an explicit
failing default first; THEN the missing rows announce themselves (the ioctl test died rc 140
on ecb the moment the default landed without the row).

**Related 6.6.8 finding — a compile-time diagnostic keyed on the number alone.** The Mach-O
reroutes for 228 / 35 / 1700 fire at ONE argument count, but the route query answered by
number, so `syscall(228, id)` built clean and faulted. `_macho_warn_unrouted` now checks the
arity. `tests/gates/platform/darwin_syscall_literals_routed.sh` probes every in-tree literal
site through the compiler at its REAL argc, and compiles every shipped program and crossos
test for both Macs, failing on any `not routed`.
"""

[[entries]]
name = "float_literal_rational_divided_at_runtime"
description = "Until cycc 6.6.10 a decimal float literal was an i64 numerator/denominator pair (numer = int*10^fd + frac, denom = 10^fd) that the backends divided at RUNTIME (cvtsi2sd/scvtf + divsd/fdiv). Three silent failures fell out: numer overflowed once the digit string reached 2^63; denom overflowed past 18 FRACTION digits whatever the significance (`0.0000000000000000001` was NEGATIVE, `9.999999999999999999` was -8.2); and the runtime divide double-rounded once numer passed 2^53, so ~3%/24%/21% of 16/17/18-digit literals were 1 ulp off, identically on every host. The fix converts at compile time with integer bignums (round-half-even, sticky, subnormals, +inf) and each backend loads the bits as one immediate. Integer literals >= 2^64 wrapped the same way and are now an error."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["lexer", "float", "literal", "rounding", "bignum", "double-rounding", "overflow", "silent-failure", "cross-target", "seed-derive", "v6.5.28", "v6.6.10"]
content = """
**Symptom.** No error and no warning, only a different number. `9.999999999999999999` was
0xc020e4bba3a48757 (-8.2). `12345678901234567890.0` and `0.0000000000000000001` came out
negative. dhvani's `0.9222578791123097` ended in ...808b instead of ...808c. x86, aarch64
(qemu), real pi and ecb all produced the same wrong bits, so no cross-host comparison could
see it.

**Why it hid.** `tests/tcyr/crossos/float_literal_precision.tcyr` scaled each literal by a
power of ten and truncated with `f64_to`, which cannot see a 1-ulp error. A test that rounds
away the property it claims to check will pass. It compares the IEEE-754 bit patterns now
(38 rows, expected values computed independently in Python).

**The trigger was misreported.** It is not "19+ significant digits". `3.141592653589793238`
(19 digits, below 2^63) was right, while a ONE-digit literal with 19 fraction digits was
garbage. The overflow was in the denominator.

**The mechanism: two roundings of one value.** numer and denom are each rounded to a double
(cvtsi2sd), and then the quotient is rounded again. Both are correctly rounded operations,
but the composition is not. Correct rounding needs the exact rational and one rounding step,
which is why the conversion now happens in the lexer, where the digits still exist.

**Bignums under cybs.** `_flit_bits` and its helpers in src/frontend/lex.cyr use 32-bit limbs
in 8-byte slots. That way no product needs 128 bits, and every shift is correct whether `>>`
is logical or arithmetic. The quotient is 56 bits, computed by restoring shift-subtract, so
there is no bignum-by-bignum long division. Every helper is small and uses few globals,
because cybs fails silently past its per-fn limits. seed-derive's gen1 was checked on a
3,000-literal sweep, since cycc's own source has no float literals and the fixpoint never
runs this code.

**The size bound is an argument, not a cap.** Only 800 significant digits are kept. A nonzero
tail becomes one trailing `1`, which cannot change the result, because every double and every
halfway point between two doubles has at most 767 significant digits. Values below 1e-324 or
at or above 1e309 are decided before any bignum is built.

**Integer twin.** LEXDEC, LEXHEX and LEXOCT had no overflow check either: `18446744073709551617`
was 1, and so was `0x10000000000000001`. Only one such literal existed in the ecosystem,
cyrld.cyr's `r_add - 0x10000000000000000`, and it was a no-op that compiled BECAUSE of the
wrap. The hard error and that line's deletion had to land in the same change, or cyrld would
stop building.

**Lesson.** A number the lexer builds into a fixed-width field needs an overflow check at
the point it is accumulated. v6.5.28 fixed the 32/32 packing of this same literal and did
not look at the i64 arithmetic one line above it. Gates:
`tests/tcyr/crossos/float_literal_precision.tcyr` and
`tests/gates/frontend/integer_literal_overflow_refused.sh`. See CHANGELOG [6.5.28] and
[6.6.10].
"""

[[entries]]
name = "file_attribution_is_a_line_lookup_so_the_lexer_line_counter_is_a_security_boundary"
description = "`private` is enforced on FM_FILEID(line): a token's FILE is a range lookup of its LINE in the `#@file` span table, so every input to that lookup is part of the security boundary. Three instances, three different inputs: the span arithmetic (v6.5.3, an unmasked base collapsed every line to one file), the markers (CVE-45, a forged `#@file`), and at cycc 6.6.11 the lexer's own line counter — LEX's string loop did not count a newline inside a string literal, every later token sat one line high per newline, tokens near an include boundary resolved to the NEIGHBOURING file, and a call to another file's private fn compiled (CVE-55). Nothing was forged; the only input was an ordinary multi-line string."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["lexer", "line-numbers", "private", "visibility", "FM_FILEID", "file-map", "security", "CVE-55", "CVE-45", "v6.5.3", "v6.6.11"]
content = """
**The chain.** The preprocessor flattens every `include` into one buffer and brackets each
file with a `#@file "NAME" BASE` marker; `FM_BUILD` turns the markers into spans (start line,
line count, source base) and `FM_FILEID(S, line)` answers a token's file by finding the span
its LINE falls in. `private` is decided on that answer. The line is whatever LEX stamped on the
token, so a lexer that miscounts lines moves tokens between files without touching a marker.

**The miscount.** LEX's string loop stored a raw LF, and the LF of a backslash-newline escape,
without calling `SCLINE`. After a literal holding N newlines every later token was N lines
high. Symptoms, mildest first:
  * diagnostics one line high per newline — "errors in backend/x86/fixup.cyr are one line
    high" was filed separately (N3); its trigger was the one raw-newline literal in
    src/frontend/parse_expr.cyr, and the `#@file` bookkeeping it was blamed on was correct;
  * line 1 of the next include reported on that include's own marker line, as a bare
    `error:4:12:` with no file name;
  * a call after a multi-line string near an include boundary attributed to the DEFINING
    file, so a cross-file call to a `private` fn COMPILED, and a privacy error, when one
    fired, landed on an innocent name.

**Why it is a CVE and not a diagnostics bug (CVE-55, P2).** No marker was wrong and nothing
was forged. Any source file could bypass `private` with a string literal. Same impact as
CVE-45, different vector.

**The class: three inputs to one lookup, each fixed on its own.**
  1. v6.5.3, the span arithmetic. `FM_BUILD` packs the source base into the high 32 bits of
     the line-count word; `FM_FILEID` read it unmasked, `start + lines` overflowed, the first
     span matched every line, and every token collapsed to one file id (access wrongly
     ALLOWED).
  2. CVE-45 (6.6.6; the first copier was guarded at v6.5.21), the markers. `FM_BUILD` accepts
     `#@file` at any offset, so an included file, a `#define` body or a `#derive` tail could
     mint a span and claim to be another file.
  3. CVE-55 (6.6.11), the key: the line numbers the lookup is indexed by.
Each fix guarded the input its repro used. None asked what else the lookup reads.

**The rule.** When a security decision is DERIVED (file = f(line), owner = f(offset)), list
every input to the derivation and treat each one as boundary code: the table, the arithmetic
over it, and the key. Here the key is the lexer's line counter, which nobody reads as security
code. Every path in the lexer that consumes a newline byte has to count it.

**Fix and pin.** Both string-loop paths call `SCLINE`; the string token keeps its OPENING line
(the column its diagnostic head prints). `tests/gates/frontend/lexer_errors_name_file_line.sh`
axis 6 pins, after a multi-line string, the diagnostic's line, the include's file name and the
`private` refusal of a cross-file call that compiled on 6.6.10. Mutations recorded in its
header: dropping the raw-LF `SCLINE` turns axis 6 red; dropping the escaped-LF one turns
`ml_bslf` red. The same bite closed the escape list (an unknown escape is a lex error). See
CHANGELOG [6.6.11] (B05, N1/N2/N3) and language/features.cyml
`string_literal_escapes_and_line_accounting_v6611`.
"""
